65b97d98ce3cc6a72dfc3612	St. Francis Xavier Church	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706655272842.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190610_StFrancisXavierChurch_Exterior_Riley.JPG" width="261" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706655298285.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190610_StFrancisXavierChurch_HolyWaterFont_Riley.JPG" width="309" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706655329510.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190610_StFrancisXavierChurch_Interior_Riley.JPG" width="443" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There were only three sounds as I first walked into the St. Francis Xavier Church: the sound of the huge wooden door creaking open as I walked inside, my clap of my sandals against the smooth floors echoing throughout the vaulted ceilings, and water dripping into a huge cement pool of holy water. Once I sat down in the wicker seats in the back, the sound of distant traffic from Grand and the Church’s blasting air conditioner became more evident, but was still muted. The air around me smelled like an old book, as there were blue bibles next to every single chair or pew in the building. The walls were full of depictions of religious figures: huge cement sculptures hung on the walls that showed the story of Jesus and the cross consecutively and stained glass on three of the four walls poured in light and color that each showed a specific religious message. Even with groups coming in and out of the Chapel, the Church was the perfect destination for quiet reflection and escape from the busy atmosphere of the city outside.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Do people still do confessions here? Is it similar to the traditional Catholic confessions that is depicted in media?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">How many people visit this site in one day? Where are the workers and clergy people?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When, if ever, is the church closed?</span></p><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Sound of water dripping into pool of holy water</span></p></li></ul><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Distant sounds of traffic outside on Grand Blvd</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Sounds of the air conditioner running, cold air runs throughout the whole church</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Sunlight comes through stained glass on three of the four walls, giving off bright red, blue, yellow, and purple colors</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A bright spotlight shines on the sculpture in the front, depicting holy figures</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A good place for quiet reflection, as no one is in the Chapel and there are only light or distant sounds coming in</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Blue bibles are in a cubby next to every seat or in front of the pews</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Confessional booths are open all along the side walls, lights and curtains are only on half of them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The cool air conditioning feels like a wall as you step into the giant doors from the summer heat</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The air smells like an old book</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Any sound echoes up into the highly vaulted ceiling, especially the sound of my sandals walking on the smooth floors</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The seats in the back are made of wicker, make a stretching sound as someone sits in it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Sounds of nearby traffic and construction become more evident</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The walls, windows, and main altar-area are covered in sculptures and portraits of religious figures</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One side’s sculptures depict Jesus being put up on the cross, the other continues the story of Jesus coming back down off the cross</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Spotlights shine down from an area near the ceiling, that looks like the balcony-area of an opera show</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The spotlights only shine on two areas: the main altar in the front and the pool of the holy water in the back</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The rest of the church is illuminated by modern-looking chandeliers along the side walls</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The nine giant columns that span from the back of the church to the front are made of a glossy stone material that reflects the light from the chandeliers</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The noise from the holy water pool gets louder every few minutes, as a larger amount of water collects and spills from the fountain</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A motorcycle speeds by on the street, giving off a loud engine noise that echoes throughout the church</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The large wooden doors squeak open and the traffic noises sound much closer</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Footsteps echo through the church as a large group walks in</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">“At least 800 students attend every Sunday night” says a tour guide to her tour group that just entered</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The stained glass windows next to me show Jesus and his disciples and reads “Follow Me”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The one next to it shows people bowing to Jesus and says “Behold, the Heart that had Loved Men so Much”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As the tour group exits the church, the quiet, peaceful noises that I heard become more apparent</span></p></li></ul><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709241569747.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709241603382.mp4">Video 2</a></p>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.63712053101447	-90.23345947265625	3628 Lindell Blvd, St. Louis, MO 63108, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "Catholicism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "sounds", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "architecture", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "building", "origin": "user"}]	2019-06-10 17:00:47.29	2024-01-30 22:52:08.375	2025-01-28 18:22:06.931	\N
664bfa753a48ef5557d2b721	Train to Temple 23	<div>Many henro use public transit where possible to move between temples. A local train line runs south to Hiwasa. Several henro were on our train from Tokushima, and additional henro got on at stations along the way</div><div><br></div>	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	33.8737382	134.6124295	Okamoto-35-2 Kuwanochō, Anan, Tokushima 779-1402, Japan	f	f	f	[]	2024-05-21 01:34:38.309	2024-05-21 01:35:49.012	2024-11-22 17:36:37.384	\N
65b977711d442f7c715c9b84	St. Louis Pride Parade	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706653574782.jpg" alt="LRDA_Experience_20190630_StLouisPrideParade_ParadeView_Riley_001.JPG" width="465" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706653668853.jpg" alt="LRDA_Experience_20190630_StLouisPrideParade_ParadeView_Riley_003.JPG" width="467" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706653704051.jpg" alt="LRDA_Experience_20190630_StLouisPrideParade_ParadeView_Riley_004.JPG" width="464" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706653737509.jpg" alt="LRDA_Experience_20190630_StLouisPrideParade_ParadeView_Riley_005.JPG" width="462" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When walking up to the Pride Parade in St. Louis, pedestrians can hear the roar of the crowd as floats pass by and the bright colors in the crowd. Rainbow flags fly high in the air and cars slowly pass by, looking for parking spots. Almost every parade goer wears rainbow attire and walks by with a smile on their face. Hardworking men push carts in the 90 degree heat and call out that they have ice cold beverages or pride gear for sale. The crowd cheers out louder when drag queens riding in convertibles wave in their direction or a float passes by that is throwing out rainbow beads and candy. While Pride celebrations are traditionally very loud and proud, this parade was actually a very calm environment. There were many families with small kids in attendance and corporations that are branded as “family-friendly” had giant floats decked out with rainbow colors, furthering the idea that pride is for all ages. All of the St. Louis community coming together to celebrate those that face discrimination every day simply for their sexuality shows the close-knit aspect of the St. Louis community.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Do the corporations that had floats in the parade actually continue to have prideful values after the parade, or do they only use it as a way to make money from LGBTQIA communities?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Are the people that come to the parade still just as much advocates for the community once they leave?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Were the people in attendance mostly from the middle class? Is there a correlation between being an ally to the LGBTQIA community and class? Race? Gender?</span></p><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Driving passed the parade location, I can see from a distance all the people that are lining up around the parade’s barriers and cars crowd the entire area</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There is honking all over and traffic is incredibly slow moving as everyone tries to find a parking spot</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Walking into the parade grounds on Market Street, cops direct traffic away from the route and make way for the crowds of people pouring in</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There seems to be an excitement in the air to finally reach the parade action</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">At the less populated end of the parade, the crowd is still three people deep from the barriers</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Many people sit in the shade behind the crowd because the weather is incredibly hot, 90 degrees today</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One of the first things I see when looking towards the crowd is a man pushing a cart around, sweating through his shirt. The cart is full of rainbow colors and bright flags shoot up way above the top of the cart. As he pushes it, the flags fly back a little bit in the breeze. A young woman in knee-high rainbow socks flags the man down and he stops his cart. She walks up and walks around the entirety of the cart, looking at all the options. The man waits patiently until she makes a decision. He takes the cash and continues on the route, fanning himself in the process.</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Everyone around me has some sort of pride gear or bright colors on them, not one person in sight’s clothing is bland</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Sitting in the shade, the crowd is slightly quieter, you can see the beads and candy thrown by the floats flying into the crowd</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People’s cheers get louder when a float comes by that is throwing free things, and much quieter as soon as it passes</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Although I thought the crowd would be mostly young people, the crowd is very diverse: many families are here with young and older children, people are here with their friends or significant others, and many people (young or old) come in groups. While the crowd is mostly caucasian, it is not by a large margin.</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People cheer and clap constantly, as the floats go by one after the other -- no breaks in between</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Many of the cars in the parade are convertibles with drag queens sitting in the back of them, waving to the people and smiling. People seem to cheer much louder for the drag queens, especially when they look in their direction. Wearing long gowns, wigs, and a lot of makeup, it seems like it would be incredibly hot</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Many people in attendance are wearing cardinals or blues gear, some of which has rainbow colors for the parade</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Towards the end of the parade, the floats are all made to represent corporations like Schnuck’s, Boeing, Bud Light, and many more. The cheers were much softer for those floats. I think this is because the other organizations in the parade, like Pride STL, seem authentically there for the right reasons</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Political candidates are also using the Pride Parade press, riding in the back of convertibles with their face, name, and office they are running for plastered on the side of the car</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The barricades seem to get more crowded every few minutes as people get up from their break in the shade and come back to see the action</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A lot of people have put out lawn chairs and blankets under the shade, missing out on most of the action to be in the shade. From their view, the crowd is blocking the floats so they can only see things that are above them, like flags and balloons</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Men who seem to have been working all morning and afternoon in the heat are walking down the route with rolling coolers or carts, yelling out that they have ice cold beverages or water bottles</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">I expected there to be trash littered all over the ground like any other big parade I’ve been to, but there is not a single piece of trash in sight. People seem to be respectful of littering and the environment at this parade</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">I also expected there to be a lot of craziness and rude people in the crowd, as usually there are some negative experiences in giant crowds like that, but all the people seem to be friendly around me and respect my personal space. When I asked someone to take a picture of my boyfriend and I, they were more than willing to help</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While Pride celebrations are traditionally very loud and proud, this parade was actually a very calm environment. There were many families with small kids in attendance and corporations that are branded as “family-friendly” had giant floats decked out with rainbow colors, furthering the idea that pride is for all ages. All of the St. Louis community coming together to celebrate those that face discrimination every day simply for their sexuality shows the close-knit aspect of the St. Louis community.&nbsp;</span></p></li></ul><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709144017067.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709144052296.mp4">Video 2</a></p>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.628002003312744	-90.20101547241211	14th St & Market St, St. Louis, MO 63103, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "LBGTQA+", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "parade", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "pride", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "festival", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "public", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "politics", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "flags", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "demonstrations", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "sexuality", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "gender", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "identity", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "clothing", "origin": "user"}]	2019-06-30 17:00:13.665	2024-01-30 22:25:53.924	2025-01-28 18:22:06.809	\N
