65bd8474ce3cc6a72dfc3618	Saint Louis Zoo	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706917417567.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_BigCatCountry_Riley_001.JPG" width="478" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706917507553.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_BigCatCountry_Riley_002.JPG" width="487" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706917567502.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_BigCatCountry_Sign_Riley.JPG" width="484" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706917614828.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_Crowds_Riley.JPG" width="481" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706917730988.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_ElephantEnclosure_Riley_001.JPG" width="483" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706917939714.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_FishCave_Riley_001.JPG" width="482" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706917827647.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_ElephantEnclosure_Riley_003.JPG" width="487" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706918001087.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_FishCave_Riley_002.JPG" width="482" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706918084256.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_MissouriMeadowSign_Riley.JPG" width="490" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706918153890.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_SeaLionExhibit_Riley_001.JPG" width="491" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706918342265.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_SeaLionExhibit_Riley_002.JPG" width="490" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706918431120.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_SeaLionExhibit_Riley_003.JPG" width="491" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706918487650.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_SeaLionExhibit_Riley_004.JPG" width="496" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706918549948.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_Zebras_Riley_001.JPG" width="493" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706918608637.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_Zebras_Riley_002.JPG" width="488" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706918737045.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190626_SaintLouisZoo_ZooRailroad_Riley.JPG" width="486" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">At the front of the Zoo, most of the people walking in pass by the map stand, already knowing their way around the park. There is an excitement in the air as people decide which animals they want to see first. Different conversations fill the air, overlapping and becoming indistinguishable. People’s sandals slap on the pavement as they seek a shady spot for a rest from the hot summer day. Some of the children have blue mouths from the slushees they begged their parents for. Since many of the visitors are St. Louis residents, it isn’t their first trip to the Zoo, not even their first trip of the summer. Many of them wear St. Louis sports gear as they pass by a sea lion statue at the front of the Park that also rocks a Blues jersey.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Standing near the elephant enclosure, one of the most popular attractions of the Zoo, I watch as families round the corner and see a giant elephant standing right in front of the fence. Each person says “wow!” or “look at that!”, staring in awe of the beautiful creature in front of them. The elephant seems to be putting on a show for them, wagging her ears back and forth and shooting water into the air with her trunk. The air smells like animal poop and bugs fly around all over the area, but no one seems to care. All different kinds of visitors walk up to the enclosure, different races, sizes, ages, groups, squeezing together along the fence to see the animal.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The St. Louis Zoo is one iconic landmark that is an integral part of the city’s vibrant and rich culture. Whether bonding through the shared fascination in animals, the city’s sports gear that they are wearing, or even just the excitement that the Zoo is free, the visitors represent the tight-knit culture of St. Louis’ population.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What is the traditional social landscape of the Zoo?</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">How many times a year does the typical St. Louis family come to the Zoo?<br><br></span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Do residents of St. Louis consider the Zoo to be an integral part of the city’s culture?</span></p><p></p><p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Near the elephant enclosure</span></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The most prominent sound is the water rushing down from the waterfall and splashing into the pond below</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The next sound you hear is the high-pitched sound of children in awe of the animal in front of them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As soon as people round the corner and see the giant elephant right in front of the enclosure’s gate, they usually say “wow!” And “look at that!”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One child tries to yell “I love you!” To the elephant</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The elephant seems to be drinking water and trying to cool herself down by picking up water with her trunk and splashing it all over her head</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Her ears are wagging back and forth like she is happy</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">She is gray and covered in wrinkles</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People are constantly posing for pictures in front of her and taking videos on their phones</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Men with strollers stand at the back of the group while moms and babies walk to the front to get the best view</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Children all around have blue lips from the slushees they drank</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Many of the people passing by are all wearing matching shirts that say a camp’s name on them, teenagers in charge of the children try to keep the group together</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Children misbehave and hang on the fence of the Enclosure, causing moms to yell at their children and apologize to everyone around them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Many strollers and wheelchairs pass through the walkway</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The walkway is cement with all different kinds of animal tracks imprinted in them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One summer camp group in yellow shirts finally gets their turn to step to the front of the fence</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Children are being held up to the fence all over, by grandparents, parents, and siblings</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">All different kinds of people walk past the Enclosure, different races, sizes, ages, groups</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The sound of the zoo train passes by in the distance, the wheels spinning and screeching on the tracks and the alarm going off on the railroad gates</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The smell of animal poop, hay, and grass fills the air</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One man gets up on the rocks near the fence to get a better view away from all the people</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Bugs fly onto people every couple of seconds, randomly people will start swatting the air</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The area is mostly shady, with a few areas of sun peeking through the trees overhead, people sit on the rocks near me to catch a breather from the hot day and from walking around</span></p></li></ul><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Near the North entrance&nbsp;</span></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Children walking past cry in their strollers and in their parents arms while walking towards the exit, obviously exhausted from their zoo experience&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One woman makes her boyfriend stop at a stand nearby so they can get pineapples with drinks in them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A woman with her family stops off to the side and fixes her shoe so that they can keep walking around the zoo</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A group of teenagers sitting at a picnic table nearby talks about how exhausted the sun makes them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A child runs past to see the train rush by</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A man walks up to the snack stand and looks up at the menu</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">All different conversations pass by my earshot, making them all indistinguishable</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A family walks up and asks to join my table, in need of shade and a place to rest</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Almost everyone here seems like they’ve been to this zoo before, knowing their way around. No one stops at the stand to grab a map</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A camp counselor nearby struggles to rangle her group of children</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The sound of a man speaking into a microphone echoes near the area, evidently in the Sea Lion arena, indicating that there is a show in progress</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">the sound of sandals on the pavement is loud everywhere I walk</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As I approach a booth that sells hamburgers and hotdogs, the smell of a barbecue fills the air</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A child in a stroller near me begs his mom for a snow cone. When the mom repeatedly tells him no, he defeatedly says “snow cone”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Only having been to the zoo a few times myself, I am lost on where I go to exit and have to stop and look at the signs</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The conductor for the zoo’s train yells out “All aboard!”&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The bell on the train rings as it starts to go on the tracks</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">All the picnic tables in the shade are taken as people try to escape the sun for a moment</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A child cries near me because her parents said no to a toy</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People gather around the statue of an elephant spitting water from its trunk</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There’s a line of children and parents at the face painting stand as the painter draws Batman on a young boy’s face</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Hundreds of summer camp kids are gathered near the entrance, a school bus parked at the entrance, waiting for them, the engine humming</span></p></li></ul><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709242812367.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709242842331.mp4">Video 2</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709242881084.mp4">Video 3</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709242897548.mp4">Video 4</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709242920026.mp4">Video 5</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709242943176.mp4">Video 6</a></p>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.63363417208087	-90.2907943725586	4 Wells Dr, St. Louis, MO 63110, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "animals", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "education", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "children", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "public", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "smells", "origin": "user"}]	2019-06-26 17:00:28.464	2024-02-03 00:10:28.605	2025-01-28 18:22:10.643	\N
65bbe94f1d442f7c715c9b8d	More Than Coffee	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813946214.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_MoreThanCoffee_TrainingNowSign_Riley (1).jpg" width="349" style="aspect-ratio: 0.652246 / 1"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The first sounds you hear at the More Than Coffee stand are the rushing cars down Manchester Avenue and the Christian music coming out from the stand’s speakers as the friendly workers wave at you as you’re walking up. While the stand resembles a traditional (yet miniaturized) coffee shop drive-thru, the experience is much more unique. One of the first things customers notice is a white sign next to the stand that reads “We train physically and/or mentally challenged adults for meaningful employment. TRAINING NOW.”&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It seems that the name “More Than Coffee” emphasizes a lot about their store’s message. Not only in the menu, as they try to stress that they have a food menu as well as coffee, but also in their purpose as a store. Their hope is to evangelize the Christian religion while also giving people, that traditionally struggle to find work, a meaningful job.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Since the drive-thru window is actually a walk-up window, the workers are able to get more personal with the customers. After asking introductory questions, they take your order and asking you more about yourself as you wait. The managers begin talking about their workers’ accomplishments like they are proud parents. There is a donation box in front of the window that says “People helping people one cup at a time.” After giving you your order, they give you a loyalty card and ask that you come back soon so they can keep working the Christian mission.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While the busy sounds of traffic may not represent the devout peace that More Than Coffee is trying to bring St. Louis, they still somehow made it feel like a religious experience. The entire endeavor just solidifies the idea that there can be religion anywhere -- even in the middle of a strip mall parking lot.&nbsp;</span><br></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What is the hiring process like?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Who owns the stand? Why did they think this was an important mission to bring to St. Louis?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">How many people normally work the stand? Are they always trained to make the experience personal for customers?</span></p><p><br></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The first noises heard are the sounds of traffic rushing down Manchester Avenue and the Christian music playing from the shop’s speakers</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Upon walking up, there were already workers waving to me and saying “Hi!”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The shop is in the middle of a strip mall with an Asian Market and a small clothing store</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It more resembles a stand than a shop, because it is a tiny building in the middle of a parking lot&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Instead of a drive thru window like most chain coffee shops, it has a walk up window, making connection and conversation between the customer and the workers more likely</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One of the first things you notice about the stand is the big “More Than Coffee” sign with a cross instead of a ‘T’ on the ‘Than’&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Next, your eye is drawn to a big white sign standing next to the shop that reads “We train physically and/or mentally challenged adults. For meaningful employment. TRAINING NOW.”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When I first started ordering, I noticed there were more workers in the little stand than I expected -- around seven workers were inside. Three of which were at the window taking my order</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The workers were incredibly nice and very eager to make conversation, they immediately asked my name</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When I did place my order, another, older man with crutches walked over and started to ask my about myself -- where I went to school, what I was studying, what kind of journalism I hoped to write, etc.</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One thing I picked up immediately is that the management is incredibly proud of their workers. The older man speaking to me talked about one of their mentally challenged workers, who used to be a cashier at the stand but started his own successful granola business and asked me to check out his website</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Plastered all over the stand are small wooden crosses</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Spray painted on the cement walkway is “We Cater” with a cross for the ‘t’ again</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Their menu is displayed as a traditional drive thru menu, but smaller, just like their stand is</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One older blonde woman was running around the inside, checking everything that the workers did. She seemed to be the manager</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">She rang up my order and gave me a loyalty card after giving me my receipt</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It seems that the name “More Than Coffee” emphasizes a lot about their store’s message. Not only in the menu, as they try to stress that they have a food menu as well as coffee, but also in their purpose as a store. Their hope is to evangelize the Christian religion while also giving people that might struggle to find work, a meaningful job</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In my view, this shop is truly working the Christian mission. While some stores might identify with religious morals, this shop is truly exemplifying them by giving those outcasted by society a place to work and live normal lives</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Inside the shop, their was homemade artwork by some of the workers, it seemed, with crosses drawn with markers and papers that read “I love Jesus” in different forms</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One of the workers even had a lanyard around his neck that said “I heart Jesus”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It seems that even the workers share this Christian belief that the store has</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Upon leaving, all the workers had big smiles and said goodbye, some even remembered my name</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The manager said goodbye as well, and told me to come back again soon&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The entire experience just solidified the idea in my mind that there can be religion anywhere -- even in the middle of a strip mall parking lot</span></p></li></ul><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709242417970.mp4">Video 1</a><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1712265587239.jpg" style="font-size: 1em; max-width: 50%; height: auto;"><span style="font-size: 1em;">&nbsp;</span></p><br>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.59234229164281	-90.50387442111969	14240 Manchester Rd, Manchester, MO 63011, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "coffee", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Christianity", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "food", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "evangelicalism", "origin": "user"}]	2019-06-24 17:00:50.634	2024-02-01 18:56:15.847	2025-01-28 18:22:09.518	\N
