65bd87e2f53d3372e0845921	Astoria Toy Shop- religious statues or toy figures?	<div>Rudy's is a hobby/toy store in Astoria Queens. They also sell Catholic statues. in context, the statues have a toy-like aesthetic to them. it begs the question- how do we know the difference between a religious statue and a toy figure? When is an object a toy? when is an object a statue to worship?&nbsp;</div>	d2v7JCxLlFT9jkQlvayOD4Z3HAL2	40.76531642120421	-73.91760659410983	35 30th Ave., Astoria, NY 11103, USA	t	f	f	[]	2024-02-03 00:23:00	2024-02-03 00:25:06.69	2025-01-28 18:22:10.833	\N
65bbd14af53d3372e084591d	St. Louis Science Center	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707495603426.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Exterior_Entrance_Riley.JPG" width="608" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707495616410.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Dinosaurs_Riley_001.JPG" width="609" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707495645904.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Dinosaurs_Riley_003.JPG" width="607" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707495663872.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Dinosaurs_Riley_002.JPG" width="609" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707495680534.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Dinosaurs_Riley_004.JPG" width="614" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707495695734.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Dinosaurs_Riley_005.JPG" width="614" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707495713193.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Riley_001.JPG" width="615" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707495728188.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Riley_003.JPG" width="614" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707495756041.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Riley_004.JPG" width="619" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707496441450.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Riley_005.JPG" width="615" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707496456513.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Riley_006.JPG" width="608" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707496472661.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Riley_007.JPG" width="604" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707496492085.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Riley_010.JPG" width="598" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707496514430.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_Riley_011.JPG" width="597" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707496536757.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TheStLouisScienceCenter_Interior_TheBridge_Riley_001.JPG" width="596" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Walking up to the St. Louis Science Center, it felt like walking up to an extravagant church. The outside is modern and white, with a huge brass dome on the top of it. Inside, children are running around everywhere while parents follow closely behind, trying to keep track of all their kids. Within sight, there are almost no groups there without children.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Science Center is a child-centered experience with differing and immersive exhibits that engage the visitors’ minds with a wide variety of ways. There were interactive, first hand, personal experiences that appealed to all ages in some way. The information presented was timeless and revealed something about our nature as a society and the world around us. It was served as a launching point for deeper inquiry, giving the audience an accessible image or concept and explaining how it affects them personally or how it has in the past. The children in attendance often lead the groups while the parents bring up the rear. The appearance of the building is similar to that of a basilica and the sense of community brought with it is not unlike that of the church.&nbsp;</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While children aren’t typically involved in traditional religious ceremonies, like being put in Sunday school during a Christian church service, the Science Center provides children a ritual-like activity that is completely centered around them. At the same time, the activities provided at the Center prompts the adults in attendance to play like children, too. In many psychological studies, it is proven that there are benefits of playing for adults -- although most have lost that sense of play with age. The St. Louis Science Center not only allows for children to have a sort of religious experience centered around themselves, but it also allows for adults to find that childish sense of play that is so beneficial to their health.</span></p><p></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Do adults that bring their young children to the Center like to come for themselves, too? Or is it just for their children to have fun?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">How often does the typical family come to the Science Center?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What is the age, race, sex, and class of the typical attendee?</span></p><p><br></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">From the outside, the building looks like a massive, white, modern church</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The two main areas of the Center seem to be totally different from each other. One looks like a pop socket on the back of a phone with a very modern shape. The other looks like a beautiful church with a rounded, bronze top. They are connected by a glass bridge that goes over the highway, which seems to be a trademark of the Science Center</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The glass bridge is something I see almost everyday driving past it. Before I knew what that building was, I just saw it as a signifier that I was getting closer to SLU when I saw it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">After finding out it was the Science Center, I always knew it as “the building with the air quality sign” on it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">My roommate and I used to joke that when the Science Center said the air quality was good, our days would be good</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Now, walking up to the huge cement front walkway, it feels like I really am walking into a St. Louis monument</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There was an outside exhibit called “Whisper Tunnel” where there are two large white dishes standing 40 feet from one another. When a person whispers into one, the sound shoots across and the person at the other dish can hear it. There were two older ladies standing at it when I walked past, each whispering into it and listening when the other said something, giggling like little kids</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There are people funneling into the large entryway from the parking lot. People seem to be walking quickly to get into the air conditioned building because it’s so hot outside</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Upon walking into the glass doors, a blast of cool air hits the visitors and they’re met with a line of people trying to pay their parking ticket</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Suddenly, the air is filled with a ton of echoed chatter from the crowded room and the high ceilings</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Your eyes are immediately drawn to the ceiling where there are all different types of pipes and wire paths where balls roll down and all kinds of crazy machinery whirs</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The paths are all different colors and make the ceiling look hectic and chaotic, but very playful</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Walking in further, you are met with all the pathways to the exhibits on the first floor</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There are restaurants to the right as soon as you walk inside that have a retro vibe to them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Families stand in line at them seemingly after a few hours at the Center, children are crying and some have blue rings around their mouths from their slushees</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">So far, every single person within sight has a young child with them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Walking up the stairs in the main room, there’s like a totally different world on the upper floor</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People are walking around everywhere and there are even more children on this floor. Kids are running around and parents are close behind them to keep track of them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There are three ways to go, all leading to different exhibits. We chose to walk up to the balcony and look out at the huge dinosaur replica exhibit with a crowd of people around it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Next to the dinosaur was a fake digging site for archaeological digs</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The dinosaur was very realistic, the skin had so many details on it and the head would turn every few seconds. The eyes would look from left to right and its presence seemed to loom over the audience below it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One man reached up and tried to touch the fake t-rex’s mouth, jumping with his hand above his head</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Kids stood in awe of the dinosaur, having to crane their necks all the way back to see it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">On the bridge, natural light poured in through the green-yellow tinted windows, seeming really bright compared to the other areas with no windows</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Cars zoomed past below us as people looked at them in the binoculars that were set up</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There are all different shapes on the carpeted floor that are glass and see-through to the street below. Kids pushed their friends and siblings on them and yelled when they stepped on it in fear</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">I got to see the back of the sign I see everyday driving down the highway</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Coming into the space center, there were cool lights coming out of all the doorways that made it feel more modern&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Technology whirs around us as there are exhibits all around that simulate actually being in a spacecraft&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">At the end of the Center, there was a big circular room where a huge summer camp sat and waited for their bus, some of the kids sitting and watching a person try to land a spacecraft in one of the exhibits, others running into the space-themed gift shop</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">History of play (boy scouts - outdoors, YMCA - indoors) and how adults grow out of it and that religious background</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">How children are typically left out of religious services to go to Sunday school, and the Science Center gives them a child-centered ritual to take part in</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Lance Armstrong’s mother recently said in an interview on Hulu that she hoped the landing gave greater glory to God</span></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Do religious groups come to the Science Center?</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Do they view the landings in this light?</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Do they come to the Science Center and see the space section in this light?</span></p></li></ul></li></ul><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709328857033.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709328868395.mp4">Video 2</a><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1712265472214.jpg" style="font-size: 1em; max-width: 50%; height: auto;"><span style="font-size: 1em;">&nbsp;</span></p><div><br></div><br>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.62900778018592	-90.27036666870119	5050 Oakland Ave 2nd floor, St. Louis, MO 63110, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "science", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "children", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "play", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "education", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "technology", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "museums", "origin": "user"}]	2019-07-23 17:00:26.311	2024-02-01 17:13:46.82	2025-01-28 18:22:08.344	\N
65c52175f53d3372e084592a	St. Louis Art Museum	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707417991720.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Exterior_Entrance_Riley.JPG" width="538" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418014764.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Exterior_Statue_Riley_002.JPG" width="538" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418027666.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Atrium_Riley_002.JPG" width="534" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418039986.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_007.JPG" width="406" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418055125.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_008.JPG" width="563" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418067152.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_010.JPG" width="559" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418079210.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_011.JPG" width="556" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418094129.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_012.JPG" width="558" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418111540.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_013.JPG" width="433" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418131544.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_014.JPG" width="435" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418152394.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_015.JPG" width="507" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418190471.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_017.JPG" width="504" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418225832.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_018.JPG" width="523" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418274911.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_016.JPG" width="431" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418293527.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190630_StLouisArtMuseum_Interior_Exhibit_Riley_019.JPG" width="431" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 11pt">Walking up to the St. Louis Art Museum, the air smells like fresh-cut grass and flowers. The sun is still up over Forest Park and people look over the Museum’s balcony to see the beautiful view from Art Hill. The statue of the Apotheosis of St. Louis, or King Louis IX of France, sits on his horse regally above the people, sword drawn in the air. Pulling open the glass door to walk inside, the environment from the wide-open, bright Park outside, the ambience totally changes. The main room is much </span><span style="font-size: 14.6667px">darker and</span><span style="font-size: 11pt"> has a much colder and less intimate atmosphere: people are scattered all over the giant room, conversations echo throughout the tall ceilings but are indistinguishable. Walking into one of the four hallways that connect to the main room, the environment feels much more warm and intimate. Spotlights on the artwork are the main source of lighting -- the gold, ornate frames shimmer underneath them. Each different room has a new mood to it, the paint color of the walls and the theme of the artwork playing a huge role in that. The wood floors creak all over the Museum as people slowly walk through each room, speaking with hushed voices. The type of people at the Museum is not very diverse, there are a lot of young to middle-aged couples coming for a date on a Friday night. A lot of the artwork has religious undertones to it or is overtly religious, especially with Christian messages. The Art Museum attracts people very interested in art, religious people looking for art similar to their beliefs, or people simply looking for a good time -- all groups find exactly what they are looking for at the St. Louis Art Museum.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What is the typical visitor of the St. Louis Art Museum like? What is their race? Class? Gender?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What is the typical motive to come to the museum? Because it is a St. Louis monument or for the artwork?</span></p><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Outside the Museum, the sun was still out over Forest Park</span></p></li></ul><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The air smelled like cut grass and fresh air</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The statue in front of the entrance seemed to tower over the entirety of the Park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Since the Museum is on a hill above the rest of Forest Park, your eyes tend to wander towards its extravagant entrance with huge columns</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People gather at the statue outside, taking pictures and looking out to the Park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The statue is of the Apotheosis of St. Louis, or King Louis IX of France, which is the namesake of St. Louis. He sits regally on his horse, with his sword drawn up (</span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apotheosis_of_St._Louis"><u><span style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apotheosis_of_St._Louis</span></u></a><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">)</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Walking up to the front doors, the steps seem intimidating and plentiful</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Some of the stairs droop in the middle from all the people that have walked up them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There’s one advertisement that is stuck on the sidewalk before walking inside, showing all the movies that SLAM is presenting this summer</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Pulling open the glass door to walk inside, the environment from the wide-open, bright Park outside, the ambience totally changes</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The main room is much darker, and has a much colder and less intimate atmosphere</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People are scattered all over the giant room, conversations echo throughout the tall ceilings but are indistinguishable&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There’s a table in the middle of the room in front of the stairs leading down, a black tablecloth on the table reads “SLAM: Underground”, meaning that there is a slam poetry reading tonight in a seminar room of the Museum</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People turn and smile as I walk through the door</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There are four hallways in each corner of the room, each leading to a different area of the Museum</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Very open, tall ceilings</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Hushed voices</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Lots of diverse areas</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Slow footsteps</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Focal point artworks that draw your eye in</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Large and small windows scattered all over&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Echoing halls</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Varied lighting all over</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Scattered people, gathering at certain pieces and wide open parts in another</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There is one exhibit in the main room, it is my favorite of the entire Museum. It is a giant silver bookshelf with disheveled metal sheets poking out of it from all over. The plaque next to it states that it is God’s bookshelf and he stores all of his information about the universe in it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">I walk down the front left hallway first, everyone enters under a huge cement archway</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The paintings and sculptures are under bright spotlights, making your eyes move from one to the next throughout the room</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The frames are all gold and detailed, shining underneath the spotlights</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The lighting change completely changes the atmosphere to a more intimate, warm feeling along with the lower ceiling that makes the room feel incredibly smaller</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The wood floors creak in some areas as visitors walk through</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The type of people at the Museum is not very diverse, there are a lot of young to middle-aged couples coming for a date on a Friday night</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Most of the couples hold hands and look at the same pieces together, but some seem to separate more and enjoy the experience on their own</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As I step into another area of the Museum painted forest green, there is an incredibly detailed silver cross that seems to draw all of the attention</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A spotlight shines on it and light is reflected into the room around it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People stop and look at it, taking pictures, talking about it in low tones</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In another room, eyes are immediately drawn to the tall circular ceiling with ornate, gold paintings on it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Below that is a vase in a glass case in front of a huge window</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The vase stands on a white pedestal and is the only art piece in the room, drawing in people for the dramaticness of it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A group of teenagers stand around, taking pictures from all angles of it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The window shows the road in front of the museum with cars buzzing passed, it seems like a stark contrast from the antique-look of the vase</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Native American and Ancient Egyptian parts of the Museum on the second floor seem even more intimate because they have no windows, dark walls, and minimal lighting</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Each room seems to have a different mood to it, in levels of intimacy with the visitors, brightness, colors of the walls</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In the room over, the slam poetry event is in full swing, people are chattering and clapping every few minutes and the person on the stage talks over a microphone</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As I pass by it, security guards for the event standing out front smile at me</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As I walk to the front door to leave, my sandals create an echoing slap on the marble floors</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">I walk through the glass doors and the sun is setting on Forest Park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">People stand at the cement wall in front of the statue and stare at the beautiful sky</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Art Museum attracts people very interested in art or people simply looking for a good time -- both groups find exactly what they are looking for at the St. Louis Art Museum</span></p></li></ul><p></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709327657309.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709327666884.mp4">Video 2</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709327673819.mp4">Video 3</a></p>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.63933294008076	-90.29439926147461	1 Fine Arts Dr, St. Louis, MO 63110, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "art", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "museum", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "sculptures", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "paintings", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "public", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "architecture", "origin": "user"}]	2019-06-30 17:00:44.257	2024-02-08 18:46:13.576	2024-06-24 21:31:57.089	\N
65c5299e1d442f7c715c9b9a	Laughing Bear Bakery	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419660020.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Buddah_Riley_001.JPG" width="631" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419673256.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_001.jpg.JPG" width="632" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419688451.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_002.jpg.JPG" width="630" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419706704.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_003.jpg.JPG" width="632" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419719763.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_004.jpg.JPG" width="629" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419732722.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_006.jpg.JPG" width="642" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419747389.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_001.jpg.JPG" width="638" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419767674.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_002.jpg.JPG" width="636" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419783442.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_004.jpg.JPG" width="397" style="aspect-ratio: 0.6666666666666666"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419797170.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_005.jpg.JPG" width="397" style="aspect-ratio: 0.6666666666666666"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419815757.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_006.jpg.JPG" width="561" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419831859.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_007.jpg.JPG" width="486" style="aspect-ratio: 0.6666666666666666"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419855895.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_008.jpg.JPG" width="551" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419895852.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Sign_Riley_001.JPG" width="668" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419913281.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_008.jpg.JPG" width="668" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419932429.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Sign_Riley_001.JPG" width="669" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419977518.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_StainedGlass_Riley_001.JPG" width="695" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419993248.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_StainedGlass_Riley_003.JPG" width="690" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707420013294.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_StainedGlass_Riley_005.JPG" width="688" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707420028251.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_StainedGlass_Riley_006.JPG" width="703" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Upon walking inside, the air smelled like desserts being baked and a humid mildew smell like from an old building</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Walking past the kitchen, the workers wearing white aprons seem to be bustling around</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Kalen McAlister greets us and welcomes us to sit down, she seems to be happy to have visitors at the bakery</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While guiding us around the Centenary Methodist Church, she seems to be proud of the location and the people she’s surrounded with. It soon becomes very obvious why, as one woman who works for the church talks about her dog treat company that hires homeless people</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While guiding us through meditation, her voice seemed to become much calmer and softer, showing a different side to us than the very friendly and bubbly Kalen she was outside the room</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">She seems to have a very strict belief system that I admire, including the ideas that: meditation is incredibly beneficial to life, the system is not kind to prisoners, and people deserve second chances</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When discussing the reason for spending so much of her time helping prisoners have a second chance at life, she said that she’s seen the ways in which the prison system has failed&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It’s interesting that many religious organizations have funded the Bakery, as well. Organizations like Sisters of St. Joseph have honored Kalen and given her funds that were desperately needed to keep the Bakery running</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It seems like her mission ties into religious messages about Jesus granting second chances and forgiving his followers, like from the bible, there is first John 1:9 “If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While there is a religious undertone to the mission of the Laughing Bear Bakery, it doesn’t seem like that is Kalen’s main reason for continuing her work. She has seen many people come out of prison and then immediately return back because they didn’t have the funds or resources to maintain a life outside again</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">She stated that in her nearly 30 employees that she’s hired since the bakery opened, only 2 have gone back to prison</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While Kalen asked that we do not question the employees about their past crimes, she didn’t seem to question this either. She believes it doesn’t matter what the crimes were, her only judgement of the employees begins the moment she meets them, their history before that is unimportant to her</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Laughing Bear Bakery seems to be the definition of an organization that works for the community and cares for the wellbeing of an underserved group</span></p></li></ul>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.59839651142084	-90.25335609912872	4001 Utah St, St. Louis, MO 63116, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "food", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "methodist", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "crime", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "prison", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "meditation", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "reform", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "jobs", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "work", "origin": "user"}]	2019-07-18 17:00:28.424	2024-02-08 19:21:02.863	2024-06-24 21:31:57.776	\N
65c53b60ce3cc6a72dfc3624	Tower Grove Park	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494454179.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Gazebo_Riley_002.JPG" width="583" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494468402.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Gazebo_Riley_001.JPG" width="578" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494486859.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Gazebo_Statue_Riley_001.JPG" width="575" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494504885.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Gazebo_Statue_Riley_002.JPG" width="470" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494520148.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_OldCarriageShelter_Riley.JPG" width="538" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494585168.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_OldCarriageShelter_Sign_Riley.JPG" width="541" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494557417.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Pond_Riley_001.JPG" width="543" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494600144.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Pond_Riley_002.JPG" width="538" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494633769.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Pond_Riley_003.JPG" width="539" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494671984.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Pond_Riley_004.JPG" width="537" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494700748.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Pond_Riley_005.JPG" width="544" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494724848.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Pond_Riley_006.JPG" width="543" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494766395.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Pond_Riley_007.JPG" width="541" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494788218.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Statue_Riley_001.JPG" width="538" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494803753.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_Statue_Riley_002.JPG" width="536" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494833524.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_TurkishPavilion_Riley_001.JPG" width="533" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707494849015.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190723_TowerGrovePark_TurkishPavilion_Riley_002.JPG" width="531" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Driving into Tower Grove Park, it feels like the greenery continues forever. The entirety of its 289-acre area is covered with beautiful natural plants and old, historic structures that make it feel like an oasis from the city around it. Birds chirp overhead, cars buzz by on the streets, and people walking or sitting on benches chatter. Each colorful gazebo in the park has picnic tables in it and a sign with its historical significance on it. One reads “Old Carriage House” and another has statues of different important figures surrounding it. One area that seems to be a hub in the park is the Fountain Pond and ruins. The ruins were created as a center-piece to the Pond from fragments of the Old Lindell House, another landmark to the area’s history. They stand regally on the side of the peaceful Pond for its visitors to enjoy.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In 1866, the park was donated by 66-year-old Henry Shaw, who declared that he would donate the land if it were to be “used as a park forever”. Today, Tower Grove Park serves 2.5 million children and adults annually of many different races, genders, and socio-economic backgrounds. The majority of the attendees coming to the park are nearby residents, of which 25% in the Park’s zip code live below the poverty level (</span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="http://TowerGrovePark.Org"><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">TowerGrovePark.Org</span></a><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">). The Park provides people of all different walks of life a common area to relax and enjoy a mindful moment in the middle of the chaotic city. Whether it be religious or not, Tower Grove Park offers a beautiful and historical setting for reflection.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">How many visitors does the Park typically get in a day? When is it busiest?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What purpose do most of the visitors have for coming to the Park?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What is the race, age, class, and sex of the typical visitor?</span></p><p></p><p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Fountain Pond and Ruins</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">&nbsp;</span></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">“Remains [of the Pond] were collected from the Old Lindell House to form the fountain stone and rockery, an artistic arrangement of stone blocks, which are the main focus of this landscape.” -</span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="http://TowerGrovePark.org"><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">TowerGrovePark.org</span></a></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The loudest sounds that visitors can hear is the fountain water splashing into the pond below it, the sounds of airplanes flying overhead, cars buzzing by on the street, and the people around the pond chattering</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There are birds chirping in the trees surrounding the water and bugs make buzzing noises</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A group of guys in their 20s sit on a bench around the lake, talking and looking at the ducks that are all around</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">They get up and explore the ruins around the fountain</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Two men in gray polos and khaki pants take photos of the fountain and all the wildlife around it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Ducks are crowding the ruins on one side, all sitting around, their brown feathers looking like brown spots in the bright green grass</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As a woman and her dog run past the group of ducks, they all flap their wings and scatter, their bright orange legs becoming visible</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The fountain looks antique and beautiful in the middle of the pond, parts of it are turning browner than others from where the water lands</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There are three tiers to the fountain and a large spray of water coming out of the top, it rushes down the three tiers that get larger from the top to the bottom</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">After the tiers, the water sprays down the base of the fountain, that looks like a mix of all different kinds of shapes, all mashed together in the concrete</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Although it is an odd fountain, it looks beautiful and fits in with the environment of the area</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A man in a golf cart who works for the park zooms past, seemingly towing lawn supplies on a trailer behind him</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A family with three young teenage daughters walks up to the fountain area and walks around the outside of it, taking in its beauty</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Some areas of the sky are bright blue, peeking through the dark gray storm clouds that are getting closer above me</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When the family gets closer to the group of ducks, they all squawk at them protectively</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A mom and her two young sons with a huge German Shepherd walk up to the pond, the two boys throwing rocks in and playing with a stick three times their size</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The mom seems to be taking her kids to play outside while she relaxes on a bench nearby, keeping a close eye on them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In the distance, a leaf blower and weed whacker loudly buzz</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The mother starts taking candid action shots of her sons playing with the fountain</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A new group of teenage boys walk up to meet their friends across the pond</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The young kids play on top of the ruins, unaware of the huge history behind them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It seems like there are a lot of visitors for a random Tuesday afternoon</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The ruins stand regally next to the fountain, browning on the tops from all the daily wear</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In the middle ruin, there is a doorway that the visitors seem to be taking pictures in front of</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A man runs past with his brightly spotted Dalmatian, grabbing the German Shepherd’s attention</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As the rain starts to pour down, all the groups around the pond take cover under a large tree</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The young boys yell as they run from the rain, their German Shepherd running after them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">All the people are in close proximity to each other and look around and laugh at how soaked everyone is</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Everyone begins to disperse and walk towards their cars</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Although the park is giant, the moment felt very intimate. The pond feels the same way</span></p></li></ul><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709328693117.mp4">Video 1</a></p>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.60607256141475	-90.25423049926754	Tower Grove Park, 4257 Northeast Dr, St. Louis, MO 63110, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "parks", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "outdoors", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "architecture", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "play", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "recreation", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "public", "origin": "user"}]	2019-07-23 17:00:02.38	2024-02-08 20:36:48.16	2024-06-24 21:31:58.159	\N
