65c523d5f53d3372e084592b	Park Avenue Coffee	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707418999998.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190716_ParkAvenueCoffee_Exterior_Riley.jpg" width="574" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419014732.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190716_ParkAvenueCoffee_Exterior_Riley_001.jpg" width="573" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419032360.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190716_ParkAvenueCoffee_Food_Riley.jpg" width="555" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419048495.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190716_ParkAvenueCoffee_Interior_Riley_001.jpg" width="567" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419060528.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190716_ParkAvenueCoffee_Interior_Riley_002.jpg" width="795" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419071518.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190716_ParkAvenueCoffee_Interior_Riley_003.jpg" width="812" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419218141.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190716_ParkAvenueCoffee_Interior_Riley.MOV"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">If you blink, you’ll miss the outside of Park Avenue Coffee. While looking for the entrance, I walked right past it. I soon realized and turned around, spotting the orange front door that seems to be camouflaged in the exterior of the building and the awning with a fading “Park Avenue Coffee” written on it. But, as soon as you open that orange door, you are met with the aroma of freshly made coffee and greeted by the friendly staff behind the counter. While you barely notice this tiny coffee shop from the street of Lafayette Square, it is technically a St. Louis landmark -- Park Avenue Coffee was voted “Best Gooey Butter Cake in St. Louis” by RiverFront Times and KSDK News.&nbsp;</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">With the rain splattering on the red brick sidewalks outside, the yellow lighting and dark walls make the interior feel so cozy. Soft music plays over the speakers and customers quietly chatter to each other in their leather seats. There is a sign sitting on the sidewalk outside that says “Welcome, Neighbor” with a Park Avenue coffee cup and blueberry muffin on it. This really exemplifies the environment of the coffee shop; making you feel like a part of the Lafayette Square neighborhood. Sitting next to the window, I look out at the cute little boutiques along the street, and it really gives me a small town feel with the small businesses&nbsp; and regular customers who are greeted by name -- even though we are still in the city. There is a tradition-like aspect to coming to a small coffee shop like Park Avenue. In the same way that people go to church every Sunday and socialize with their fellow members, the regulars from around the area come to the coffee shop and socialize with the workers and customers.&nbsp;</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In my opinion, Park Avenue Coffee deserves the ranking of “Best Gooey Butter Cake in St. Louis” -- and “Coziest Coffee Shop in St. Louis”, if that were a thing.</span></p><p></p><p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What percentage of their customers come to Park Avenue because of the location, not just because they like it the best?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What is the race, gender, sex, and class of the typical Park Avenue customer?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Does the other location near Tower Grove Park have the same small-town feel to it, or is it just because it’s in Lafayette Square?</span></p><p></p><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">If you blink, you’ll miss the outside of Park Avenue Coffee</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">I walked right past it when I was looking for it, and had to turn around and walk back</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The awning has fading letters that say “Park Avenue Coffee” on it in white letters</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The orange door seems to be camouflaged in the exterior of the building</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">But, as soon as you open that orange door, you are met with the aroma of freshly made coffee and greeted by the friendly staff behind the counter</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">This tiny coffee shop that you barely notice was voted “Best Gooey Butter Cake in St. Louis” by RiverFront Times and KSDK News</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As a new customer walks in, the three women behind the counter greet him by name and ask about his new apartment</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Two people sit next to the window and type on their laptops, sipping on coffee every few minutes</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Business men in professional clothing walk in, presumably on their lunch break, and get some coffee</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Two women who seemingly have come to the coffee shop to talk about work sit in two of the eight leather chairs in the room</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Three hanging banners in the room say “Good people, good coffee, good gooey butter cake.”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Soft alternative music plays over the store’s speakers</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The loudest noises that the customers can hear are the conversations going on in the store that echo throughout the room, the sound of people walking on the creaky wooden floors, the heavy door slamming shut, and the clank and whir of the baristas making coffee</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The seating arrangement varies all over the room, there are two tables in the middle of the room, two in front of the window, eight leather chairs with tables between them, and a granite bar-type seating along one of the walls. Those seem to be used only when there are no available tables</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Upon walking in, the first thing you see is the large counter where the baristas work with glass pastry containers sitting on top of it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When I ordered gooey butter cake, the barista asked “What kind?” I had never heard of different kinds of gooey butter cake, so I asked what options they had. She began listing almost ten different types that she seemed to have memorized. These included cookies and cream, cookie dough, and red velvet</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">After settling with traditional gooey butter cake (since it is the one most highly rated) and a cold brew, I sat down at a table and observed the environment</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Two girls around my age walked right past the store like I did, circling back when they realized they passed the entrance</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The store is located right in the middle of Lafayette Square, in a storefront with apartments on top of it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It has a small boutique on one side and a restaurant on the other</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There is a sign sitting on the sidewalk outside that says “Welcome, Neighbor” with a Park Avenue coffee cup and blueberry muffin on it -- this really exemplifies the environment of the coffee shop, it makes you feel like a part of the Lafayette Square neighborhood&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There is a sign hanging on the wall by the door that states there is a patio in the back of the cafe, which seems very surprising as this is a small space, showing how a little hole in the wall place like this can be much more substantial than what meets the eye, which is a storefront that people don’t even notice</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Outside, rain falls and splatters on the street and the red brick sidewalks</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It feels cozy in here with the rain outside and the warm, yellow lighting inside</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Sitting next to the window, it really gives a small town feel with the boutique storefronts and regular customers who are greeted by name, even though we are in the city still</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Residents of Lafayette Square walk down the streets, some taking their dogs for a walk</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In my opinion, it deserves the ranking of Best Gooey Butter Cake in St. Louis and Coziest Coffee Shop, if that were a thing</span></p></li></ul><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709328376280.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1712266560560.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190716_ParkAvenueCoffee_Exterior_Riley_001 (1).jpg" width="491" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1712266747558.jpg" style="\n      max-width: 50%;\n      height: auto; /* Maintain aspect ratio */\n      /* Additional CSS properties for sizing */\n    ">&nbsp;<br><br>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.63009735587716	-90.19449234008789	1001 Locust St, St. Louis, MO 63101, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "food", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "coffee", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "bakery", "origin": "user"}]	2019-07-16 17:00:00.347	2024-02-08 18:56:21.217	2024-06-24 21:31:57.179	\N
65d8ff06f53d3372e0845963	Cab ride	<p>While driving from my hotrel to the conventi0on centrer, my cab drive queried   - are you here with the group?&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>I'm here on research. &nbsp;</p><p>they are beautiful people. &nbsp;</p><p>these are my people... &nbsp;I'm Muslim.  &nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Arab Muslim. Yemen.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p>grandpa came in 1940. &nbsp;most of family from NYC.&nbsp;</p>	Ct3NKIBgHhYBQZYMjzgv7eFJfNt2	42.33085044630871	-83.06523641311519	1385 Michigan Ave, Detroit, MI 48226, USA	f	f	f	[]	2024-02-23 20:23:17.228	2024-02-23 20:24:38.678	2024-06-21 20:54:54.886	\N
65c9180cf53d3372e084592d	Test Case/Demo	<p><mark data-color="#ffff00" style="background-color: #ffff00; color: inherit">Here is a quick(ish) tutorial for how to use the Where's Religion? mobile app</mark>. </p><p></p><p>I'm using a tablet for this demo but it is also very similar on your phone or other mobile devices. The desktop app has additional functionality and we will demo that later!<br></p><p>For starters, you've noticed that I can type directly into the Field Note using my device's keyboard. If I was on my phone, you'd see the on-screen keyboard. Like this: See. I can type here using the touch pad. But since my tablet has a Bluetooth keyboard, I'm using that for this entry.</p><p><br></p><p>I can also use my stylus if I want to write into this field. But my handwriting is pretty terrible so I will spare you this method. (Oh, I can also drop in emojis if I wanted, but the verdict is out on how to use those in academic prose :) 🤗.</p><p><br></p><p>Here are some functions you will find useful.</p><p><br></p><p>For starters, I can just type as I need to in order to process my environment. But when I'm ready, there are a number of text editing functions that can come in handy.</p><p><br></p><p><strong><u>Text Functions</u></strong></p><p><strong><u><br></u></strong></p><p>For instance, I've just used both the<strong>&nbsp;bold</strong> and the <u>underline</u> functions. You can add these with the toggles above or by using standard keyboard commands.</p><p><br></p><p>Same with <em>italic.&nbsp;</em></p><p><em><br></em></p><p>When I want to quote a source directly, I can make that text stand apart using the quote function:</p><p><br></p><blockquote><p>Here is my quote! (make sure to cite sources lol)</p></blockquote><p>And if I want to list observations, citations, ideas for interpretation, or other elements of ethnographic writing, I can create a bulleted list using that function:</p><p><br></p><ul><li><p>think about sound and smell as well as what I see</p></li><li><p>what is my mood right now and is it affecting my observations?</p></li><li><p>what do i know about the history of this place and where can I find more reliable information to better contextualize my note?</p></li></ul><p><strong><u>Multimedia Functions</u></strong></p><p><strong><u><br></u></strong></p><p>What sets Where's Religion? apart from other broadly available tools is that it prioritizes multi-sensory descriptions and analyses of religion in place. We have a crackerjack team of research computing specialists, developers, programmers, and coders who have built this customized interface and brilliant API frame to collect not just words--our words as researchers--but multimedia as well.</p><p><br></p><p><u>Photos</u></p><p><u><br></u></p><p>When I want to add photos, I click the image icon above. Notice how the photo entered the Field Note where my cursor was located instead of just loading to the image bar at the top of the page. This is designed so we can keep images in the flow of our notes and analyses. Images aren't fluff they are data!</p><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707675866784.jpg" width="4032" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p><span style="font-size: 1em">&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="font-size: 1em"><br></span></p><p>I can also load images from my mobile device if needed:</p><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707675976368.jpg" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p>&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>This photo is from a tattoo parlor where there was a lot of religious iconography on display. The image is added directly into the note flow here, too.</p><p><br></p><p><u>Video</u></p><p><br></p><p>k</p><p>Video files can also be captured with your device's camera or loaded from your device's library. Sorry about that. Lol. But you'll notice that the video option is embedded in the camera function. You may also have noticed that, at this time, video isn't inserted into the text flow.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><u>Audio</u></p><p><br></p><p>The audio function is located to the right of the image/video function on the tool bar. This material loads to the Note but isn't inserted into the text flow. So, as with video, it is important to reference, comment, cite, and unpack in the Note.</p><blockquote><p>Audio file: Test audio for demo.&nbsp;</p></blockquote><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong><u>Metadata</u></strong></p><p><strong><u><br></u></strong></p><p>You may have noticed that this app is called <em>Where's Religion?</em>&nbsp;(If you hadn't, now you know! Ha.) This is in big part because we want to set aside the question of "what is religion" so we can focus attention on the time and place of the materials we encounter in our daily lives. Of course there is still some of the necessary interpretive work of classification that occurs in the act of recognition, but "where's religion" asks a different kind of interaction with material and sources than "what's religion." To achieve this, we have built the app to automatically collect geocoordinates and time stamps. But you can activate and edit them as needed. (For instance, you might NOT want to have a precise geocoordinate (also called a lat/long), especially if you are studying/identifying potentially sensitive or restricted or protected material, like people's front lawns or vulnerable populations).&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><u>Location</u></p><p><u><br></u></p><p>Location information is gathered by clicking the pin drop icon above. If you do nothing, the app will record where you are when you enter your note the first time. You can enter in a manual lat/long or adjust the pin drop on the desktop application. A good rule of thumb, especially when you don't want to have precise geocoordinates attached to your note, is to wait on publishing until you've had a chance to review and edit in the desktop app.</p><p><br></p><p><u>Time</u></p><p><u><br></u></p><p>Time stamps are also automatically collected. But you can adjust them if needed. Say, for instance, you begin your note in the field but you update it or add multimedia files later on. You, as the researcher, determine how the time is recorded. Click the clock icon on the toolbar to open the time function. We strongly encourage every researcher to take good notes about time and to reflect that in your Note and analysis. Religion is never static.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Finishing up and Publishing!</strong></p><p><strong><br></strong></p><p>Once I've created (see what I did there?! ethnography isn't just entering data, it's creating knowledge!) my Note and am satisfied with the materials, I have two options. Oops! I just realized I haven't titled my Note yet! <strong>Now</strong>&nbsp;that I'm done creating my Note (at least for now), I can exit back to my list of entries <em>or</em>&nbsp;I can publish to the global Where's Religion? map. If I don't publish, the Note automatically saves, so I don't have to worry about losing my work.</p><p><br></p><p>I can also create tags to connect my work with other Notes and to help other researchers and users find my own Notes. Click or tap the Tag icon on the tool bar. At this time, we encourage researchers/users to create tags that gesture to broad contexts as well as more specific iterations of religion that their Notes convey. The geocoordinates and time are already collected, so we encourage users to create tags that go beyond the purely descriptive.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Now that my Note is finished and I've created tags, I'm ready to publish! I can alway edit again if needed, or if I want to add to the note at a later time.&nbsp;</p>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	38.610870764933516	-90.31990646737529	2810 Sutton Blvd, Maplewood, MO 63143, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "Coffee shop", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Demo", "origin": "user"}]	2024-02-11 18:50:00	2024-02-11 18:55:08.289	2024-06-21 20:55:09.434	\N
