65d8f9121d442f7c715c9bd9	Dar ul Islam Mosque	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1708718399156.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190617_DarUlIslamMosque_Interior_Safa_001.JPG" width="665" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1708718420371.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190617_DarUlIslamMosque_Interior_Safa_002.JPG" width="662" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1708718435238.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190617_DarUlIslamMosque_Interior_Safa_003.JPG" width="657" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p><span style="font-family: serif">The Dar ul Islam Mosque is considered the most popular in St. Louis for a reason. In its large and spacious glory, it sits in the heart of St. Louis and welcomes people of all ages (there is a day school for children here) and backgrounds. One can see fountains as they walk to the entrance and upon entering, one is met with a host of stalls for different community events. There is a great hustle and bustle of people both inside and outside. Everyone is following the basic rules for modest prayer dressing, but people still have their own style to it. The call for prayer can be heard from far away as the imam speaks into a small microphone in the men’s area. The sound echoes and people quickly begin to rush into their respective sides of the mosque-women and children upstairs, men downstairs. The women’s prayer area isn’t much different from that of the men. It is covered in a patterned green carpet with horizontal lines running parallel to each other for the purposes of providing an outline for how people should stand in prayer-side by side and in rows altogether. The second call for prayer comes about and this time, people stand up and arrange themselves tightly into these lines and silence ensues. Only the imam can be heard and as soon as the prayer is over, the same chatter commences almost immediately.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: serif; font-size: 11pt">The mosque blended in with its surroundings because of how unconventionally “normal” it looked. It didn’t look like a mosque- it was a one and a half story house on the street. Situated right across vintage gun shops, this mosque was inconspicuous in its rock and tree-clad space. Construction cones lined the rocky and hilly entrance and were the only indicator of a new addition to this otherwise old street. Upon entering the mosque through a wooden “front door”, I was met with a sudden surge of chatter. A lot of people had congregated for the Friday sermon already and were waiting for the imam to begin. Makeshift prayer areas and the use of regular house space for religious activity showed that practice doesn’t require special spaces and objects for Muslims. Whether it was the scent of mud and grass, the constant pitter patter of feet against the foam floor covering, or even the sight of “Home Depot” logos on the floor as they sat in congregation, the Muslims’ practice continued just as it would in a large intricate mosque elsewhere.</span></p><p></p><ol><li><p><span style="font-family: serif; font-size: 16px">Although people of many backgrounds come here, most seem to be of South-Asian and Arabic origin. Does this change how people from other backgrounds feel when it comes to a feeling of communion? (does your background matter when you come together to pray?)</span></p></li><li><p><span style="font-family: serif; font-size: 16px">Some mosques have strictly separate prayer areas for women that don’t allow them to see the men’s area. Does the view of the imam from the women’s area at Dar ul-Islam have any effect on the feelings of inclusion amongst women?</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: serif; font-size: 16px">Do Muslims feel different when they practice in a house as opposed to a mosque? Do they feel more relaxed as a result?</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: serif; font-size: 16px">What impact does the size of a community have? Friday prayers are usually performed in large groups. Does a smaller group size of people make this prayer feel less elaborate?</span></p></li></ol>	h5IM6ZhPGxVEAdSpwywH4C1OZLB2	38.6031716057153	-90.49618721008301	517 Weidman Rd, Ballwin, MO 63011, USA	f	f	f	[]	2019-06-17 17:00:36.924	2024-02-23 19:59:14.468	2024-06-21 21:25:18.207	\N
