65f4aab95d508d3d8495237c	St. Charles Antique Mall	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1710533657175.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190815_StCharlesAntiqueMall_Trunk_Safa.JPG" width="724" style="aspect-ratio: 0.5627198124267292"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1710533591823.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190815_StCharlesAntiqueMall_JesusSacredHeartBust_Safa.JPG" width="864" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1710533558942.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190815_StCharlesAntiqueMall_Cross_Safa.JPG" width="543" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1710533572204.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190815_StCharlesAntiqueMall_Dolls_Safa.JPG" width="400" style="aspect-ratio: 0.5622188905547226"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1710533632914.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190815_StCharlesAntiqueMall_LewisGlass_Safa.JPG" width="460" style="aspect-ratio: 0.5627198124267292"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The antique mall was really large. It had three main galleries, all on one level. As soon as you entered, you were met with a continuous and monotonous arrangement, row after row of old antiques. I tried to figure out if the arrangement had some sort of pattern to it. Maybe a certain aisle of rows was dedicated to artwork, the next one to radio, etc., but I couldn’t figure it out. Everything about the types of antiques and how they were displayed was quite random. For obvious reasons, I tried to look for hints of religion here. Perhaps old “Mary and Jesus” statues would catch my eye at some point. I eventually asked the staff at the front if and where I could find such pieces and they immediately directed me to three distinct gallery and aisle numbers- it was interesting to see how they did this so quickly all by memory; finding two to three articles in a space that contained thousands really shows that the people who work there are well-versed in knowledge of these objects. Not a lot of people were present at the time and they all looked around and left. No one bought anything and it seemed to me that the purpose of visiting was just recreational. Maybe there’s something about viewing rich history through objects that you can touch and feel that connects people to different parts of the past, perhaps even their own. At least that’s how I felt when I came across old action figures in the rear gallery.</span></p><p></p><p></p><ol><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Is the purpose of visiting an antique mall more to look at things or do visitors often make purchases as well?</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Although St Louis has a variety of religions that it caters to, it is majority Protestant. How come most pieces here are more culturally instead of religiously inclined?</span></p></li></ol><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1710533747496.mp4">Video 1</a></p>	h5IM6ZhPGxVEAdSpwywH4C1OZLB2	38.74982027706614	-90.56459963321686	E, 3400 S St Peters Pkwy, St Peters, MO 63303, USA	f	f	f	[]	2019-08-15 17:00:38.924	2024-03-15 20:08:25.647	2024-06-21 21:25:20.091	\N
