65eb6c3cce3cc6a72dfc3800	Saint Joseph Radio	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709928089272.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_StJosephRadioStJoesJava_Interior_Statues_Safa.JPG" width="698" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709928047399.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_StJosephRadioStJoesJava_Interior_RecordingEquipment_Safa.JPG" width="832" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709928066746.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_StJosephRadioStJoesJava_Interior_Safa_001.JPG" width="816" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709928076974.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_StJosephRadioStJoesJava_Interior_Safa_002.JPG" width="830" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709928125346.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_StJosephRadioStJoesJava_PhotoWall_Detail_Safa_002.JPG" width="801" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709928112760.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_StJosephRadioStJoesJava_PhotoWall_Detail_Safa_001.JPG" width="808" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709928136377.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_StJosephRadioStJoesJava_PhotoWall_Safa.JPG" width="739" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709927905565.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">When we think of religion, we think of rituals, sacred spaces, holy persons, and even gatherings amongst religious communities. We essentially construct a vivid picture of a community. Often, we also look attribute the lack of communion to perhaps a lack of bonds between individuals. Of course, rushing to conclusions such as these skews and narrows down our view of community and religion. Religion need not be limited to traditional rituals and practices- this would make it unable to adapt to our progressing society. The use of radio is one such method that has made its way to the realm of religion in our present-day and age. Radio not only brings people together in the way traditional rituals do, but it also helps reach people farther away from a traditional grasp.</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">St. Joseph’s Radio in St Peter’s Missouri is one such organization that makes use of radio to spread the word of Catholicism. Upon entering, what you will find may seem like an ordinary gift shop, with neat stacks of books, CDs, artwork and other articles in one corner, along with a comfy wooden seating area with coffee in the main space. However, you simply cannot miss the rest of the space which contains radio recording rooms, microphones and other apparatus, beautiful photo walls clad with collages of visitors ranging from Hollywood stars to ordinary folk, and so on. Clearly, St. Joeseph’s radio brings out its identity as a radio broadcaster for the people of the Christian faith.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">As modern as it may seem, however, St. Joseph's Radio has a long and rich history that began with the need to provide correct information about Catholicism and by using means accessible to most people at that time. Hence, the main source of media was, of course, the radio, and their mission was simple- provide the truth about Catholicism. This served as all the more important of a goal given that Catholicism was and still is misunderstood by many people, as is any religion for a matter of fact.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br>Transcription of Interview:</p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: Why a radio station?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: we’re better described as a production company. We produce materials to put on radio and tv. We have our own tv channel with catholic media, Christian radio rather. You get it on the internet, with roku on your tv, and for the radio programs, we do live on Saturdays at 1200 with Covenant Radio. Covenant radio is the local catholic radio station and they have over 35 stations in the area, and we produce a program that goes on air on Saturdays at about 12 o clock. Sometimes we have prerecorded programs something that was probably applicable to the season or whatever in the past and that’s , when u see our work, our intention is to provide truthful commentary about our faith to the general public. In doing so ,we sometimes record local speakers to share that with the general public. And Lou Cortese, the director, is very good about analyzing which program would go good along with different areas. We have a studio in California , orange county, and we produce programs there as well as here. Sometimes we’re called upon to record programs from different areas like, we went to Pittsburgh last year, and this year, we’re going to New Orleans to record the international leaders conference for Magnifica Organization. So that’s kind of, that’s our intention, to help people understand what is true and about our faith, more about our faith so that they&nbsp; can have hope in their life.</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: and Is there anything that made u choose radio over other means to communicate with people?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: uhm, no its just we started 35 years ago, so that was, at that time, the best source of communication. It was before a lot of current things we use now. We use internet media and we now have grown to be able to do all of that actually, you can see with our website, and we have 247 livestreaming on the website. We also have with STAN tv, over 100 programs that are being watched in over 45 countries across the world. We got a response from somebody in Pakistan not too long ago saying that they really enjoyed the program and things like that. So, its encouraging, and rly we just want to make use of the things&nbsp; that god provides for us, and we know a person that has a a great source of thinking and about this or that subject, and we’d like to share that with the world.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: how has current media helped you further your message?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: well, only because its current and popular. And a lot of people spend their eyes and ears glues either to the iphone or ipad, or a tv, and so we have all of that available.&nbsp; We have our programming available on all of that media that we possibly can. We’re looking at a twitter page right now.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: that’d be a good idea.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: you think so?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: yeah, because youngsters and most people are comfortable with messages that are shorter and to-the-point.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: yeah sure. I can relate to that. Because I get emails from people with these lengthy podcasts and I don’t have time to listen to all of that. But there is so much out there that is twisting the truth around s that people may be confused about many different things. So we’re grateful&nbsp;that we have the opportunity to put out the truth and anyone who knows our station knows that they’re gonna get the right information, so we’re grateful to be able to do that, and the current media helps, but its uh, we try to jump in wherever we can.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: what would u say your focal audience is, with your programming?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: the audience, we expect, well, we have children’s programs as well as ageless programs, you know for any age. For ex, we have a program on death and dying produced by this priest who experienced that in his life. he was given 3 months to live and he lived for 10 years after that, and he has the most remarkable way of describing death and dying in little stories. He tells little stories about experience he had, like if you stopped at an automobile accident and he tells the story of how that interaction went and ,uh, he tells a story of a gentlemen who learned how to pray to the Lord and he was at his bedside when he was bout to die, and he describes all of the interactions between he and the gentleman uh so, with these little stories, you get a better idea of death and dying , you know. He also speaks about the God that I love and the particular circumstances of children when they’re dying. And his name was Father Arkfeld. And he passed away because of his cancer but he left us all his wonderful material. Cant say I’ve heard anyone speak about a sensitive subject as well as he. So we have the truth about certain areas of life and death that a lot of people don’t hear, yet you hear a lot of twisted truth or personal opinions or people have a way of dismissing kind of the tough things of life and we don’t , we just rather stay with the truth. We feel that that is going to bring you the most peace in your life, and the fact that faith is a tremendous part of everyone’s life. We fell that everyone is created for the experience of faith and the” god that I love”, that’s one of father Arkfeld’s talks. He describes our relationship with god, so we have people that were converted to the faith. Like Roselyn moss, who is now sister Miriam, she converted from Hebrew to Catholicism. And the way she describes it is, you can’t help but be impressed, especially when she describes the Holy Eucharist. We believe as Catholics that the Eucharist is the body and blood of Christ as it is said in the Scripture, and yet a lot of people are apprehensive about believing that truth. So we are grateful to share the truth with anybody that’s listening. You know sometimes people will just be switching channels on the radio and they’ll back to us and say “oh I was skimming through the radio and I heard your talk” and they’re very grateful. So we don’t need to make it a point to talk down anybody else’s’ religion or thoughts because that’s besides the point. Everything is between you and God, so we do want to take the opportunity to go ahead and tell what we understand the truth is through the voices of whoever the Lord puts in our </span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="http://path.so"><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">path.so</span></a><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt"> that’s kind of a big issue with us and we’re grateful to be able to serve the world in that capacity.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: when it comes to your interactions with people, what are popular things/topics that people like to know/talk about?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: much of the conversations have to do with family crisis, you know, people that have gone astray or have gone into a pattern of not attending church anymore and do we have any ideas or suggestions, that’s one big issue. We have grandparents that are worried about their grandchildren, or husbands and wives with their marriage situation, um and so we’re, we have so many resources that can really help people understand what’s going on. Like today, I guess there’s always been a battle, I know we have bishop Herman, who speaks about spiritual warfare, and that’s a very common issue although a lot of people don’t realize that. I know that one time I personally had an opportunity to invite a seminarian to teach in a school, at junior high, and my wish that I gave him, I wanted him to describe to the junior high students the issue of spiritual warfare. There’s a battle out there., there’s people that want to drag you down and make you think there’s no god and there’s no hope and you just do what everyone else does without any consideration of its moral value. So this young man was very good at approaching that subject. I think he used this classes books, I think of Chesterton maybe, or cs Lewis, one classic book that referred to that, and so he did a very wonderful job. I feel like those kids, when they graduated form 8</span><sup><span style="font-size: 0.6em">th</span></sup><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt"> grade, would have an idea of how to approach life, how to make choices with friends, and everything else. So once you realize there’s a battle going on, a lot of times people don’t understand it that there’s any battle going on, you know, “why would anyone want to convince me of anything”, and yet these same young people are open to hearing what the popular thing is to do and follow it, so we hope to interfere with that in a way by saying there are choices you can make, and there are good choices, better choices, and let them understand that life is about choosing. The lord welcomes everyone with his heart to live a life of faith and eventually upon death to be with him in heaven, but we do have to make choices to make that happen.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: is it easier to talk to the youth rather than to an adult who can’t have his/her mind changed?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: that’s a good point because there are adults that think they know everything, well there are also some young people that think they know everything , so you do have to approach it in a common sense pattern you have to demonstrate by examples and by real life interviews to show the difficulties that real life people ran into and how they overcame. So many instances, I was reading&nbsp; about a child who was very sick , perhaps given few months to live&nbsp; and the family was tremendously upset as you can imagine, but when they learned to turn themselves to the Lord and surrender their feelings and their thoughts to the faith that we have, then they turned to prayer, and prayer gave them peace. And in some instances the child can be heled or cured, and in some instances, he won’t be, so what the faith dimension gives you and answer that you can be peaceful with, and the answer would be perhaps the Lord understands that this life so far has been as productive as is going to be and/or that the child would be healed because there’s a lot more to be dine with his life or her life. And the Lord gives us every opportunity no matter what our circumstance is, but families often question why these things are allowed, “why is god not intervening “. They portray god as something that takes away all evil, not that he couldn’t , but he’s given us a free will, and a&nbsp; free will is the one thing that demonstrates to god that we really love him, because we’ve made a choice there o the right thing, to do the best thing. In our ignorance sometimes, we do make the wrong choices, and we feel god is ready to forgive us if we ask for it, and so we ask him for his grace to lead us in the right direction. So a lot of the questions that we get are related to family issues or relationship issues with people.</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: how does coming here facilitate this conversation? How do the radio station and coffee shop come together?</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: well we have the coffee shop because different groups like to come in and we may have a speaker for them, like we have prayer groups come and they have their prayer group meetings here, and the environment, because we have the chapel , is a good environment for them. Its centrally located for many areas, they might have a prayer group or other kinds of meetings and its something that will hep them persevere. The prayer could meet at different location s, at church or be it somebody’s home, but this is kind of a neutral area and besides, we have so man sources of information and help that they might want to use with our tv, so that’s a good opening. We also like to have volunteers come in and offer their help with different projects. It could be related to the coffee ship or the gift shop, putting things together. We currently have a young man who is totally blind and disabled. He comes with his caregiver and they’re looking for work… and we have little jobs for them to do like count cards into packages of 50 or something, he could do that even if he’s blind. Different people come in and volunteer, whatever their talent or ability is, we need their help.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Safa: there’s always something for everyone.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Ann: yes, it's a very broad place of talent that can used.</span></p><p><br></p>	h5IM6ZhPGxVEAdSpwywH4C1OZLB2	38.7611946795313	-90.52877068519592	1985 Bluestone Dr, St Charles, MO 63303, USA	f	f	f	[]	2019-07-12 17:00:01.992	2024-03-08 19:51:24.986	2024-06-21 21:25:18.9	\N
65f4aff55d508d3d8495237d	United Hebrew Congregation	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1710534449122.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190724_UnitedHebrewCongregation_Altar_Safa.JPG" width="597" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1710534480689.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190724_UnitedHebrewCongregation_Altar_Sculpture_Safa.JPG" width="590" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1710534495896.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190724_UnitedHebrewCongregation_Tanakh_Safa.JPG" width="580" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Two large doors made way for what looked like a regular office setting- oak walls, sleek minimalist architecture, and the same soft white light that most fancy hotels make use of in their lobbies. Upon entering I heard absolutely nothing. The only way I knew where I needed to go was through a muffled voice at the foot of the door-the intercom buzzed and asked me for my name and why I visited. I then made my way to the main visitor’s office and met the owner of the gift shop, Ms. Iris. Jewish faith has a rich history, as she explained to me while giving me a tour of the empty synagogue. Most people only really visited on the day of Shabbat, which is something she wanted me to come to and experience. That is what I did three days later on a Friday evening (typical for Jewish Shabbat timing). Unlike the atmosphere during my initial walkthrough, the air this time was full of all kinds of sensory triggers. Besides the freezing cold air conditioning that the humid weather mandated that day, a warm and ambiguous scent wafted through the long hallway as I walked towards the open meeting hall. Furthermore, the loud and uneven sounds of chatter bounced back and forth through the big hall and became even clearer as I reached the hall. Around five to six officials stood at the foot of the hall, and the hall itself was full of food, people, and laughter.</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Not too soon after I introduced myself to a group of people at the only table that wasn’t full, I headed into the main service room and witnessed a traditional Jewish service. The service was very custom and rule based in how the crowd responded to certain command words by the Rabbi at the end of certain verses or hymns, but that’s about where the formalities ended. The topics of sermons, the attitude and language of the Rabbi and Assistant Rabbi all had a casual vibe. At one point, the Rabbi was joking about </span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="http://Amazon.com"><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Amazon.com</span></a><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">. Needless to say, people at this Hebrew Congregation were much more of cultural relativists than those of their other sects, and this isn’t just an assumption but something they insisted on letting me know as soon as I walked in.</span></p><p><br></p>	h5IM6ZhPGxVEAdSpwywH4C1OZLB2	38.64769589972053	-90.50976991653442	13742 Conway Rd, St. Louis, MO 63141, USA	f	f	f	[]	2019-07-24 17:00:22.123	2024-03-15 20:30:45.727	2024-06-21 21:25:20.629	\N
