66267f67496553f03ae35c78	Atheists United Interview	<p><em>This is an interview transcript created by a CSUN undergraduate as part of her research project for the RS capstone class. The student is identified as Int (interviewer) and the respondents are identified as R1 and R2. </em></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Okay. And how did you become a member of Atheist United?</p><p>[R1] :</p><p>Atheist United.</p><p>Simply one day during the pandemic, I was kind of just bored. And, um, I was like, this is when</p><p>I became comfortable with being a, uh, an atheist. And I just, I was kind of bored of the. online</p><p>space. You know, I wanted to meet people like me physically.</p><p>Uh, and so I legit just looked up on Google atheist meetups. Atheist United was the first one that</p><p>came up on meetup. com literally. And they had a food distribution that weekend and I had</p><p>volunteer hours from work and so I just took it. And I was like, the worst case scenario is that I</p><p>do some good, don't vibe with the group.</p><p>Uh, best case, I do some good and I vibe with the group. So the best case scenario won out. Oh,</p><p>wow. And how long have you been a member, um, since the pandemic happened? Uh, yes, since</p><p>the pandemic. And so it's been like two, three years, I would like to say I haven't kept track, but</p><p>it's been, it's been multiple years.</p><p>It's been multiple years. I can say.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Okay, awesome. And okay, I'm gonna just bounce back and forth. Is that okay?</p><p>[R1] &amp; [R2] :</p><p>Okay. Yeah. Okay, awesome.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>So, [R2], um, why do you keep coming back to Atheist United?</p><p>[R2] :</p><p>I keep coming back because, um, it just feels, I like the being able to have like minded people in</p><p>regards of the subject of non beliefs, non believers.</p><p>Um, there's times where I'm working, I feel like I'm the only one that feels that way. As are</p><p>constantly people telling me, you know, God may, only God knows, or, uh, only, uh, just, just</p><p>that, that, that reference to just a higher power is just, I find it silly. But when I meet up with this</p><p>group of people, it's just nice not to hear that all the time and have other conversations besides</p><p>just, so that's why I really enjoy hanging out with them.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Okay, and what role do you think this plays in your life?</p><p>[R2] :</p><p>What role? Uh, I guess it's just for my own psychological, uh, healthiness, I guess. Uh, just so</p><p>much, so much religious stuff that I see every day. It's not a depression thing. It's just like, I just</p><p>wish people would look beyond that and maybe question their beliefs, you know, and I've come</p><p>across to where people have the beliefs and I don't think they understand them.</p><p>So with this group, I've, I've been able to, to think about other things and just reflect on other</p><p>subjects, just not just that, you know, and it's helped me just myself. And it's just, it feels good to</p><p>know that I'm not the only one.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>That's awesome. And [R1], how about you? What about yourself? Why do you keep coming</p><p>back?</p><p>No, it's just a reinforcement of my values, you know? Um, When I, when I first came to Atheist</p><p>United as a volunteer at the food distribution and meeting Evan and just seeing how friendly and</p><p>personable and, you know, just positive that guy could be and, and attracting that kind of energy</p><p>with everyone else.</p><p>Everyone was friendly and positive. It really. And it really destroyed this, you know, I guess</p><p>this cultural myth that atheists are nothing more than these nihilistic, you know, no meaning at</p><p>all people who just want to sit around and complain about God. It just was the complete</p><p>opposite of that, you know, like the everyone was very much had a purpose when being there.</p><p>Sure, it wasn't this universal alignment purpose. It wasn't this mega meta battle of good and evil.</p><p>It was just simply humans getting together and doing good for each other. And that really</p><p>inspired me into, you know, thinking about my own value. And even then, um, why I became a</p><p>humanist or why I identified myself as a humanist was because one of the members was a</p><p>humanist herself.</p><p>And so because of that, I was like, Oh, what, what, what is humanism? And so looking into that</p><p>and then thus You know, be, you know, understanding the values of that was like, wow, okay,</p><p>like this is, this is it. This is the, you know, this is what I am, you know, I never knew the word</p><p>for it. And I wouldn't have known the word for it if I didn't explore these secular spaces, because</p><p>you're not going to hear this kind of talk in a church, or traditional church, I would say, I'm sure</p><p>you know, Unitarian Universalist Church would say, Bring up the term, uh, humanism and</p><p>atheism, but, you know, in, in like, you know, in theistic churches, like Christianity or something</p><p>like that, they, they tend to have such a bad, you know, rap about just those two terms that it</p><p>almost feels like.</p><p>The same term as communism, you know, it makes it, they, they, they villainize it to the point</p><p>that you don't even know what it means. And you're kind of scared to figure out what it actually</p><p>means. And when you do, it's kind of like, Oh, that's it. I, you know, like once, once that</p><p>boogeyman is gone, you're just like, great.</p><p>I, you know, this is who I am. This is why, what I like. And, you know, and being with these</p><p>people just keep reinforcing the faith. My values and, you know, making me proud to be an</p><p>atheist and a humanist.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Awesome. And what, uh, follow up question, what role does this play in your life?</p><p>[R1] :</p><p>You know, every day it just makes me, you know, think about my choices. You know, in life,</p><p>when there is a way to help people not to, you know, and just to do it with no expectation of</p><p>some higher reward, you know, most of the, you know, most of the time, if not all the time,</p><p>whenever I'm thinking about the turmoil, the political, um, leanings of, of our country and our</p><p>division, um, it helps because not only am I thinking about, you know, What can I do to help</p><p>other people, you know, as the old saying goes about like planting trees on the shade that you'll</p><p>never feel of.</p><p>It's, it's more of just like, I'm only here for a limited amount of time. I don't know what happens</p><p>after most likely nothing. But what can I do in this limited amount of time to help the most</p><p>amount of people? And that reinforces and, you know, influences my decisions, who I'm friends</p><p>with. In fact, even with today's misinformation.</p><p>You know, our Atheist United tends to have like this critical thinking aspect of it all. We question</p><p>a lot of things. We have Science Sundays that encourages this kind of critical thinking. And I've</p><p>taken those kinds of skills and I've applied them into my own life to Not just the bunk to avoid</p><p>these disinformation pitfalls.</p><p>I think that after we saw what the bridge collapse and, um, with the solar eclipse just recently, we</p><p>need more of that. We need to stop trying to, you know, just believe everything that we see on</p><p>the Internet, just the same as we don't believe everything that we see on TV. It seems like with</p><p>just the Internet, which is more people saying such things.</p><p>We are just, we tend to believe it more often. And, you know, and for me, it's just like, I</p><p>questioned a lot of things, but not to the point of conspiracy, but to the point of like, I dealt what</p><p>you're saying, not because I don't want to believe you, but I need more evidence to believe you.</p><p>Yeah. Just be like skepticism, right?</p><p>Would you be skepticism? Not to the point of I'm wearing a tinfoil hat.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Sorry, I didn't mean to cut you off there. Sorry. No, it's exactly what it is. Healthy skepticism.</p><p>Okay. So let's go on to the next question. I'm so fast. I'm like, Oh my God. I may get a PhD now.</p><p>Um, So question number three, [R2], some scholars suggest that atheism is a sect of religion</p><p>that is filling religious needs. What do you think about that?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>It annoys me and annoys the heck out of me. We'll use another word, but I have friends that, that</p><p>constantly tease me about that, that it's replacing, or it is a church that I'm attending on a Sunday,</p><p>you know, they, they, And instead of asking questions of why I'm attending this and why I feel</p><p>the way I feel, um, I think they're afraid to ask me the questions that they know they doubt</p><p>things, you know?</p><p>And by asking me and knowing who I am, it would, it would reinforce those thoughts, and I</p><p>think they're afraid of those, those questions to be answered in my thought, in my, in my words.</p><p>Uh, it, it, it gets, It gets annoying. It gets annoying after a while, like, yeah, you guys don't seem</p><p>to understand, and instead of just Creating a thought about what we do and what we're all about</p><p>and why not ask me instead of just teasing about it, you know, so yeah,</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>what about you, [R1]? Um, do you need me to repeat the question or no?</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>No, I think that, um, this is another example of that vilification of the word atheism, right?</p><p>Because if we Talk about atheism. Atheism is just an answer to one question. Do you believe in</p><p>any gods or gods? And there's two camps, you know, there's yes. And then there's no.</p><p>And personally for me, um, when I've heard this, I've heard this too, you know, I think it's just to</p><p>try to bring. Atheism to the level of religion, either up or down it, but it has to be the same level</p><p>because you don't have the same kind of vilification when it comes to any other opinions, right?</p><p>Um, but people tend to make, you know, something that they're trying to vilify the same thing as</p><p>them, maybe because it's easier to attack it or something like that.</p><p>But for me, it's, you know, It's hard to, like, explain to them, atheism is not a religion. It's, you</p><p>know, when you're in that group, right, we don't agree on everything, you know? It's not, we're</p><p>not lock and step with every single thing. I mean, We have in Atheist United, you know,</p><p>committee or board meetings, um, I mean, we have, you know, arguments and all the time about</p><p>just like minor things, you know, not every atheist is a vegan, even though it's more ethically</p><p>sound to be more vegan or vegetarian, and some atheists are Our, you know, hardcore mediators,</p><p>you know, and it's just like, well, there you go.</p><p>Like there's an example of just like, not all of us just locking and stepping with everything that</p><p>we talk about. And it's just when people say, Oh, atheism is just another religion. It just kind of</p><p>shows the ignorance of it all because you know, what is a religion, you know, how do you</p><p>identify a religion with atheism?</p><p>Like, where do you find that just because we just disagree on that one. Aspect, but we still have</p><p>the community because we're humans. We're social creatures. We need community in order to</p><p>thrive and survive, but you can't just say that because You know, we just disagree on the God</p><p>aspect and yet we just do this similar things as religions like, you know, give food to the poor or</p><p>donate blood or donate money to colleges or to need it to students in need.</p><p>That isn't an exclusive religious thing. Right. That is something that religion has taken up and</p><p>decided that as a society, we just decided that like, Oh, only religious people can work in soup</p><p>kitchens or religious organizations can organize food drives. No, anyone can do that. Religions</p><p>just happen to have the resources to do that.</p><p>If you, if we spread these resources and these financial powers out to people who want to do that,</p><p>but just aren't affiliated or don't want to be affiliated with religions, there'd be so many more. But</p><p>if, you know, religions tend to have a monopoly and when we have Atheists United who are</p><p>trying to do good, people just tend to lump us in all together as, as a religion, which is.</p><p>kind of just like, you know, minimizing our work a bit. It's, it's the whole idea of we, what is,</p><p>how does it go? We fear that what we don't understand, you know, they're just like, they don't</p><p>want to understand it. So we're going to go ahead and fear it and make fun of it. Make fun of it.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Yeah. So these are, so my question is pertaining to like religious studies scholars, not necessarily</p><p>Um, not necessarily like people in the community that don't understand atheism or like people</p><p>like religious studies scholars look at atheists and I'm wondering if it's just the word religion is</p><p>triggering.</p><p>So if, if we took the word. religion out, we're just looking at it as a sense of like a collective</p><p>group identity. And so if we look at the word religion is very triggering for like a lot of people.</p><p>And so because of Western colonial powers and you know, corrupt Christianity and whatnot.</p><p>Um, it has a negative stigma attached to it, especially with all of the trauma and the violence and</p><p>everything that has happened.</p><p>Um, so are you, are you mostly mainly asking, like, Like, so people like, you know, like in</p><p>university people are discussing or in academia are looking at atheism as like, it's fulfilling, is it</p><p>fulfilling a social function of religion in the sense that it's creating a community, not that not</p><p>living in like a duality, dualistic, good versus evil.</p><p>Nothing like that. Just purely. As you were saying, like, it's, um, a create like a sense of</p><p>community and a gathering point and a ideology, um, not necessarily in a way to, unfortunately,</p><p>within the religious studies scholar, uh, religious studies community, you know, we have major</p><p>religions and minor religions.</p><p>And so the major religions would consist of Christianity, Islam, Judaism, Hinduism. And so a lot</p><p>of scholars previously only wrote about that. Wrote about these as like, these are like the big</p><p>ones and then everything else like indigenous religion, African diasporic religions, or like minor</p><p>religions, and it's just kind of looking at atheists like, like we're stuck with the term or word</p><p>religion because so many people know this word.</p><p>And in a sense, it's, there's a phenomenon happening. And so if we're looking at like religion as</p><p>a phenomenon of like groups. And so I was just curious in the sense of like, outside, um, like,</p><p>let's say like, not family members looking at atheism and projecting their ideas and their values. I</p><p>was just curious as far as like academics, Looking at atheism or atheists as As a religion slash</p><p>the social function of religion, meaning getting together community and whatnot.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>Oh, okay. So, as like a social function. Um, I guess it is, you know, it really depends. I mean,</p><p>when it comes to atheists. There, there is this term of like, uh, like hurting cats because it is kind</p><p>of hard to get a group of atheists together as, uh, you know, just to do something. A lot of people</p><p>are, are, um, affected by the, you know, the church hurting them or their religion hurting them in</p><p>general.</p><p>And being part of another group or who have the same. Uh, markers, I guess, like just coming</p><p>together, talking about similar topics, uh, agreeing on the broad strokes and, um, you know, a lot</p><p>of people tend to be wary about that and, and, and, you know, in, in, in the, in the social aspect of</p><p>it, in the, in the social way that we use religion Is to is for people to get together and with</p><p>atheism, it's, it's kind of hard because like, there's not that many groups.</p><p>I mean, atheists United is the biggest atheist group, right. And that's not that many people. And,</p><p>you know, it's kind of hard to answer that kind of question, but I do think with the people that are</p><p>in that group, uh, with the regulars and the members who send money and also who go to the</p><p>events. Um, I guess in that kind of way it is, but like, I would say that it's, it's, it's a similar as</p><p>saying a car and a truck are vehicles, but they have different purposes, sure.</p><p>They can both get you from A to B and service, you know. What you need. But sometimes, you</p><p>know, we need to understand and distinguish the difference. You cannot just point out a truck and</p><p>say it's a car and you cannot point out a car and say it's a truck because you're expecting different</p><p>purposes out of it.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>I have to agree with [R1]. You put that so well. I misunderstood the question, but yeah, that</p><p>completely makes sense the way you put it. Yeah, it does. It does help. It is a, not a</p><p>replacement, but I completely understood what you meant by herding cats and the vehicle, you</p><p>know, comparison. Thanks, [R1]. Yeah, that's Awesome.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Okay. So let's look at my, um, question number four. Um, I'll go with [R2] first. Do people in</p><p>your life know that you're an atheist? Your friends, family, and coworkers?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>Yes, they all know. And if they don't know, they'll find out. I have stickers on my car. I have a</p><p>big flag in front of my house. Um, I, I, I want them to know that I am, and I want them to</p><p>understand that I want, I like to treat people with kindness and respect.</p><p>And of course I have my thresholds like every human being, but when they see who I am, and</p><p>the way I approach things, And I hope they take that away from me, from me and say, look,</p><p>[R2] is this way and he's not affiliated or believes in all the stuff that I believe. Maybe there's</p><p>something to look into about that.</p><p>And what kind of response do they share with you about your atheism? Uh, response. You mean</p><p>like in the beginning when they first find out or after they already know after years? Um, let's</p><p>talk about both. How are they in the beginning? And then how are they now? In the beginning,</p><p>they, of course, one of them is fear.</p><p>The questions are like, Oh, you believe the first thing that comes out of the mouth. So you</p><p>believe in the devil? No, I don't. You know, I don't believe in that. Um, it's just like, I have no, I</p><p>no longer hold that belief of a higher power. And my thoughts as far as approaching life is</p><p>questioning things. And I need more information is something I don't understand.</p><p>That's how I move forward. And that's what I tell them. Um, after they know that I'm an atheist,</p><p>um, it's so funny. They, they come to me and ask me questions. What I think about things that</p><p>have come out in the news in regards of religion, like based on religion or asked other people I've</p><p>done because of religion.</p><p>And I love those conversations, you know, I'll give them the thoughts on that. And then even</p><p>then when they, when they, they recite a Bible verse or something and they'll ask me what I think</p><p>about it. I, I do enjoy those conversations and I tell everybody, anybody and everybody when</p><p>they first meet me, go ahead and tell them I don't care about that.</p><p>I've had people where they know I'm an atheist, but they don't want to ask questions or they're</p><p>afraid to ask me or they're afraid to approach me. As time goes on, of course, they'll start getting</p><p>curious. And they can see who I am, you know, and I'm hoping it's a good thing that, you know,</p><p>the way I, I, I held myself in front of them to show, hey, look.</p><p>I hold this belief, or non belief, and I see the world differently from you.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Yeah, and um, [R1], do your friends, family, and co workers know that you're an atheist? Yeah,</p><p>I mean, it's no worries, uh, atheists, humanists,</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>it's kind of inter, uh, interchangeable for me. Uh, but yes, they do. Um, at first it was definitely</p><p>an uncomfortable conversation with, um, especially with my father in law.</p><p>Um, You know, because like I took pictures of me being at my first food distribution and literally</p><p>that same weekend, he wanted to go talk and I hold him into a high regard. I respect him, uh, at</p><p>a high regard and everything. And it was just like, he was kind of confused because for a period</p><p>of time before that, I was, um, looking into Christianity.</p><p>Actually, I was. You know, after getting, you know, getting settled down in California, I went on</p><p>this spiritual journey, myself, and I was like, well, let me try, you know, seeing about</p><p>Christianity. Curious about it. I was asking him questions, I was going to his, to uh, services,</p><p>and, you know, when I just realized it just wasn't for me, I, and went to Atheist United and</p><p>started to become more open about being an atheist and a non believer, um, it was definitely</p><p>some growing pains.</p><p>Because, you know, I feel like when you open that door a little bit to religion and then you shut</p><p>it again, simply because it's just like, man, you know, I just can't, I just can't believe it. Um,</p><p>people get really offended or really, really hurt or betrayed by that. My wife herself, she, she</p><p>was just like, she, she gave me the freedom and it was just like, you know, just do whatever you</p><p>feel like it's right.</p><p>And, um, my friends, they're, they're all nonbelievers generally, and they just don't care, but, um,</p><p>yeah, you know, like nobody really talks about it, you know, at, at my, um, you know, when we</p><p>get to family get togethers and stuff like that. Um, everyone knows I have a bumper sticker in my</p><p>car that says drive carefully, there is no heaven.</p><p>And, um, you know, I just, I just leave it out. I just leave it out there. And yeah. If people are</p><p>curious, they can talk and ask. Uh, I don't, you know, I, I see it as more of just like, as long as</p><p>I'm kind, as long as I'm humanistic, as long as I'm generous, as long as I'm, you know, you</p><p>know, always trying my best to be the best version of myself, it really doesn't matter if their fear</p><p>blocks that because eventually they're just going to see.</p><p>Because now they know. Now they have a non believer in their family. They have an atheist in</p><p>their family of Christians. And now that, you know, now that I'm in their head, whenever</p><p>somebody's gonna be talking about an atheist in the future in a bad light, they're gonna be</p><p>thinking about me, and they're like, He doesn't act like that, though.</p><p>He's not upset. He's not depressed. He's not living a life of meaningless and going around</p><p>sinning. He's normal. He, you know, he's not a criminal. He's, he's perfectly fine. He's doing well</p><p>for his life. He's well respected. So I feel like just being in that presence and just acting as, as I</p><p>am is enough to dispel, again, that myth of villainy of, and atheism at the same time.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>And, oh no, I guess I'll save this for, um, after. Um, my next question is. Um, how long have,</p><p>like, my question is in regards to, in regard, with regard to how long have members stayed in</p><p>Atheist United? And, like, do people ever leave Atheist United? And if so, like, do you know</p><p>why?</p><p>[R2] :</p><p>I wouldn't know the answer to that.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>I wouldn't know. Um, yeah, maybe click clicking. I have very limited knowledge on that, but</p><p>from what I can say, what I have observed, uh, and what I've heard from, um, other members,</p><p>um, as time has gone on, and as Atheist United, because Atheist United has been around For a</p><p>long time decades Um, and it used to be this type of group, but as it became more progressive</p><p>and stuff like that people left because they didn't like how it was going and And other people</p><p>because some people just wanted to become like a group just to be a god defying group And</p><p>others kind of wanted to take it to a more communal aspect and some people just didn't like that</p><p>um You I know personally for myself, this new change has kept me in the group for longer, you</p><p>know, I have been on a member of the Atheist United for a long time at this point, and it's</p><p>because like, Atheists United, there's, there's enough of God defying groups, you know?</p><p>There's not many community defying groups which are desperately needed for those third</p><p>spaces. And I think Atheists United is going in the right direction of that. I just think that like the</p><p>old members or people who are, still had this misconception of just like, oh, I'm just going to go,</p><p>you know, someone who's a new atheist, who's probably just, just, you know.</p><p>Through their angry, like betrayed phase and just trying to figure out themselves wanting to go,</p><p>you know, to a group that are kind of in their same state phase or stage, right? Um, You know,</p><p>and, and this group can, you know, a little bit can satisfy that. It's like, we can understand like</p><p>what happened, you know, like, yeah, you know, you went through this hard time.</p><p>Your family doesn't understand it yet. Yeah, we will be here and we can talk to you about that.</p><p>And, and it's just like, but we're also beyond that, we can also help you establish your new</p><p>values, figure out who you are, uh, figure out if, you know, if you are, if you would rather stay,</p><p>not stay as an atheist, but like we can also tell you like.</p><p>Yeah, there's also humanism. If you've never heard of humanism, maybe think about that.</p><p>Because, like, when I became, when I transitioned or, you know, chose to identify myself as a</p><p>humanist rather than an atheist, it was simply because, you know, atheism had, you know, was</p><p>based on not believing in something.</p><p>And humanism was based on the values of believing in myself, in my, you know, in generosity,</p><p>community, and just, you know, being more virtuous. And doing our best that like we're not</p><p>putting a faith into into into gods of thousands of years ago, but on the humanity of today,</p><p>because if you look at the history of humanity, we have done things that are pretty much God</p><p>defying, you know, like we, you know, we've eradicated diseases, we have, you know, Change</p><p>landscapes for the better and for worse, but yeah, you know, it's just you know with with that</p><p>Yeah, it's just like that's what I feel like That's what I feel like it is, you know, on, on the, I</p><p>think that, like you stated, the angry stages that you go through and maybe when new members</p><p>come in, they're still going through that process.</p><p>And I think there's sometimes where you go through a step where there's a fear of that, and</p><p>maybe that's why we do lose some members. Honestly, I don't have that information, but I can</p><p>see that happening. Um, on some of the meetings I've, I've, I've visited at the Atheist United. I</p><p>noticed that some are very, um, shy about being very open about their atheism.</p><p>I mean, I can understand why, you know, it's just a family members, you know, whatever their</p><p>job or their own community, wherever that may be. And because of that bracket of religion. If</p><p>they start stating that, you know, I'm an atheist, it could harm them, you know, economically or</p><p>psychologically. So I can see why people would leave, but if they, how much they leave their</p><p>membership, I wouldn't know that, that answer to that.</p><p>Yeah, I mean, that is, that is a good point too. A lot of people have, I think the reason, another</p><p>big reason why people don't join Atheist United or another atheistic community is that it is</p><p>dangerous. Mm-Hmm. Or that they do have this fear of danger. Mm-Hmm. I mean, I've had my</p><p>bumper sticker on my car for years now.</p><p>Right. Um, and, and just recently my mother-in-law passed away. So can you imagine how, like,</p><p>even then I didn't bring my car over for a couple times to my father in law's place because it was</p><p>just like, well, I'm not going to just take this off. Because it's, it's, you know, it's just a funny</p><p>joke, but I can understand the nuance of the car of the situation of like, I'm not going to bring this</p><p>car over to my father in law who's just grieving.</p><p>And then he sees that because I don't know how he's going to react to that, but in a general</p><p>sense, In society, people do have this like fear of like, Oh my God, like what if I have this on my</p><p>car? What if somebody keys my car? What if somebody, you know, breaks into my car or</p><p>something like that? And, and, and, and the fact of the matter is, is like, yeah, there is that risk.</p><p>There is that risk because even Evan has had his like magnets. Take it from his vehicle and he</p><p>had to get a new one and those are not easy to come by and the fact of the matter is it's just like</p><p>it's it's people who are who have been raised up to be intolerant of others of differing beliefs or</p><p>not believing in the same religions that they believe in that kind of makes people like, you know,</p><p>that's their like, Humanity off and they just react, you know, instead of just being like, Oh, like</p><p>this person doesn't believe in what I believe in.</p><p>I wonder why it's like, Oh, it's an attack on me. Thus, I have to attack them first. Even if it's just</p><p>the car sitting in a parking lot, even if I, you know, even if it's like, I'm just going to my car and</p><p>I'm just sitting in my vehicle. And I'm going to drive off. People just see it as an attack. I mean,</p><p>I've had that happen a couple times where people, when I'm driving in my vehicle, people, you</p><p>know, give me an extra harsh room behind me because they don't, they just don't want to see it.</p><p>Or I've seen people's reactions where they point and they're laughing or they're shaking their</p><p>head about it and they just, you know, and I've had even a couple compliments about it. There's</p><p>like, Oh man, that's a funny bumper sticker. And it's just like, the thing is that people, you know,</p><p>as we expose atheism and what it means to be an atheist, as more and more people become</p><p>atheists and are, are not afraid of becoming atheists, we are dispelling that, that myth.</p><p>And it's kind of like a, like a, like a herd immunity effect. The more people who know about it,</p><p>the less it's, you know, the less dangerous, um, It's perceived. And the more normalized it is, and</p><p>that's the whole goal, right? Uh, it's just to be, it's just to know that, like, in a society, you can</p><p>have, like, people who believe in several different gods, one Abrahamic god, and someone who</p><p>doesn't believe in any gods, and we can all get along, and we can all talk about it, and we can just</p><p>not start a religious war about it.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>So, my question, um, is, In one of the studies that I read was that the reason, or not the sole</p><p>reason, that, you know, atheist groups put out billboards and whatnot was to attract or to let</p><p>Others know that may be questioning their theistic beliefs and whatnot, that there is a community</p><p>out there of non believers that they could join this group and that within, you had touched on</p><p>this in that same study, it was talking about the fear of closeted atheists Um, afraid to step out</p><p>due to feeling ostracized, the stigma, it could be overwhelming, um, the loneliness.</p><p>Do you guys, um, do you guys want to talk about that at all? Or your thoughts on that?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>Um, you want to go first, [R1]?</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>No, [R2], you can go first.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>Um, so the question was the billboards and Atheist being open and hey we're here. Is that the</p><p>question? I guess the question is the um, I can understand the, the fear of being closeted and</p><p>like let's say one's family is all evangelical Christian and if they come out they're afraid they're</p><p>gonna lose their family and Um, so Or community, or you know, some people may be shunned.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Do you guys know anybody that that may have happened to?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>Or, um, I don't know anybody personally that that's happened to. Um, I, I watch a lot of</p><p>podcasts, a lot of things on YouTube. As far as, uh, atheist communities. And I do hear the horror</p><p>stories as far as like, even with, with young, with young people, uh, where they come to less than</p><p>18 years old and they're still living with their mother and or their parents, excuse me, and they're,</p><p>they're hardcore religious there, whatever that religion may be.</p><p>And then themselves find themselves questioning things about their own religion or about life in</p><p>general. Yeah. And there's been stories where I've heard where, um, they've been shunned.</p><p>They've been kicked out of their homes. They've been, they just like the parents do not want to</p><p>be dealing with this person that no longer live, no longer believes in the religion, you know?</p><p>And that's horrible. And there is a community out there that does help an atheist community that</p><p>does help people like that in order for them to get homes or find, or somewhat financially help</p><p>them. To get themselves out of that situation because that's a horrible thing for you and your</p><p>loving family who you thought loved you so much.</p><p>But there was, there's, there's a carrot. The only way you can stay here is to believe, you know,</p><p>and that's, excuse me, that's BS, you know, that, that, and that helps us as an atheist community.</p><p>Yeah. We posted out there to say, Hey, we're here there. If you need, if we have trouble or there's</p><p>something that's happening to you, we're here to help, you know, and there's no carrot with it.</p><p>We're here to help, you know, and there's constantly a fear, like, especially with the Muslim</p><p>religion, I. When I first started to find, look for a group, I found a Muslim religion where I just</p><p>wanted to see how it was for them because I know they're non believers and it's, it's a Muslim</p><p>religion. I mean, it's people get killed for, I mean, they get killed for other religions, but that's a</p><p>lot more hardcore than the ones I've known.</p><p>Um, and when I sent a message to them, I never got answered. And I was telling the leader from</p><p>my group, the one in San Gabriel here, Um, I was like, why do you think they never answer? She</p><p>goes, you gotta watch out, [R2], you gotta understand that these people are constantly having</p><p>to protect themselves.</p><p>And if they receive a message, it could be someone just trying to, you know, do something</p><p>they're supposed to do by following their own religion and harming these people. So yeah, I see</p><p>why, as a community, we want to be out there, we want to show for myself. With my stickers, I</p><p>want them to see like, look, this is a person that is out and open about it.</p><p>And it's okay to not believe. And if you need help to start asking questions, you can approach</p><p>that person. You can approach it. You can reach out. You don't necessarily have to attend. You</p><p>can do a zoom meeting and not post your video or just listen in. You know, there's some</p><p>wonderful things that you can do in order to get more information and maybe help yourself out.</p><p>I'll make you understand things about your world view in regards to religion.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Thank you.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>Yeah, I think he hit it, uh, hit the nail on the head with that one. Um, yeah, I mean, it's just when</p><p>we put up these billboards and when we are wearing our t shirts, even like I have a couple of</p><p>atheists, United t shirts that are just brazen, just offensive, but it's just like, yeah, I'm an atheist.</p><p>It's like, Um, I think the thing is, is that, yeah, we tend to make it seem like it's like a secret</p><p>society, or something like that, when it's really not. I mean, it's just, it's just the lack of You</p><p>know, resources to make it. It's hard to make an atheistic community. It's hard to make any kind</p><p>of community, um, and especially when it when it pertains to something as personal as, you</p><p>know, atheism and non, you know, non beliefs.</p><p>Um, I think that like, even then, it just makes people feel a little bit more comfortable. Just to</p><p>know that like, oh, there's somebody who does not believe the same thing as my parents does,</p><p>my parents do, or, you know, somebody who believes the same thing as me and, uh, and it's just,</p><p>you know, just being out there, I feel like gives people enough comfort to maybe start later on</p><p>being like, you know, I've seen a lot more.</p><p>You know, people who are just nonbelievers and I've seen this poster here. I've seen this</p><p>billboard here. Maybe it's not as secret as I thought. And then they start looking into it and then</p><p>they start looking in the communities and, um, and I think one more thing that like kind of</p><p>hinders people doing that is that I think people just don't think that they're smart enough to be an</p><p>atheist.</p><p>A lot of people tend to think that when you're an atheist, you have to be a debater. When you're</p><p>an atheist, you have to be a religious scholar who has read all the Bible, read the Quran, read the</p><p>Torah, and still hasn't, and still hasn't become, like, An atheist fully or, or like they, they have to</p><p>like get through this impossible standard of pretty much being like having a doctorate in</p><p>religious studies.</p><p>And then even on top of that, people still doubt that you, you know, that you're an atheist. Right.</p><p>But like the fact of the matter is, is that like, just like religion, atheists come in different levels of</p><p>education and, you know, everyone, is valid, you know, I wouldn't think of a Christian West</p><p>because they believe in, in the supernatural, you know, because there are smart Christians and</p><p>then there are Christians who, who didn't go to college or who maybe never even completed a</p><p>high school diploma, but like their religion and their beliefs.</p><p>It's still valid to them because it's valid to them. And so, I think it's just like, you know, you</p><p>don't have to read a bunch of books to be an atheist. Just the same as some Christians don't read</p><p>their even one book and become Christian. And so, it's like, why do we have this double standard</p><p>of just like, of this, this, this Uh, intellectualism when it's not needed.</p><p>And I think part of that is, I don't think it's like something that was made from. Christians or any</p><p>other religion. I think it just happened to be, just happened to come a thing by accident because</p><p>we all, because we only know like the prominent atheists as like Richard Dawkins or, uh, Sam</p><p>Harris or Carl Sagan, you know, very, very, very intellectual people, and that's all we know about</p><p>atheists, right?</p><p>That's all who we know, you know, whenever somebody thinks about atheism. Bye. I think that</p><p>as we, you know, again, as we expose more people to different types of people who are atheists,</p><p>they'll realize like, oh, you don't have to be smart as, you know, as Carl Sagan to become an</p><p>atheist. So I can become an atheist.</p><p>And it's like, yes, yes, you can. Yeah. I didn't get any smarter after I became an atheist. I started,</p><p>I think I started to become more open minded, but not any smarter. Right. Right. I mean, don't</p><p>get me wrong. Like I have a lot more tools to discern back from fiction, but I wouldn't say that</p><p>I'm going for my doctorate or for my PhD, you know, I'm just a simple, you know, network</p><p>engineer and that's.</p><p>You know, that's it for me. And I'm okay with that. And I don't need to have like a religious</p><p>studies, but like, you know, a lot of time you see it on Tik TOK and, and Facebook and Twitter,</p><p>like debate me, debate me, debate me. It's like, no, I don't want to, cause I don't need to, you</p><p>know, it's not a Pokemon battle.Yeah.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Okay. So this is my last question. Um, what else would either of you like? Me to know need to</p><p>know anything. Uh, would what? What else would you like me to know? Just open ended</p><p>question.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>As for myself, uh, me being an atheist and being very open about it, I mean, I actually have a</p><p>tattoo with a little icon showing it, you know, it's just, I am proud to be an atheist.</p><p>Uh, I love when people approach me to ask me about it because I want them to know that, hey,</p><p>we're here, you know, and we're not the ones with the horns. I love it. You know, one of the, one</p><p>of the comments that I've been given to me is like, [R2], you're more religious than the</p><p>religious people. I don't, why do you say that?</p><p>Because you're more honest in regards of like, the way you approach life. You're not bullshitting</p><p>things. You're not, you're not trying to like, dupe a person. And I go, well, no, I just, I want to be</p><p>as honest as I can, and if I don't know something, I'm not going to make it up. You know, I'll tell</p><p>you, you know what, don't know the answer to that question, let me round back to that.</p><p>You know, let me find out what I can, and I'll answer your question. You know, and me posting</p><p>up my stickers, I love it, it says, your friendly neighborhood atheist, big red letters, and white</p><p>background. And I've had a couple of people like give me the thumbs up or just stare at me like</p><p>[R1] said, I have one gentleman like, hey, I'm an atheist, he had a big old atheist icon on his, on</p><p>his, on his elbow, and those are moments where I love, you know, I got someone out there that</p><p>hey, you know, we're on the same level as far as like, we don't believe, you know, um, yeah,</p><p>there's other little things that we may not agree with.</p><p>We're here, you know, we're here and we're not, we're not the ones with the horns, like I said.</p><p>Yeah.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>Yeah, pretty much what [R2] said. It's just like we are, uh, you know, just ask, you know,</p><p>don't be afraid to ask, like, what do I believe in? You know, it seems that whenever somebody</p><p>hears that you're an atheist, they seem to already know.</p><p>When they don't know, because like, then you're just like, well, what do you, what do you think?</p><p>I believe it. And it's like, you believe in nothing. It's like, no, I don't believe in your one question.</p><p>I said, no, it's your one question, but what do I believe in? And you know, that is where</p><p>conversations can really happen.</p><p>But, um, you know, it's very interesting because like, I haven't won. I'm not yet, but I haven't had</p><p>somebody come up to me. And just ask me, like, what does it mean to be a humanist or an</p><p>atheist, you know? And, you know what? Only one. You know, actually, I did have that happen</p><p>once. It was with my father in law, and he was asking me, where do I get my morality?</p><p>And I know it's not the same as my beliefs, but he was asking where do I get my morality if I</p><p>don't get it from God? And I just simply said I got it from everyone else. You know, because it's</p><p>just, it's, it's just as simple as that, you know, you, you can, you can be a good and moral person</p><p>without needing a religion to, uh, to guide you on that path.</p><p>You can only just be inspired because I was inspired by everyone around me for all of my life.</p><p>And it's just, you know, if my father in law kind of just understood that you can get your, that</p><p>your morals and your values aren't these like divine texts, but they're from everyday small</p><p>actions. I think it would really make every, it would really humanize.</p><p>It would really humanize a humanist as well, because where are most of these people getting</p><p>their misinformation and their stereotypes of atheists from? Not from atheists, of course not, but</p><p>it's always coming from a different side who wants to make atheists, like, be something to be, to</p><p>be feared and villainized when they shouldn't be, you know?</p><p>So, I just, you know, if, you know, if this goes out to, to other people and other people read that</p><p>and other people understand, and want to understand us, It would just be, just talk to an, to a</p><p>local atheist, just be like, what are your values, um, as a human being? You know, you know,</p><p>because like, you're not going to get anything from a religious standpoint.</p><p>You're just going to get them from an, you know, a human experience standpoint. One of the</p><p>funniest questions I got asked by a friend of mine, um, he goes, do atheists mourn? And I was</p><p>like, I took a step back. I'm like, well, brother, of course, of course we mourn. We just, we just</p><p>don't affiliate. Like we don't, when, when we lose someone or we feel that those feelings.</p><p>We don't talk to our knees or clasp our hands, you know, that doesn't happen for me anymore. I</p><p>just self reflect on, just for example, having someone pass on in my life. I'll reflect on that person</p><p>and yes, I'll be sad, but for myself, I try to concentrate on how, how well that person touched me</p><p>in my life.</p><p>And that's what helps me more. And I just, he's like, Fredo, you're laughing. I'm like, well, yeah,</p><p>because I, I find it funny. It's not just a religious thing, you know, like, they go over, they reset</p><p>and start, you know, crossing themselves or go to the local church. It's like, we may reach out to</p><p>loved ones, and that's how we mourn.</p><p>That's how we just like, get through that. We're human beings, of course we mourn. And it's just</p><p>one of the, I mean, I ever got. Yeah, I mean, the same thing that happened with my mother in</p><p>law. She died, uh, two months ago. Uh, we were there on her, when she took her final breaths.</p><p>And, um, everyone was mourning. I will, you know, there was not a dry eye in the room.</p><p>And just because I was, just because I didn't believe where they believed that she was going.</p><p>Doesn't mean that I wasn't feeling that deep wound, that deep emotional wound. And. You know,</p><p>processing it my way was very different from processing is processing it their way like we have,</p><p>like we still have the service we have to go to we still have the celebration of life we have to go</p><p>to.</p><p>And, you know, for me, I had to process it in my own humanist way of like, I lost. A human</p><p>being that I deeply loved and I deeply respected, and that pain and loss is just a reflection of that</p><p>love and care I had for them, um, and I still have for them, and it's just, just because I don't</p><p>believe that they're in, like, this, like, heavenly amusement park or paradise doesn't mean that I,</p><p>like, wish, you Because I think a lot of people tend to think like, if you don't wish that they're</p><p>like up in the clouds being happy playing with their, you know, the, the, with their past dogs or</p><p>something like that, that you wish that they were like in this hellscape or worse, like they're in</p><p>this like a null void area.</p><p>And I'm just like, no, I don't wish that. I just see it as like, we don't know where we, you know,</p><p>we don't know where we go. Most likely, you know, most likely it's like, once we're gone, we're</p><p>gone. We'd have no awareness of anything else because like, that's where our, our awareness, our</p><p>feelings and our touch and our experiences and our memories come from our brain, if our brain</p><p>does not get any more oxygen and it's starved and it dies, we die with it.</p><p>But there's no pain and there is no, there's no misery. There's no anguish, but also there is no</p><p>happiness. There is no pleasure. There is no, uh, you know, excitement. And I'm, you know, and</p><p>it's like, you got to take them both at hand. You know, some, I think it's kind of like a half, you</p><p>know, a glass half full, half empty kind of thing.</p><p>I, you know, I'm just more of just like, I'm just glad she isn't suffering anymore, you know, and</p><p>that's what I'm looking at at. I'm glad she's not suffering anymore, but I'm not also like hoping</p><p>that like she's, you know, in the heavenly clouds playing chess with Jesus. You know, I just, I</p><p>just don't believe that it just goes against, it doesn't go against my values, but it just, as I navigate</p><p>through this world and what has helped me was going through this scientific, more evidence</p><p>based kind of thinking and the science and the evidence, you know, haven't shown that there is</p><p>some kind of afterlife after this.</p><p>And so thus it does. help with, you know, with my own mortality, because, you know, seeing</p><p>something like that gets you thinking. And it's like, yeah, I mean, it has really solidified a lot of</p><p>things for me as an atheist. And as a humanist myself, it's just like, You know, you, you tend to</p><p>think that you're prepared for it, but when you see it happen, it kind of, it tests the waters, it tests</p><p>your foundations.</p><p>I mean, it didn't make me start thinking like, Oh man, maybe I should go to church. Um, it's just</p><p>like, you kind of have to like reevaluate yourself and be like, you know, like, yeah, as an atheist,</p><p>I am prepared to leave, pass on, die. And I'm prepared for nothing after that. There's nothing,</p><p>you know, I am, I'm ready for nothing after that.</p><p>And I'm going to be okay with that.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>I'm sorry, [R1]. Um, I just, I had a thought right now. Um, I think with me, as far as like with</p><p>death and other things that are affiliated with religion, you know, it's just like, I want to maybe</p><p>drop a little bit in this world and say, Hey, look, [R2] was this kind of person.</p><p>He wasn't religious, but he did things that people always thought was affiliated with, with</p><p>religion or church or something like that. You know, I've done things where normally people</p><p>think, oh, you have to be such and such in order to do that. No, you don't. You don't. You know,</p><p>those are things that anybody can still do without affiliating yourself with religion, you know?</p><p>And that's something that I, I hope people see in the world that are religious in my world. And</p><p>say, well, he used to do it. Why do I have to do it that way? I can do it another way I want, you</p><p>know, and that's what I hope I take people take away from. And when you mean by that, do you</p><p>mean like being a good person, doing good deeds, like these things that are Affiliated with a</p><p>religious institution doing being a good person and not thinking like, Hey, I'm doing this just</p><p>because I'm going to go up.</p><p>I'm doing this because I want to avoid this over here. You know, like, I want to do things</p><p>because, hey, [R2] was this and he came all the way from here. Just. This is what he wanted</p><p>to do because he wanted to hop out. You know, he just wanted to do good. You just do good. Just</p><p>like, yeah. I mean, like doing good is feels good.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>You know, that, that, that is the deeper thing than anything else. Like I don't. I don't think of it as</p><p>like, you know, when I, when I go to a food distribution, I don't think of it as like, Oh, I'm</p><p>punching a hole in my heaven ticket. And, uh, you know, I'm not thinking of it like that. I'm just</p><p>like, because I don't like seeing people suffer in, in, in, in that kind of way.</p><p>I don't like seeing. a family wondering where their next meal is going to come from. And we</p><p>gave like 30 to 30 to like 40 pounds of like food to like a family and like how grateful they are.</p><p>Just it is something that. You just have to see and experience for yourself and understand that</p><p>like, Oh, that didn't come like I, you know, that didn't come from like God rewarding me.</p><p>That's from my own brain rewarding me because deep down inside we as social creatures that we</p><p>are, we depend on each other. And, you know, it doesn't need, you know, we don't need a religion</p><p>to, to connect us. It's just, you know, you know, we're human beings. Thanks. We, you know, and</p><p>you know, the only reason why we kind of separate each other and from helping one another is</p><p>because of like these minuscule at the grand scheme of things differences, whether it's like the</p><p>gods that we believe in or the color of somebody's skin or whatever their political party is at.</p><p>It's like, I don't, you know, I don't care if you have a different belief than me. I don't care if you</p><p>just don't like me because of my color of my skin. Do you need help is my first question. It</p><p>reminds me of that, um, of that photo of, I don't know if you guys have seen it, but like of that</p><p>old photo of that Klansman.</p><p>On the operating table and there was like a, a team of, of black nurses and doctors that were just</p><p>helping him out and it's like that, like that right there that shows it's like your hate or your diff or</p><p>your, your, your misinterpretation of me doesn't matter. If you need help, I'm going to help you.</p><p>And then we can discuss about like, what do you think that you're the master race or not, or</p><p>whatever.</p><p>But like, at least you're going to be here. You're right. At least this could change your mind a</p><p>little bit. And it's like those acts of good tend to change a lot more people than acts of superiority</p><p>or even worse villainy, because you cannot just. a race, a group of atheists and been like problem</p><p>solved, the entire world is religious.</p><p>There's always going to be people who are going to be questioning, uh, their religious dogma.</p><p>There's always going to be people who are going to be questioning whatever, uh, whatever that</p><p>they were taught by their, um, um, by their parents or tradition. But it's that kind of questioning</p><p>and that kind of, you know, A little bit of defiance that progresses that makes us progress go</p><p>forward in our lives and like and to get to where we were like 300 years ago to now that took a</p><p>lot of defiance that took a lot of just like stepping into the unknown and being curious and just</p><p>questioning things and that's I mean, it keeps, it keeps working, you know.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Awesome. Well, it looks like it's 1106. I know, that was fast, huh?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>It was fun, to tell you the truth, it was fun.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>I really, truly appreciate both of you contributing to this. It means a lot to me, it means a lot to,</p><p>you know, I think a part of this work that I want to take on, at least to present to my, because</p><p>this is going to end up being like a paper that I'm writing.</p><p>And hopefully like, if I want to go to graduate school, I can use this as a writing sample for</p><p>graduate school. And then eventually maybe get a PhD, but what I'm really taking, what I've</p><p>really taken away and what I feel like as a duty for myself is that when I do this presentation,</p><p>I'm going to be so happy to advocate.</p><p>For your community and to talk, cause I'm going to be talking to, you know, faculty and students,</p><p>and to be able to advocate for you guys in the sense that these are just ordinary people, atheists</p><p>are humans too, you know, and so I'm just, beg your pardon.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>I mean, that's exactly it. That's the, that's the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:////Users/abaugh/Library/Group%20Containers/UBF8T346G9.Office/TemporaryItems/msohtmlclip/clip_filelist.xml">goal.It</a>'s just. Yeah, we're just human beings that</p><p>just happened to not just happen to say no on the God question.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>And so I'm just really happy to be a part of destigmatizing atheism and atheists. And so I'm just</p><p>so ever grateful. And, um, next semester I'm actually taking a class on So I'm excited to maybe</p><p>I'll do another research project.</p><p>I'll reach out to Evan again. Um, maybe to either further this work that I'm doing or maybe start a</p><p>new one, but I'm just so grateful for both of you contributing and being open and honest and</p><p>vulnerable. And so I. I'm truly, you know, really grateful. So thank you so much. Of course, of</p><p>course. Now, I got a question.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>Um, is it possible, like, once you finish with that paper, that we could, like, read it? Because I'm</p><p>so curious about, like, how it's gonna, you know, how, like, we are influencing your paper and</p><p>everything.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Yes, definitely. I will. Oh my gosh. I have an audience.</p><p>It was so funny in my, in like writing this paper, it's like, my teacher's having us read these books</p><p>and like, Oh, you have to think from it. Like, what is the audience going to think? And I'm like, I</p><p>don't have an audience now. I have an audience. So now. Great. So thank you so much. Yeah, I'd</p><p>be happy to, um, I have your email, [R1], and, um, I know, um, [R2], you're connected with</p><p>Arlene, and I have Arlene's email, or if you want My email, I can put it on the chat if you like.</p><p>Yeah, great. So let's</p><p>open the chat.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>I'm going to send it to you. I'm not sure if you can see that. Yes. Also, um, I'm not sure if he's</p><p>still doing research. Um, an article was written about me and my wife and Arlene's group, uh, on</p><p>the Los Angeles Times some time back. So if you look up on my name, [R2] Beltran, Los</p><p>Angeles Times atheist, I think it says, you'll be able to see it.</p><p>There's a picture of myself and my wife. The article here, I will put in my email</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>if you'd like to send that over to me.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>Yeah, that was a fun thing. I even got a, I got, I received a letter from some anonymous religious</p><p>person in regards to that article. Oh, really? Oh, really? Wow. Um, Evan had said that when I</p><p>had reached out first, he wanted to, um, we had a, we had a discussion, you know, wanted to</p><p>make sure that like he said that unfortunately, um, some people are like really kooky.</p><p>You know, like you were saying, like, oh, they have ulterior, like, alternate motives or whatever.</p><p>And so, I was just taken aback by that, too, like, holy cow, like, there's, um, Oh, wait, maybe I</p><p>said the wrong thing, holy cow. Well, that's, that's not a God, right? It's like, oh, no, did I say</p><p>something inappropriate? I'm not sure if it's appropriate.</p><p>But it's so funny when people say, uh, God bless you. I'm sorry. I'm afraid I'm like, I don't care.</p><p>It's just the funny thing to a lot of people tend to think because they have this, like, Oh my God,</p><p>we have to respect them as a religion. They tend to think that, Oh, we can't say, Oh my God. Or,</p><p>you know, I say, God damn it all the time at work.</p><p>It's really interesting because it's just like, Well, ask the atheist. Does he feel offended by that?</p><p>It's like, no. I understand the sentiment, you know, like, but just please don't be offended if I say</p><p>thing if I don't say thank you. Cause you know, I just. Whatever, it's just like, I'm sorry, excuse</p><p>me, I'm pretty much all say, but thank you for trying to be polite to us.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>I mean, at the food distribution, so many people say bless you or God bless you and everything. I</p><p>just say, I just see it as a thank you, you know, it's like their way of saying thank you. And I'm</p><p>like, all right, cool. I don't care.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Awesome. Well, so now I have your email, [R2]. You have my email, [R1]. I have your email</p><p>in the thread.</p><p>And so, yeah, hopefully, um, once I get this done, I'll share it and I'll be open to feedback. Um,</p><p>and, you know, I'm Um, thanks I look forward to keeping the dialogue going. Um, Of course,</p><p>yeah.</p><p>[R1] / [R2] :</p><p>If you have any other questions and later on, or, you know, you start a new project and you feel</p><p>like, you know, you, you can get some good, like, advice or input from me, or I'm sure even</p><p>[R2], just, you know, don't hesitate to contact us.</p><p>[Int] / [R2] / [R1]:</p><p>Yeah. so much. Don't ever hesitate when it comes to stuff like that. Okay, cool. Thank you so</p><p>much I hope you all have a great day and thank you so much You're welcome. Have a good one.</p><p>You too. Bye</p><p>&nbsp;</p>	to6gNGICQ1X1EHBDN6KQVeykKas2	34.26996183866345	-118.52071758447873	17735 1/2 Horace St, Granada Hills, CA 91344, USA	f	f	f	[]	2024-04-22 15:15:36.852	2024-04-22 15:16:55.556	2024-06-21 20:54:16.007	\N
66267fc2a1162cbdb9d349b0	Atheists United Interview	<p><em>This is an interview transcript created by a CSUN undergraduate as part of her research project for the RS capstone class. The student is identified as Int (interviewer) and the respondents are identified as R1 and R2.</em></p><p></p><p><span>[Int]: Um,</span></p><p><span>so yeah, it's really fascinating and there's, in one of the studies that I read, it was about creating,</span></p><p><span>you know, uh, collective identity, group identity, and, um, also the, because I was also really</span></p><p><span>curious on, um, the effects of loneliness. Yes, that's happening in the community. Um, that will</span></p><p><span>loneliness is an epidemic worldwide. Um, I'm wondering if that's like, is that due to technology?</span></p><p><span>Is that due to secularism? Is that due to like, what are the contributing factors? And so that was</span></p><p><span>the my question as far as community and that how how are you guys getting together in a</span></p><p><span>society that's claims that we're not supposed to put like a religion but it's putting theism like In</span></p><p><span>first place.</span></p><p><span>And so how does that make you feel apart? Like in society and like, how, how do you connect</span></p><p><span>with people? How, so I find it.</span></p><p><span>[R1]:</span></p><p><span>That is actually pretty fascinating. Um, I think the thing is, is that we kind of get permission in</span></p><p><span>this kind of capitalistic world and this hustle, this grinding culture to always constantly find a</span></p><p><span>different way to make money.</span></p><p><span>And the people give certain permissions when it comes to religion, you know, you like, I have a</span></p><p><span>coworker, right? We work, you know, collectively, we all work about 40 to 50 hours, depending</span></p><p><span>on what we're doing. But every Friday, he gets, he gets to leave early. We work night shift and</span></p><p><span>we work until like 930 at night sometimes, and he gets to get out at 530.</span></p><p><span>Am I jealous? Not really. I'm not really hurt by that, but it is fascinating to see that, like, because</span></p><p><span>of his religion, he gets a carve out for his third space versus somebody who's secular, like myself,</span></p><p><span>who would like to, like, have that time. You know, off and get to go do something else that I</span></p><p><span>enjoyed, not just going straight home, but I also have all their hobbies and, and other interests</span></p><p><span>that may not be in a divine sense, but it is still important, just as important to me as it is to him to</span></p><p><span>go do whatever he has to do for his own, uh, theistic community.</span></p><p><span>[Int]:</span></p><p><span>Yeah, I think this is [R2]. Hi, [R2]. Um, my name is Sasha Dean. I'm a CSUN student.</span></p><p><span>Um, I was asking, um, I am gonna, I'm asking if it's okay to record this meeting. Um, the</span></p><p><span>recording is just so that I can make a transcript so that when I write my paper, um, I could get</span></p><p><span>either quotes and whatnot, but your name and your identity will remain anonymous.</span></p><p><span>And I will destroy this recording as soon as I make my transcript. And then once I, once I finish</span></p><p><span>with my paper, I will destroy the transcript as well. Um, so if you're okay with me recording this,</span></p><p><span>it's just for data collection, nothing else.</span></p><p><span>[R2]:</span></p><p><span>Yeah, I'm, I'm fine with it. I, I'm not, I have nothing to hide, so I'm good.</span></p><p><span>Okay. And if you want to see me, you can see me if you like.</span></p><p><span>[Int] :</span></p><p><span>Okay, great. If you want to have your camera on, great. If you would like it off, that's good too.</span></p><p><span>So it's 10. 01. So I guess let's get this started. Um, Okay, so my first question is, please tell me</span></p><p><span>about yourself or please tell me about your religious identity and your background.</span></p><p><span>[R2] :</span></p><p><span>Um, my religious identity was Catholic. Um, both my parents are originally from Mexico,</span></p><p><span>Sinaloa, and, um, L. D. F. And, uh, age 49, and I've been living in California for all my life.</span></p><p><span>[Int] :</span></p><p><span>Okay, and how did you become a member of Atheists United?</span></p><p><span>[R2] :</span></p><p><span>Uh, the way I became an Atheist with, uh, United was through, uh, Atheists, Latinos Atheist of</span></p><p><span>San Gabriel, I believe it is, the one with Arlene. Um, I met Arlene at a conference boy gathering,</span></p><p><span>um, to meet Matt Delahunty from, uh, forget the name, from Texas, forget the name of the, uh, of</span></p><p><span>the, the people that he was with at the time.</span></p><p><span>Um, and we happened to be in line to meet him, and she was right in front of me, and she said,</span></p><p><span>Are you Latino? I'm like, yeah, where do you live? I told her and everything and I go, hey, I'm</span></p><p><span>trying to start a group in San Gabriel and I'm affiliated with Atheist United. I'm like, oh, okay,</span></p><p><span>cool. So it was just the snowball effect from there.</span></p><p><span>I started like hanging out. I'm not hanging out with him as much as often as I did because I got</span></p><p><span>other things going on in my life, but that's how I ended up in, uh, affiliated with him.</span></p><p><span>[Int]:</span></p><p><span>Okay, and how long have you been a member?</span></p><p><span>[R2]:</span></p><p><span>I've been a member, I want to say roughly about five or six years, I think it's been.</span></p><p><span>[Int]:</span></p><p><span>Okay. And [R1], would you mind telling me about your religious identity and background?</span></p><p><span>[R1]:</span></p><p><span>Right. Yeah. So my mom, uh, was, my mom is Haitian and she was, I think she still is. Um, she's</span></p><p><span>part of Jehovah's Witness. Um, She tried to raise me into</span></p><p></p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Okay. And how did you become a member of Atheist United?</p><p>[R1] :</p><p>Atheist United.</p><p>Simply one day during the pandemic, I was kind of just bored. And, um, I was like, this is when</p><p>I became comfortable with being a, uh, an atheist. And I just, I was kind of bored of the. online</p><p>space. You know, I wanted to meet people like me physically.</p><p>Uh, and so I legit just looked up on Google atheist meetups. Atheist United was the first one that</p><p>came up on meetup. com literally. And they had a food distribution that weekend and I had</p><p>volunteer hours from work and so I just took it. And I was like, the worst case scenario is that I</p><p>do some good, don't vibe with the group.</p><p>Uh, best case, I do some good and I vibe with the group. So the best case scenario won out. Oh,</p><p>wow. And how long have you been a member, um, since the pandemic happened? Uh, yes, since</p><p>the pandemic. And so it's been like two, three years, I would like to say I haven't kept track, but</p><p>it's been, it's been multiple years.</p><p>It's been multiple years. I can say.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Okay, awesome. And okay, I'm gonna just bounce back and forth. Is that okay?</p><p>[R1] &amp; [R2] :</p><p>Okay. Yeah. Okay, awesome.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>So, [R2], um, why do you keep coming back to Atheist United?</p><p>[R2] :</p><p>I keep coming back because, um, it just feels, I like the being able to have like minded people in</p><p>regards of the subject of non beliefs, non believers.</p><p>Um, there's times where I'm working, I feel like I'm the only one that feels that way. As are</p><p>constantly people telling me, you know, God may, only God knows, or, uh, only, uh, just, just</p><p>that, that, that reference to just a higher power is just, I find it silly. But when I meet up with this</p><p>group of people, it's just nice not to hear that all the time and have other conversations besides</p><p>just, so that's why I really enjoy hanging out with them.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Okay, and what role do you think this plays in your life?</p><p>[R2] :</p><p>What role? Uh, I guess it's just for my own psychological, uh, healthiness, I guess. Uh, just so</p><p>much, so much religious stuff that I see every day. It's not a depression thing. It's just like, I just</p><p>wish people would look beyond that and maybe question their beliefs, you know, and I've come</p><p>across to where people have the beliefs and I don't think they understand them.</p><p>So with this group, I've, I've been able to, to think about other things and just reflect on other</p><p>subjects, just not just that, you know, and it's helped me just myself. And it's just, it feels good to</p><p>know that I'm not the only one.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>That's awesome. And [R1], how about you? What about yourself? Why do you keep coming</p><p>back?</p><p>No, it's just a reinforcement of my values, you know? Um, When I, when I first came to Atheist</p><p>United as a volunteer at the food distribution and meeting Evan and just seeing how friendly and</p><p>personable and, you know, just positive that guy could be and, and attracting that kind of energy</p><p>with everyone else.</p><p>Everyone was friendly and positive. It really. And it really destroyed this, you know, I guess</p><p>this cultural myth that atheists are nothing more than these nihilistic, you know, no meaning at</p><p>all people who just want to sit around and complain about God. It just was the complete</p><p>opposite of that, you know, like the everyone was very much had a purpose when being there.</p><p>Sure, it wasn't this universal alignment purpose. It wasn't this mega meta battle of good and evil.</p><p>It was just simply humans getting together and doing good for each other. And that really</p><p>inspired me into, you know, thinking about my own value. And even then, um, why I became a</p><p>humanist or why I identified myself as a humanist was because one of the members was a</p><p>humanist herself.</p><p>And so because of that, I was like, Oh, what, what, what is humanism? And so looking into that</p><p>and then thus You know, be, you know, understanding the values of that was like, wow, okay,</p><p>like this is, this is it. This is the, you know, this is what I am, you know, I never knew the word</p><p>for it. And I wouldn't have known the word for it if I didn't explore these secular spaces, because</p><p>you're not going to hear this kind of talk in a church, or traditional church, I would say, I'm sure</p><p>you know, Unitarian Universalist Church would say, Bring up the term, uh, humanism and</p><p>atheism, but, you know, in, in like, you know, in theistic churches, like Christianity or something</p><p>like that, they, they tend to have such a bad, you know, rap about just those two terms that it</p><p>almost feels like.</p><p>The same term as communism, you know, it makes it, they, they, they villainize it to the point</p><p>that you don't even know what it means. And you're kind of scared to figure out what it actually</p><p>means. And when you do, it's kind of like, Oh, that's it. I, you know, like once, once that</p><p>boogeyman is gone, you're just like, great.</p><p>I, you know, this is who I am. This is why, what I like. And, you know, and being with these</p><p>people just keep reinforcing the faith. My values and, you know, making me proud to be an</p><p>atheist and a humanist.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Awesome. And what, uh, follow up question, what role does this play in your life?</p><p>[R1] :</p><p>You know, every day it just makes me, you know, think about my choices. You know, in life,</p><p>when there is a way to help people not to, you know, and just to do it with no expectation of</p><p>some higher reward, you know, most of the, you know, most of the time, if not all the time,</p><p>whenever I'm thinking about the turmoil, the political, um, leanings of, of our country and our</p><p>division, um, it helps because not only am I thinking about, you know, What can I do to help</p><p>other people, you know, as the old saying goes about like planting trees on the shade that you'll</p><p>never feel of.</p><p>It's, it's more of just like, I'm only here for a limited amount of time. I don't know what happens</p><p>after most likely nothing. But what can I do in this limited amount of time to help the most</p><p>amount of people? And that reinforces and, you know, influences my decisions, who I'm friends</p><p>with. In fact, even with today's misinformation.</p><p>You know, our Atheist United tends to have like this critical thinking aspect of it all. We question</p><p>a lot of things. We have Science Sundays that encourages this kind of critical thinking. And I've</p><p>taken those kinds of skills and I've applied them into my own life to Not just the bunk to avoid</p><p>these disinformation pitfalls.</p><p>I think that after we saw what the bridge collapse and, um, with the solar eclipse just recently, we</p><p>need more of that. We need to stop trying to, you know, just believe everything that we see on</p><p>the Internet, just the same as we don't believe everything that we see on TV. It seems like with</p><p>just the Internet, which is more people saying such things.</p><p>We are just, we tend to believe it more often. And, you know, and for me, it's just like, I</p><p>questioned a lot of things, but not to the point of conspiracy, but to the point of like, I dealt what</p><p>you're saying, not because I don't want to believe you, but I need more evidence to believe you.</p><p>Yeah. Just be like skepticism, right?</p><p>Would you be skepticism? Not to the point of I'm wearing a tinfoil hat.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Sorry, I didn't mean to cut you off there. Sorry. No, it's exactly what it is. Healthy skepticism.</p><p>Okay. So let's go on to the next question. I'm so fast. I'm like, Oh my God. I may get a PhD now.</p><p>Um, So question number three, [R2], some scholars suggest that atheism is a sect of religion</p><p>that is filling religious needs. What do you think about that?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>It annoys me and annoys the heck out of me. We'll use another word, but I have friends that, that</p><p>constantly tease me about that, that it's replacing, or it is a church that I'm attending on a Sunday,</p><p>you know, they, they, And instead of asking questions of why I'm attending this and why I feel</p><p>the way I feel, um, I think they're afraid to ask me the questions that they know they doubt</p><p>things, you know?</p><p>And by asking me and knowing who I am, it would, it would reinforce those thoughts, and I</p><p>think they're afraid of those, those questions to be answered in my thought, in my, in my words.</p><p>Uh, it, it, it gets, It gets annoying. It gets annoying after a while, like, yeah, you guys don't seem</p><p>to understand, and instead of just Creating a thought about what we do and what we're all about</p><p>and why not ask me instead of just teasing about it, you know, so yeah,</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>what about you, [R1]? Um, do you need me to repeat the question or no?</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>No, I think that, um, this is another example of that vilification of the word atheism, right?</p><p>Because if we Talk about atheism. Atheism is just an answer to one question. Do you believe in</p><p>any gods or gods? And there's two camps, you know, there's yes. And then there's no.</p><p>And personally for me, um, when I've heard this, I've heard this too, you know, I think it's just to</p><p>try to bring. Atheism to the level of religion, either up or down it, but it has to be the same level</p><p>because you don't have the same kind of vilification when it comes to any other opinions, right?</p><p>Um, but people tend to make, you know, something that they're trying to vilify the same thing as</p><p>them, maybe because it's easier to attack it or something like that.</p><p>But for me, it's, you know, It's hard to, like, explain to them, atheism is not a religion. It's, you</p><p>know, when you're in that group, right, we don't agree on everything, you know? It's not, we're</p><p>not lock and step with every single thing. I mean, We have in Atheist United, you know,</p><p>committee or board meetings, um, I mean, we have, you know, arguments and all the time about</p><p>just like minor things, you know, not every atheist is a vegan, even though it's more ethically</p><p>sound to be more vegan or vegetarian, and some atheists are Our, you know, hardcore mediators,</p><p>you know, and it's just like, well, there you go.</p><p>Like there's an example of just like, not all of us just locking and stepping with everything that</p><p>we talk about. And it's just when people say, Oh, atheism is just another religion. It just kind of</p><p>shows the ignorance of it all because you know, what is a religion, you know, how do you</p><p>identify a religion with atheism?</p><p>Like, where do you find that just because we just disagree on that one. Aspect, but we still have</p><p>the community because we're humans. We're social creatures. We need community in order to</p><p>thrive and survive, but you can't just say that because You know, we just disagree on the God</p><p>aspect and yet we just do this similar things as religions like, you know, give food to the poor or</p><p>donate blood or donate money to colleges or to need it to students in need.</p><p>That isn't an exclusive religious thing. Right. That is something that religion has taken up and</p><p>decided that as a society, we just decided that like, Oh, only religious people can work in soup</p><p>kitchens or religious organizations can organize food drives. No, anyone can do that. Religions</p><p>just happen to have the resources to do that.</p><p>If you, if we spread these resources and these financial powers out to people who want to do that,</p><p>but just aren't affiliated or don't want to be affiliated with religions, there'd be so many more. But</p><p>if, you know, religions tend to have a monopoly and when we have Atheists United who are</p><p>trying to do good, people just tend to lump us in all together as, as a religion, which is.</p><p>kind of just like, you know, minimizing our work a bit. It's, it's the whole idea of we, what is,</p><p>how does it go? We fear that what we don't understand, you know, they're just like, they don't</p><p>want to understand it. So we're going to go ahead and fear it and make fun of it. Make fun of it.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Yeah. So these are, so my question is pertaining to like religious studies scholars, not necessarily</p><p>Um, not necessarily like people in the community that don't understand atheism or like people</p><p>like religious studies scholars look at atheists and I'm wondering if it's just the word religion is</p><p>triggering.</p><p>So if, if we took the word. religion out, we're just looking at it as a sense of like a collective</p><p>group identity. And so if we look at the word religion is very triggering for like a lot of people.</p><p>And so because of Western colonial powers and you know, corrupt Christianity and whatnot.</p><p>Um, it has a negative stigma attached to it, especially with all of the trauma and the violence and</p><p>everything that has happened.</p><p>Um, so are you, are you mostly mainly asking, like, Like, so people like, you know, like in</p><p>university people are discussing or in academia are looking at atheism as like, it's fulfilling, is it</p><p>fulfilling a social function of religion in the sense that it's creating a community, not that not</p><p>living in like a duality, dualistic, good versus evil.</p><p>Nothing like that. Just purely. As you were saying, like, it's, um, a create like a sense of</p><p>community and a gathering point and a ideology, um, not necessarily in a way to, unfortunately,</p><p>within the religious studies scholar, uh, religious studies community, you know, we have major</p><p>religions and minor religions.</p><p>And so the major religions would consist of Christianity, Islam, Judaism, Hinduism. And so a lot</p><p>of scholars previously only wrote about that. Wrote about these as like, these are like the big</p><p>ones and then everything else like indigenous religion, African diasporic religions, or like minor</p><p>religions, and it's just kind of looking at atheists like, like we're stuck with the term or word</p><p>religion because so many people know this word.</p><p>And in a sense, it's, there's a phenomenon happening. And so if we're looking at like religion as</p><p>a phenomenon of like groups. And so I was just curious in the sense of like, outside, um, like,</p><p>let's say like, not family members looking at atheism and projecting their ideas and their values. I</p><p>was just curious as far as like academics, Looking at atheism or atheists as As a religion slash</p><p>the social function of religion, meaning getting together community and whatnot.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>Oh, okay. So, as like a social function. Um, I guess it is, you know, it really depends. I mean,</p><p>when it comes to atheists. There, there is this term of like, uh, like hurting cats because it is kind</p><p>of hard to get a group of atheists together as, uh, you know, just to do something. A lot of people</p><p>are, are, um, affected by the, you know, the church hurting them or their religion hurting them in</p><p>general.</p><p>And being part of another group or who have the same. Uh, markers, I guess, like just coming</p><p>together, talking about similar topics, uh, agreeing on the broad strokes and, um, you know, a lot</p><p>of people tend to be wary about that and, and, and, you know, in, in, in the, in the social aspect of</p><p>it, in the, in the social way that we use religion Is to is for people to get together and with</p><p>atheism, it's, it's kind of hard because like, there's not that many groups.</p><p>I mean, atheists United is the biggest atheist group, right. And that's not that many people. And,</p><p>you know, it's kind of hard to answer that kind of question, but I do think with the people that are</p><p>in that group, uh, with the regulars and the members who send money and also who go to the</p><p>events. Um, I guess in that kind of way it is, but like, I would say that it's, it's, it's a similar as</p><p>saying a car and a truck are vehicles, but they have different purposes, sure.</p><p>They can both get you from A to B and service, you know. What you need. But sometimes, you</p><p>know, we need to understand and distinguish the difference. You cannot just point out a truck and</p><p>say it's a car and you cannot point out a car and say it's a truck because you're expecting different</p><p>purposes out of it.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>I have to agree with [R1]. You put that so well. I misunderstood the question, but yeah, that</p><p>completely makes sense the way you put it. Yeah, it does. It does help. It is a, not a</p><p>replacement, but I completely understood what you meant by herding cats and the vehicle, you</p><p>know, comparison. Thanks, [R1]. Yeah, that's Awesome.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Okay. So let's look at my, um, question number four. Um, I'll go with [R2] first. Do people in</p><p>your life know that you're an atheist? Your friends, family, and coworkers?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>Yes, they all know. And if they don't know, they'll find out. I have stickers on my car. I have a</p><p>big flag in front of my house. Um, I, I, I want them to know that I am, and I want them to</p><p>understand that I want, I like to treat people with kindness and respect.</p><p>And of course I have my thresholds like every human being, but when they see who I am, and</p><p>the way I approach things, And I hope they take that away from me, from me and say, look,</p><p>[R2] is this way and he's not affiliated or believes in all the stuff that I believe. Maybe there's</p><p>something to look into about that.</p><p>And what kind of response do they share with you about your atheism? Uh, response. You mean</p><p>like in the beginning when they first find out or after they already know after years? Um, let's</p><p>talk about both. How are they in the beginning? And then how are they now? In the beginning,</p><p>they, of course, one of them is fear.</p><p>The questions are like, Oh, you believe the first thing that comes out of the mouth. So you</p><p>believe in the devil? No, I don't. You know, I don't believe in that. Um, it's just like, I have no, I</p><p>no longer hold that belief of a higher power. And my thoughts as far as approaching life is</p><p>questioning things. And I need more information is something I don't understand.</p><p>That's how I move forward. And that's what I tell them. Um, after they know that I'm an atheist,</p><p>um, it's so funny. They, they come to me and ask me questions. What I think about things that</p><p>have come out in the news in regards of religion, like based on religion or asked other people I've</p><p>done because of religion.</p><p>And I love those conversations, you know, I'll give them the thoughts on that. And then even</p><p>then when they, when they, they recite a Bible verse or something and they'll ask me what I think</p><p>about it. I, I do enjoy those conversations and I tell everybody, anybody and everybody when</p><p>they first meet me, go ahead and tell them I don't care about that.</p><p>I've had people where they know I'm an atheist, but they don't want to ask questions or they're</p><p>afraid to ask me or they're afraid to approach me. As time goes on, of course, they'll start getting</p><p>curious. And they can see who I am, you know, and I'm hoping it's a good thing that, you know,</p><p>the way I, I, I held myself in front of them to show, hey, look.</p><p>I hold this belief, or non belief, and I see the world differently from you.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>Yeah, and um, [R1], do your friends, family, and co workers know that you're an atheist? Yeah,</p><p>I mean, it's no worries, uh, atheists, humanists,</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>it's kind of inter, uh, interchangeable for me. Uh, but yes, they do. Um, at first it was definitely</p><p>an uncomfortable conversation with, um, especially with my father in law.</p><p>Um, You know, because like I took pictures of me being at my first food distribution and literally</p><p>that same weekend, he wanted to go talk and I hold him into a high regard. I respect him, uh, at</p><p>a high regard and everything. And it was just like, he was kind of confused because for a period</p><p>of time before that, I was, um, looking into Christianity.</p><p>Actually, I was. You know, after getting, you know, getting settled down in California, I went on</p><p>this spiritual journey, myself, and I was like, well, let me try, you know, seeing about</p><p>Christianity. Curious about it. I was asking him questions, I was going to his, to uh, services,</p><p>and, you know, when I just realized it just wasn't for me, I, and went to Atheist United and</p><p>started to become more open about being an atheist and a non believer, um, it was definitely</p><p>some growing pains.</p><p>Because, you know, I feel like when you open that door a little bit to religion and then you shut</p><p>it again, simply because it's just like, man, you know, I just can't, I just can't believe it. Um,</p><p>people get really offended or really, really hurt or betrayed by that. My wife herself, she, she</p><p>was just like, she, she gave me the freedom and it was just like, you know, just do whatever you</p><p>feel like it's right.</p><p>And, um, my friends, they're, they're all nonbelievers generally, and they just don't care, but, um,</p><p>yeah, you know, like nobody really talks about it, you know, at, at my, um, you know, when we</p><p>get to family get togethers and stuff like that. Um, everyone knows I have a bumper sticker in my</p><p>car that says drive carefully, there is no heaven.</p><p>And, um, you know, I just, I just leave it out. I just leave it out there. And yeah. If people are</p><p>curious, they can talk and ask. Uh, I don't, you know, I, I see it as more of just like, as long as</p><p>I'm kind, as long as I'm humanistic, as long as I'm generous, as long as I'm, you know, you</p><p>know, always trying my best to be the best version of myself, it really doesn't matter if their fear</p><p>blocks that because eventually they're just going to see.</p><p>Because now they know. Now they have a non believer in their family. They have an atheist in</p><p>their family of Christians. And now that, you know, now that I'm in their head, whenever</p><p>somebody's gonna be talking about an atheist in the future in a bad light, they're gonna be</p><p>thinking about me, and they're like, He doesn't act like that, though.</p><p>He's not upset. He's not depressed. He's not living a life of meaningless and going around</p><p>sinning. He's normal. He, you know, he's not a criminal. He's, he's perfectly fine. He's doing well</p><p>for his life. He's well respected. So I feel like just being in that presence and just acting as, as I</p><p>am is enough to dispel, again, that myth of villainy of, and atheism at the same time.</p><p>[Int] :</p><p>And, oh no, I guess I'll save this for, um, after. Um, my next question is. Um, how long have,</p><p>like, my question is in regards to, in regard, with regard to how long have members stayed in</p><p>Atheist United? And, like, do people ever leave Atheist United? And if so, like, do you know</p><p>why?</p><p>[R2] :</p><p>I wouldn't know the answer to that.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>I wouldn't know. Um, yeah, maybe click clicking. I have very limited knowledge on that, but</p><p>from what I can say, what I have observed, uh, and what I've heard from, um, other members,</p><p>um, as time has gone on, and as Atheist United, because Atheist United has been around For a</p><p>long time decades Um, and it used to be this type of group, but as it became more progressive</p><p>and stuff like that people left because they didn't like how it was going and And other people</p><p>because some people just wanted to become like a group just to be a god defying group And</p><p>others kind of wanted to take it to a more communal aspect and some people just didn't like that</p><p>um You I know personally for myself, this new change has kept me in the group for longer, you</p><p>know, I have been on a member of the Atheist United for a long time at this point, and it's</p><p>because like, Atheists United, there's, there's enough of God defying groups, you know?</p><p>There's not many community defying groups which are desperately needed for those third</p><p>spaces. And I think Atheists United is going in the right direction of that. I just think that like the</p><p>old members or people who are, still had this misconception of just like, oh, I'm just going to go,</p><p>you know, someone who's a new atheist, who's probably just, just, you know.</p><p>Through their angry, like betrayed phase and just trying to figure out themselves wanting to go,</p><p>you know, to a group that are kind of in their same state phase or stage, right? Um, You know,</p><p>and, and this group can, you know, a little bit can satisfy that. It's like, we can understand like</p><p>what happened, you know, like, yeah, you know, you went through this hard time.</p><p>Your family doesn't understand it yet. Yeah, we will be here and we can talk to you about that.</p><p>And, and it's just like, but we're also beyond that, we can also help you establish your new</p><p>values, figure out who you are, uh, figure out if, you know, if you are, if you would rather stay,</p><p>not stay as an atheist, but like we can also tell you like.</p><p>Yeah, there's also humanism. If you've never heard of humanism, maybe think about that.</p><p>Because, like, when I became, when I transitioned or, you know, chose to identify myself as a</p><p>humanist rather than an atheist, it was simply because, you know, atheism had, you know, was</p><p>based on not believing in something.</p><p>And humanism was based on the values of believing in myself, in my, you know, in generosity,</p><p>community, and just, you know, being more virtuous. And doing our best that like we're not</p><p>putting a faith into into into gods of thousands of years ago, but on the humanity of today,</p><p>because if you look at the history of humanity, we have done things that are pretty much God</p><p>defying, you know, like we, you know, we've eradicated diseases, we have, you know, Change</p><p>landscapes for the better and for worse, but yeah, you know, it's just you know with with that</p><p>Yeah, it's just like that's what I feel like That's what I feel like it is, you know, on, on the, I</p><p>think that, like you stated, the angry stages that you go through and maybe when new members</p><p>come in, they're still going through that process.</p><p>And I think there's sometimes where you go through a step where there's a fear of that, and</p><p>maybe that's why we do lose some members. Honestly, I don't have that information, but I can</p><p>see that happening. Um, on some of the meetings I've, I've, I've visited at the Atheist United. I</p><p>noticed that some are very, um, shy about being very open about their atheism.</p><p>I mean, I can understand why, you know, it's just a family members, you know, whatever their</p><p>job or their own community, wherever that may be. And because of that bracket of religion. If</p><p>they start stating that, you know, I'm an atheist, it could harm them, you know, economically or</p><p>psychologically. So I can see why people would leave, but if they, how much they leave their</p><p>membership, I wouldn't know that, that answer to that.</p><p>Yeah, I mean, that is, that is a good point too. A lot of people have, I think the reason, another</p><p>big reason why people don't join Atheist United or another atheistic community is that it is</p><p>dangerous. Mm-Hmm. Or that they do have this fear of danger. Mm-Hmm. I mean, I've had my</p><p>bumper sticker on my car for years now.</p><p>Right. Um, and, and just recently my mother-in-law passed away. So can you imagine how, like,</p><p>even then I didn't bring my car over for a couple times to my father in law's place because it was</p><p>just like, well, I'm not going to just take this off. Because it's, it's, you know, it's just a funny</p><p>joke, but I can understand the nuance of the car of the situation of like, I'm not going to bring this</p><p>car over to my father in law who's just grieving.</p><p>And then he sees that because I don't know how he's going to react to that, but in a general</p><p>sense, In society, people do have this like fear of like, Oh my God, like what if I have this on my</p><p>car? What if somebody keys my car? What if somebody, you know, breaks into my car or</p><p>something like that? And, and, and, and the fact of the matter is, is like, yeah, there is that risk.</p><p>There is that risk because even Evan has had his like magnets. Take it from his vehicle and he</p><p>had to get a new one and those are not easy to come by and the fact of the matter is it's just like</p><p>it's it's people who are who have been raised up to be intolerant of others of differing beliefs or</p><p>not believing in the same religions that they believe in that kind of makes people like, you know,</p><p>that's their like, Humanity off and they just react, you know, instead of just being like, Oh, like</p><p>this person doesn't believe in what I believe in.</p><p>I wonder why it's like, Oh, it's an attack on me. Thus, I have to attack them first. Even if it's just</p><p>the car sitting in a parking lot, even if I, you know, even if it's like, I'm just going to my car and</p><p>I'm just sitting in my vehicle. And I'm going to drive off. People just see it as an attack. I mean,</p><p>I've had that happen a couple times where people, when I'm driving in my vehicle, people, you</p><p>know, give me an extra harsh room behind me because they don't, they just don't want to see it.</p><p>Or I've seen people's reactions where they point and they're laughing or they're shaking their</p><p>head about it and they just, you know, and I've had even a couple compliments about it. There's</p><p>like, Oh man, that's a funny bumper sticker. And it's just like, the thing is that people, you know,</p><p>as we expose atheism and what it means to be an atheist, as more and more people become</p><p>atheists and are, are not afraid of becoming atheists, we are dispelling that, that myth.</p><p>And it's kind of like a, like a, like a herd immunity effect. The more people who know about it,</p><p>the less it's, you know, the less dangerous, um, It's perceived. And the more normalized it is, and</p><p>that's the whole goal, right? Uh, it's just to be, it's just to know that, like, in a society, you can</p><p>have, like, people who believe in several different gods, one Abrahamic god, and someone who</p><p>doesn't believe in any gods, and we can all get along, and we can all talk about it, and we can just</p><p>not start a religious war about it.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>So, my question, um, is, In one of the studies that I read was that the reason, or not the sole</p><p>reason, that, you know, atheist groups put out billboards and whatnot was to attract or to let</p><p>Others know that may be questioning their theistic beliefs and whatnot, that there is a community</p><p>out there of non believers that they could join this group and that within, you had touched on</p><p>this in that same study, it was talking about the fear of closeted atheists Um, afraid to step out</p><p>due to feeling ostracized, the stigma, it could be overwhelming, um, the loneliness.</p><p>Do you guys, um, do you guys want to talk about that at all? Or your thoughts on that?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>Um, you want to go first, [R1]?</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>No, [R2], you can go first.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>Um, so the question was the billboards and Atheist being open and hey we're here. Is that the</p><p>question? I guess the question is the um, I can understand the, the fear of being closeted and</p><p>like let's say one's family is all evangelical Christian and if they come out they're afraid they're</p><p>gonna lose their family and Um, so Or community, or you know, some people may be shunned.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Do you guys know anybody that that may have happened to?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>Or, um, I don't know anybody personally that that's happened to. Um, I, I watch a lot of</p><p>podcasts, a lot of things on YouTube. As far as, uh, atheist communities. And I do hear the horror</p><p>stories as far as like, even with, with young, with young people, uh, where they come to less than</p><p>18 years old and they're still living with their mother and or their parents, excuse me, and they're,</p><p>they're hardcore religious there, whatever that religion may be.</p><p>And then themselves find themselves questioning things about their own religion or about life in</p><p>general. Yeah. And there's been stories where I've heard where, um, they've been shunned.</p><p>They've been kicked out of their homes. They've been, they just like the parents do not want to</p><p>be dealing with this person that no longer live, no longer believes in the religion, you know?</p><p>And that's horrible. And there is a community out there that does help an atheist community that</p><p>does help people like that in order for them to get homes or find, or somewhat financially help</p><p>them. To get themselves out of that situation because that's a horrible thing for you and your</p><p>loving family who you thought loved you so much.</p><p>But there was, there's, there's a carrot. The only way you can stay here is to believe, you know,</p><p>and that's, excuse me, that's BS, you know, that, that, and that helps us as an atheist community.</p><p>Yeah. We posted out there to say, Hey, we're here there. If you need, if we have trouble or there's</p><p>something that's happening to you, we're here to help, you know, and there's no carrot with it.</p><p>We're here to help, you know, and there's constantly a fear, like, especially with the Muslim</p><p>religion, I. When I first started to find, look for a group, I found a Muslim religion where I just</p><p>wanted to see how it was for them because I know they're non believers and it's, it's a Muslim</p><p>religion. I mean, it's people get killed for, I mean, they get killed for other religions, but that's a</p><p>lot more hardcore than the ones I've known.</p><p>Um, and when I sent a message to them, I never got answered. And I was telling the leader from</p><p>my group, the one in San Gabriel here, Um, I was like, why do you think they never answer? She</p><p>goes, you gotta watch out, [R2], you gotta understand that these people are constantly having</p><p>to protect themselves.</p><p>And if they receive a message, it could be someone just trying to, you know, do something</p><p>they're supposed to do by following their own religion and harming these people. So yeah, I see</p><p>why, as a community, we want to be out there, we want to show for myself. With my stickers, I</p><p>want them to see like, look, this is a person that is out and open about it.</p><p>And it's okay to not believe. And if you need help to start asking questions, you can approach</p><p>that person. You can approach it. You can reach out. You don't necessarily have to attend. You</p><p>can do a zoom meeting and not post your video or just listen in. You know, there's some</p><p>wonderful things that you can do in order to get more information and maybe help yourself out.</p><p>I'll make you understand things about your world view in regards to religion.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Thank you.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>Yeah, I think he hit it, uh, hit the nail on the head with that one. Um, yeah, I mean, it's just when</p><p>we put up these billboards and when we are wearing our t shirts, even like I have a couple of</p><p>atheists, United t shirts that are just brazen, just offensive, but it's just like, yeah, I'm an atheist.</p><p>It's like, Um, I think the thing is, is that, yeah, we tend to make it seem like it's like a secret</p><p>society, or something like that, when it's really not. I mean, it's just, it's just the lack of You</p><p>know, resources to make it. It's hard to make an atheistic community. It's hard to make any kind</p><p>of community, um, and especially when it when it pertains to something as personal as, you</p><p>know, atheism and non, you know, non beliefs.</p><p>Um, I think that like, even then, it just makes people feel a little bit more comfortable. Just to</p><p>know that like, oh, there's somebody who does not believe the same thing as my parents does,</p><p>my parents do, or, you know, somebody who believes the same thing as me and, uh, and it's just,</p><p>you know, just being out there, I feel like gives people enough comfort to maybe start later on</p><p>being like, you know, I've seen a lot more.</p><p>You know, people who are just nonbelievers and I've seen this poster here. I've seen this</p><p>billboard here. Maybe it's not as secret as I thought. And then they start looking into it and then</p><p>they start looking in the communities and, um, and I think one more thing that like kind of</p><p>hinders people doing that is that I think people just don't think that they're smart enough to be an</p><p>atheist.</p><p>A lot of people tend to think that when you're an atheist, you have to be a debater. When you're</p><p>an atheist, you have to be a religious scholar who has read all the Bible, read the Quran, read the</p><p>Torah, and still hasn't, and still hasn't become, like, An atheist fully or, or like they, they have to</p><p>like get through this impossible standard of pretty much being like having a doctorate in</p><p>religious studies.</p><p>And then even on top of that, people still doubt that you, you know, that you're an atheist. Right.</p><p>But like the fact of the matter is, is that like, just like religion, atheists come in different levels of</p><p>education and, you know, everyone, is valid, you know, I wouldn't think of a Christian West</p><p>because they believe in, in the supernatural, you know, because there are smart Christians and</p><p>then there are Christians who, who didn't go to college or who maybe never even completed a</p><p>high school diploma, but like their religion and their beliefs.</p><p>It's still valid to them because it's valid to them. And so, I think it's just like, you know, you</p><p>don't have to read a bunch of books to be an atheist. Just the same as some Christians don't read</p><p>their even one book and become Christian. And so, it's like, why do we have this double standard</p><p>of just like, of this, this, this Uh, intellectualism when it's not needed.</p><p>And I think part of that is, I don't think it's like something that was made from. Christians or any</p><p>other religion. I think it just happened to be, just happened to come a thing by accident because</p><p>we all, because we only know like the prominent atheists as like Richard Dawkins or, uh, Sam</p><p>Harris or Carl Sagan, you know, very, very, very intellectual people, and that's all we know about</p><p>atheists, right?</p><p>That's all who we know, you know, whenever somebody thinks about atheism. Bye. I think that</p><p>as we, you know, again, as we expose more people to different types of people who are atheists,</p><p>they'll realize like, oh, you don't have to be smart as, you know, as Carl Sagan to become an</p><p>atheist. So I can become an atheist.</p><p>And it's like, yes, yes, you can. Yeah. I didn't get any smarter after I became an atheist. I started,</p><p>I think I started to become more open minded, but not any smarter. Right. Right. I mean, don't</p><p>get me wrong. Like I have a lot more tools to discern back from fiction, but I wouldn't say that</p><p>I'm going for my doctorate or for my PhD, you know, I'm just a simple, you know, network</p><p>engineer and that's.</p><p>You know, that's it for me. And I'm okay with that. And I don't need to have like a religious</p><p>studies, but like, you know, a lot of time you see it on Tik TOK and, and Facebook and Twitter,</p><p>like debate me, debate me, debate me. It's like, no, I don't want to, cause I don't need to, you</p><p>know, it's not a Pokemon battle.Yeah.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Okay. So this is my last question. Um, what else would either of you like? Me to know need to</p><p>know anything. Uh, would what? What else would you like me to know? Just open ended</p><p>question.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>As for myself, uh, me being an atheist and being very open about it, I mean, I actually have a</p><p>tattoo with a little icon showing it, you know, it's just, I am proud to be an atheist.</p><p>Uh, I love when people approach me to ask me about it because I want them to know that, hey,</p><p>we're here, you know, and we're not the ones with the horns. I love it. You know, one of the, one</p><p>of the comments that I've been given to me is like, [R2], you're more religious than the</p><p>religious people. I don't, why do you say that?</p><p>Because you're more honest in regards of like, the way you approach life. You're not bullshitting</p><p>things. You're not, you're not trying to like, dupe a person. And I go, well, no, I just, I want to be</p><p>as honest as I can, and if I don't know something, I'm not going to make it up. You know, I'll tell</p><p>you, you know what, don't know the answer to that question, let me round back to that.</p><p>You know, let me find out what I can, and I'll answer your question. You know, and me posting</p><p>up my stickers, I love it, it says, your friendly neighborhood atheist, big red letters, and white</p><p>background. And I've had a couple of people like give me the thumbs up or just stare at me like</p><p>[R1] said, I have one gentleman like, hey, I'm an atheist, he had a big old atheist icon on his, on</p><p>his, on his elbow, and those are moments where I love, you know, I got someone out there that</p><p>hey, you know, we're on the same level as far as like, we don't believe, you know, um, yeah,</p><p>there's other little things that we may not agree with.</p><p>We're here, you know, we're here and we're not, we're not the ones with the horns, like I said.</p><p>Yeah.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>Yeah, pretty much what [R2] said. It's just like we are, uh, you know, just ask, you know,</p><p>don't be afraid to ask, like, what do I believe in? You know, it seems that whenever somebody</p><p>hears that you're an atheist, they seem to already know.</p><p>When they don't know, because like, then you're just like, well, what do you, what do you think?</p><p>I believe it. And it's like, you believe in nothing. It's like, no, I don't believe in your one question.</p><p>I said, no, it's your one question, but what do I believe in? And you know, that is where</p><p>conversations can really happen.</p><p>But, um, you know, it's very interesting because like, I haven't won. I'm not yet, but I haven't had</p><p>somebody come up to me. And just ask me, like, what does it mean to be a humanist or an</p><p>atheist, you know? And, you know what? Only one. You know, actually, I did have that happen</p><p>once. It was with my father in law, and he was asking me, where do I get my morality?</p><p>And I know it's not the same as my beliefs, but he was asking where do I get my morality if I</p><p>don't get it from God? And I just simply said I got it from everyone else. You know, because it's</p><p>just, it's, it's just as simple as that, you know, you, you can, you can be a good and moral person</p><p>without needing a religion to, uh, to guide you on that path.</p><p>You can only just be inspired because I was inspired by everyone around me for all of my life.</p><p>And it's just, you know, if my father in law kind of just understood that you can get your, that</p><p>your morals and your values aren't these like divine texts, but they're from everyday small</p><p>actions. I think it would really make every, it would really humanize.</p><p>It would really humanize a humanist as well, because where are most of these people getting</p><p>their misinformation and their stereotypes of atheists from? Not from atheists, of course not, but</p><p>it's always coming from a different side who wants to make atheists, like, be something to be, to</p><p>be feared and villainized when they shouldn't be, you know?</p><p>So, I just, you know, if, you know, if this goes out to, to other people and other people read that</p><p>and other people understand, and want to understand us, It would just be, just talk to an, to a</p><p>local atheist, just be like, what are your values, um, as a human being? You know, you know,</p><p>because like, you're not going to get anything from a religious standpoint.</p><p>You're just going to get them from an, you know, a human experience standpoint. One of the</p><p>funniest questions I got asked by a friend of mine, um, he goes, do atheists mourn? And I was</p><p>like, I took a step back. I'm like, well, brother, of course, of course we mourn. We just, we just</p><p>don't affiliate. Like we don't, when, when we lose someone or we feel that those feelings.</p><p>We don't talk to our knees or clasp our hands, you know, that doesn't happen for me anymore. I</p><p>just self reflect on, just for example, having someone pass on in my life. I'll reflect on that person</p><p>and yes, I'll be sad, but for myself, I try to concentrate on how, how well that person touched me</p><p>in my life.</p><p>And that's what helps me more. And I just, he's like, Fredo, you're laughing. I'm like, well, yeah,</p><p>because I, I find it funny. It's not just a religious thing, you know, like, they go over, they reset</p><p>and start, you know, crossing themselves or go to the local church. It's like, we may reach out to</p><p>loved ones, and that's how we mourn.</p><p>That's how we just like, get through that. We're human beings, of course we mourn. And it's just</p><p>one of the, I mean, I ever got. Yeah, I mean, the same thing that happened with my mother in</p><p>law. She died, uh, two months ago. Uh, we were there on her, when she took her final breaths.</p><p>And, um, everyone was mourning. I will, you know, there was not a dry eye in the room.</p><p>And just because I was, just because I didn't believe where they believed that she was going.</p><p>Doesn't mean that I wasn't feeling that deep wound, that deep emotional wound. And. You know,</p><p>processing it my way was very different from processing is processing it their way like we have,</p><p>like we still have the service we have to go to we still have the celebration of life we have to go</p><p>to.</p><p>And, you know, for me, I had to process it in my own humanist way of like, I lost. A human</p><p>being that I deeply loved and I deeply respected, and that pain and loss is just a reflection of that</p><p>love and care I had for them, um, and I still have for them, and it's just, just because I don't</p><p>believe that they're in, like, this, like, heavenly amusement park or paradise doesn't mean that I,</p><p>like, wish, you Because I think a lot of people tend to think like, if you don't wish that they're</p><p>like up in the clouds being happy playing with their, you know, the, the, with their past dogs or</p><p>something like that, that you wish that they were like in this hellscape or worse, like they're in</p><p>this like a null void area.</p><p>And I'm just like, no, I don't wish that. I just see it as like, we don't know where we, you know,</p><p>we don't know where we go. Most likely, you know, most likely it's like, once we're gone, we're</p><p>gone. We'd have no awareness of anything else because like, that's where our, our awareness, our</p><p>feelings and our touch and our experiences and our memories come from our brain, if our brain</p><p>does not get any more oxygen and it's starved and it dies, we die with it.</p><p>But there's no pain and there is no, there's no misery. There's no anguish, but also there is no</p><p>happiness. There is no pleasure. There is no, uh, you know, excitement. And I'm, you know, and</p><p>it's like, you got to take them both at hand. You know, some, I think it's kind of like a half, you</p><p>know, a glass half full, half empty kind of thing.</p><p>I, you know, I'm just more of just like, I'm just glad she isn't suffering anymore, you know, and</p><p>that's what I'm looking at at. I'm glad she's not suffering anymore, but I'm not also like hoping</p><p>that like she's, you know, in the heavenly clouds playing chess with Jesus. You know, I just, I</p><p>just don't believe that it just goes against, it doesn't go against my values, but it just, as I navigate</p><p>through this world and what has helped me was going through this scientific, more evidence</p><p>based kind of thinking and the science and the evidence, you know, haven't shown that there is</p><p>some kind of afterlife after this.</p><p>And so thus it does. help with, you know, with my own mortality, because, you know, seeing</p><p>something like that gets you thinking. And it's like, yeah, I mean, it has really solidified a lot of</p><p>things for me as an atheist. And as a humanist myself, it's just like, You know, you, you tend to</p><p>think that you're prepared for it, but when you see it happen, it kind of, it tests the waters, it tests</p><p>your foundations.</p><p>I mean, it didn't make me start thinking like, Oh man, maybe I should go to church. Um, it's just</p><p>like, you kind of have to like reevaluate yourself and be like, you know, like, yeah, as an atheist,</p><p>I am prepared to leave, pass on, die. And I'm prepared for nothing after that. There's nothing,</p><p>you know, I am, I'm ready for nothing after that.</p><p>And I'm going to be okay with that.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>I'm sorry, [R1]. Um, I just, I had a thought right now. Um, I think with me, as far as like with</p><p>death and other things that are affiliated with religion, you know, it's just like, I want to maybe</p><p>drop a little bit in this world and say, Hey, look, [R2] was this kind of person.</p><p>He wasn't religious, but he did things that people always thought was affiliated with, with</p><p>religion or church or something like that. You know, I've done things where normally people</p><p>think, oh, you have to be such and such in order to do that. No, you don't. You don't. You know,</p><p>those are things that anybody can still do without affiliating yourself with religion, you know?</p><p>And that's something that I, I hope people see in the world that are religious in my world. And</p><p>say, well, he used to do it. Why do I have to do it that way? I can do it another way I want, you</p><p>know, and that's what I hope I take people take away from. And when you mean by that, do you</p><p>mean like being a good person, doing good deeds, like these things that are Affiliated with a</p><p>religious institution doing being a good person and not thinking like, Hey, I'm doing this just</p><p>because I'm going to go up.</p><p>I'm doing this because I want to avoid this over here. You know, like, I want to do things</p><p>because, hey, [R2] was this and he came all the way from here. Just. This is what he wanted</p><p>to do because he wanted to hop out. You know, he just wanted to do good. You just do good. Just</p><p>like, yeah. I mean, like doing good is feels good.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>You know, that, that, that is the deeper thing than anything else. Like I don't. I don't think of it as</p><p>like, you know, when I, when I go to a food distribution, I don't think of it as like, Oh, I'm</p><p>punching a hole in my heaven ticket. And, uh, you know, I'm not thinking of it like that. I'm just</p><p>like, because I don't like seeing people suffer in, in, in, in that kind of way.</p><p>I don't like seeing. a family wondering where their next meal is going to come from. And we</p><p>gave like 30 to 30 to like 40 pounds of like food to like a family and like how grateful they are.</p><p>Just it is something that. You just have to see and experience for yourself and understand that</p><p>like, Oh, that didn't come like I, you know, that didn't come from like God rewarding me.</p><p>That's from my own brain rewarding me because deep down inside we as social creatures that we</p><p>are, we depend on each other. And, you know, it doesn't need, you know, we don't need a religion</p><p>to, to connect us. It's just, you know, you know, we're human beings. Thanks. We, you know, and</p><p>you know, the only reason why we kind of separate each other and from helping one another is</p><p>because of like these minuscule at the grand scheme of things differences, whether it's like the</p><p>gods that we believe in or the color of somebody's skin or whatever their political party is at.</p><p>It's like, I don't, you know, I don't care if you have a different belief than me. I don't care if you</p><p>just don't like me because of my color of my skin. Do you need help is my first question. It</p><p>reminds me of that, um, of that photo of, I don't know if you guys have seen it, but like of that</p><p>old photo of that Klansman.</p><p>On the operating table and there was like a, a team of, of black nurses and doctors that were just</p><p>helping him out and it's like that, like that right there that shows it's like your hate or your diff or</p><p>your, your, your misinterpretation of me doesn't matter. If you need help, I'm going to help you.</p><p>And then we can discuss about like, what do you think that you're the master race or not, or</p><p>whatever.</p><p>But like, at least you're going to be here. You're right. At least this could change your mind a</p><p>little bit. And it's like those acts of good tend to change a lot more people than acts of superiority</p><p>or even worse villainy, because you cannot just. a race, a group of atheists and been like problem</p><p>solved, the entire world is religious.</p><p>There's always going to be people who are going to be questioning, uh, their religious dogma.</p><p>There's always going to be people who are going to be questioning whatever, uh, whatever that</p><p>they were taught by their, um, um, by their parents or tradition. But it's that kind of questioning</p><p>and that kind of, you know, A little bit of defiance that progresses that makes us progress go</p><p>forward in our lives and like and to get to where we were like 300 years ago to now that took a</p><p>lot of defiance that took a lot of just like stepping into the unknown and being curious and just</p><p>questioning things and that's I mean, it keeps, it keeps working, you know.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Awesome. Well, it looks like it's 1106. I know, that was fast, huh?</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>It was fun, to tell you the truth, it was fun.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>I really, truly appreciate both of you contributing to this. It means a lot to me, it means a lot to,</p><p>you know, I think a part of this work that I want to take on, at least to present to my, because</p><p>this is going to end up being like a paper that I'm writing.</p><p>And hopefully like, if I want to go to graduate school, I can use this as a writing sample for</p><p>graduate school. And then eventually maybe get a PhD, but what I'm really taking, what I've</p><p>really taken away and what I feel like as a duty for myself is that when I do this presentation,</p><p>I'm going to be so happy to advocate.</p><p>For your community and to talk, cause I'm going to be talking to, you know, faculty and students,</p><p>and to be able to advocate for you guys in the sense that these are just ordinary people, atheists</p><p>are humans too, you know, and so I'm just, beg your pardon.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>I mean, that's exactly it. That's the, that's the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:////Users/abaugh/Library/Group%20Containers/UBF8T346G9.Office/TemporaryItems/msohtmlclip/clip_filelist.xml">goal.It</a>'s just. Yeah, we're just human beings that</p><p>just happened to not just happen to say no on the God question.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>And so I'm just really happy to be a part of destigmatizing atheism and atheists. And so I'm just</p><p>so ever grateful. And, um, next semester I'm actually taking a class on So I'm excited to maybe</p><p>I'll do another research project.</p><p>I'll reach out to Evan again. Um, maybe to either further this work that I'm doing or maybe start a</p><p>new one, but I'm just so grateful for both of you contributing and being open and honest and</p><p>vulnerable. And so I. I'm truly, you know, really grateful. So thank you so much. Of course, of</p><p>course. Now, I got a question.</p><p>[R1]:</p><p>Um, is it possible, like, once you finish with that paper, that we could, like, read it? Because I'm</p><p>so curious about, like, how it's gonna, you know, how, like, we are influencing your paper and</p><p>everything.</p><p>[Int]:</p><p>Yes, definitely. I will. Oh my gosh. I have an audience.</p><p>It was so funny in my, in like writing this paper, it's like, my teacher's having us read these books</p><p>and like, Oh, you have to think from it. Like, what is the audience going to think? And I'm like, I</p><p>don't have an audience now. I have an audience. So now. Great. So thank you so much. Yeah, I'd</p><p>be happy to, um, I have your email, [R1], and, um, I know, um, [R2], you're connected with</p><p>Arlene, and I have Arlene's email, or if you want My email, I can put it on the chat if you like.</p><p>Yeah, great. So let's</p><p>open the chat.</p><p>[R2]:</p><p>I'm going to send it to you. I'm not sure if you 