66a4f5fabe3b9c9941b16e25	Unity of Fort Lauderdale Spiritual Center	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086239089.jpg" alt="IMG_1924.JPG" loading="lazy" width="371" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086239085.jpg" alt="IMG_1682.JPG" loading="lazy" width="371" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>Situated in a trendy strip mall located just a few blocks from the beach rests the new home of Unity of Fort Lauderdale Spiritual Center. Unity was first founded in 1889 by Charles and Myrtle Fillmore, and it is often historically located within the lineage of New Thought and the Prosperity Gospel (see Kate Bowler, <em>Blessed, </em>33-35). Headquartered near Kansas City, Missouri, today <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.unity.org/static/about-us">Unity boasts</a> over 600 study groups and churches established across the globe.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086392787.jpg" alt="IMG_1929.JPG" loading="lazy" width="328" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086428136.jpg" alt="IMG_1928.JPG" loading="lazy" width="329" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><p>Nestled between restaurants, spas, salons, and bars, Unity’s newest Fort Lauderdale location promises to offer an inclusive and tranquil space for the spiritual seeker. Peering through the glass, the interior looks like an empty office space filled with desks and chairs but peppered with affirmations printed on plain white paper and hung throughout the building, stating: “We create sacred space. We be the energetic vibration of LOVE/PEACE/APPRECIATION/ABUNDANCE/ALLOWING/BEINGNESS/DIGNITY…”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086583270.jpg" alt="IMG_E2060.JPG" loading="lazy" width="400" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086605102.jpg" alt="IMG_2062.JPG" loading="lazy" width="349" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086638922.jpg" alt="IMG_E2064.JPG" loading="lazy" width="497" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>More information is available at the door for the curious passerby, including a card with the “5 Guiding Principles of Unity” and a 55-page booklet titled “WORTHY: LGBTQ Stories of Overcoming Rejection and Religion to Find Truth” (published by Unity Village in Missouri).</p><p>While remnants of the late nineteenth century New Thought movement are still clearly evident in statements such as “Thoughts held in MIND produce after their kind,” the Unity of the twenty-first century also appears to include a strong focus on a different kind of healing than that of the past – the healing of religious trauma for those deemed unacceptable and unworthy by other religious traditions.</p><p></p><p>Sources:</p><p>“About Us,” <em>Unity</em>, accessed June 24, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.unity.org/static/about-us</a>.<br>Kate Bowler, <em>Blessed: A History of the American Prosperity Gospel</em>, New York: Oxford University Press, 2013.</p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	26.1686453	-80.1013607	3331 NE 32nd St, Fort Lauderdale, FL 33308, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "Unity", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "StripMall", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "LGBTQIA+", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-09 13:14:57.567	2024-07-27 13:28:26.521	2024-11-22 20:05:06.695	\N
