66a4f60970d026a9e2a2ec54	Unity of Fort Lauderdale Spiritual Center	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086239089.jpg" alt="IMG_1924.JPG" loading="lazy" width="371" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086239085.jpg" alt="IMG_1682.JPG" loading="lazy" width="371" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>Situated in a trendy strip mall located just a few blocks from the beach rests the new home of Unity of Fort Lauderdale Spiritual Center. Unity was first founded in 1889 by Charles and Myrtle Fillmore, and it is often historically located within the lineage of New Thought and the Prosperity Gospel (see Kate Bowler, <em>Blessed, </em>33-35). Headquartered near Kansas City, Missouri, today <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.unity.org/static/about-us">Unity boasts</a> over 600 study groups and churches established across the globe.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086392787.jpg" alt="IMG_1929.JPG" loading="lazy" width="328" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086428136.jpg" alt="IMG_1928.JPG" loading="lazy" width="329" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><p>Nestled between restaurants, spas, salons, and bars, Unity’s newest Fort Lauderdale location promises to offer an inclusive and tranquil space for the spiritual seeker. Peering through the glass, the interior looks like an empty office space filled with desks and chairs but peppered with affirmations printed on plain white paper and hung throughout the building, stating: “We create sacred space. We be the energetic vibration of LOVE/PEACE/APPRECIATION/ABUNDANCE/ALLOWING/BEINGNESS/DIGNITY…”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086583270.jpg" alt="IMG_E2060.JPG" loading="lazy" width="400" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086605102.jpg" alt="IMG_2062.JPG" loading="lazy" width="349" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722086638922.jpg" alt="IMG_E2064.JPG" loading="lazy" width="497" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>More information is available at the door for the curious passerby, including a card with the “5 Guiding Principles of Unity” and a 55-page booklet titled “WORTHY: LGBTQ Stories of Overcoming Rejection and Religion to Find Truth” (published by Unity Village in Missouri).</p><p>While remnants of the late nineteenth century New Thought movement are still clearly evident in statements such as “Thoughts held in MIND produce after their kind,” the Unity of the twenty-first century also appears to include a strong focus on a different kind of healing than that of the past – the healing of religious trauma for those deemed unacceptable and unworthy by other religious traditions.</p><p></p><p>Sources:</p><p>“About Us,” <em>Unity</em>, accessed June 24, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.unity.org/static/about-us</a>.<br>Kate Bowler, <em>Blessed: A History of the American Prosperity Gospel</em>, New York: Oxford University Press, 2013.</p><p></p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	26.1686453	-80.1013607	3331 NE 32nd St, Fort Lauderdale, FL 33308, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "Unity", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "StripMall", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "LGBTQIA+", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-09 13:14:57.567	2024-07-27 13:28:41.23	2024-11-22 20:05:12.854	\N
66ad4fe570ac713135a7615e	Ermita de la Caridad	<p>Just five miles south of Miami’s Little Havana, on the coast of Biscayne Bay, sits the conical-shaped National Shrine of Our Lady of Charity – what Thomas Tweed once described as "the sacred center of the Cuban Catholic community in exile” (Thomas Tweed, <em>Our Lady of the Exile: Diasporic Religion at a Cuban Catholic Shrine in Miami</em>, 1997, pg. 3).</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633422319.jpg" alt="IMG_1709.JPG" loading="lazy" width="875" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633465902.jpg" alt="IMG_1694.JPG" loading="lazy" width="875" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633521957.jpg" alt="IMG_1721.JPG" loading="lazy" width="876" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633556150.jpg" alt="IMG_1754.JPG" loading="lazy" width="883" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The centerpiece of the site is located in the sanctuary, where behind the altar rests a doll-size figurine of Our Lady of Charity, wearing a fanned-out golden dress and illuminated from below. The figurine is positioned in a way that incorporates her into the stunning mural painted on the wall behind the altar, which extends from the floor of the altar to the ceiling. According to a brochure available at the shrine, the mural narrates the history of Cuban Catholicism. The mural includes 44 important figures in this history from 1492-1977, beginning with Christopher Columbus in the lower left corner, and culminating in an image of two angels carrying a Cuban flag at the top, which the brochure describes as a representation of “carrying the homeland to God.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633601663.jpg" alt="IMG_1748.JPG" loading="lazy" width="894" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633622483.jpg" alt="IMG_1723.JPG" loading="lazy" width="891" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633671279.jpg" alt="IMG_1728.JPG" loading="lazy" width="880" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633711366.jpg" alt="IMG_1725.JPG" loading="lazy" width="892" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p>In 1997 Thomas Tweed published his extensive ethnographic study of the shrine to Our Lady of Charity, which offers in-depth information on the history of the shrine and its devotees in the 1990s. That being said, the shrine is not frozen in time, and messaging added to the exterior grounds of the site has been updated to include slogans from the 2021 protests in both Cuba and Miami, criticizing the Cuban socialist government. For example, the hashtag #SOSCuba, used to spread awareness of the protests on social media, is printed on a banner below the petition, <em>Virgen de la Caridad, Salva a Cuba!</em> (Virgin of Charity, Save Cuba!). Further, attached to the fence on the opposite side of the grounds is a phrase that can be seen on many flags hanging from balconies in Little Havana: <em>Patria y Vida. </em>This phrase comes from a reggaeton song which criticized the Cuban government and was adapted as the unofficial slogan of the 2021 protests. On the grounds of the shrine, this slogan has been further modified to include the word <em>Dios</em>, as it is written above an image of the Cuban flag and American flag, connected in the center by Our Lady of Charity: <em>Dios: Patria y Vida</em> (God: Homeland and Life).</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633761010.jpg" alt="IMG_1698.JPG" loading="lazy" width="909" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633827166.jpg" alt="IMG_1703.JPG" loading="lazy" width="876" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633849479.jpg" alt="IMG_1712.JPG" loading="lazy" width="879" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633865260.jpg" alt="IMG_1711.JPG" loading="lazy" width="890" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633883753.jpg" alt="IMG_1773.JPG" loading="lazy" width="893" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Sources:</p><p>Anamaria Artemisa Sayre, “Explaining 'Patria Y Vida,' The Song That's Defined The Uprising In Cuba,” <em>NPR</em>, July 20, 2021, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.npr.org/sections/altlatino/2021/07/19/1017887993/explaining-patria-y-vida-the-cuban-song-defying-an-evil-revolution</a>.</p><p><em>The Mural at the Shrine</em> (Miami: National Shrine of Our Lady of Charity).</p><p>Thomas Tweed, <em>Our Lady of the Exile: Diasporic Religion at a Cuban Catholic Shrine in Miami</em> (New York: Oxford University Press), 1997.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	25.7413124	-80.2115432	3609 S Miami Ave, Miami, FL 33129, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "Shrine", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Catholicism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Cuba", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-07 21:15:29.276	2024-08-02 21:30:13.347	2026-04-15 13:48:36.847	\N
66abc97e70ac713135a76150	Mother Jones Monument	<p>SOME EDIT</p><p></p><p>I found my way to the Mother Jones monument by the beckoning of a life-size image of Mary Harris Jones (1837-1930), located inside the Coalfield Rest Area on Interstate 55, just 25 miles south of Springfield, Illinois. Dressed in a sharp purple overcoat and positioned in front an image of a diverse group of coal miners, the stern stare of “Mother Jones” urged me to take a short detour on my drive home from Milwaukee to St. Louis to learn more about this intriguing woman and “her boys.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533320283.jpg" alt="IMG_1976.JPG" loading="lazy" width="295" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533365480.jpg" alt="IMG_1974.JPG" loading="lazy" width="413" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The Mother Jones Monument is located 20 miles south of the Coalfield Rest Area in Mt. Olive, Illinois, just a few turns off Old Route 66, behind a small neighborhood speckled with political yard signs suggestive of a small town with a conservative majority. The monument is situated within the Union Miners Cemetery, which is claimed to be “the only labor union-established and union-owned cemetery in the United States.” The cemetery was purchased in an effort to secure a burial site for miners who perished in the “Battle of Virden” (a deadly struggle that took place between miners and forces of the Chicago-Virden Coal Company in 1898), and all miners thereafter.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533424736.jpg" alt="IMG_1984.JPG" loading="lazy" width="511" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533446701.jpg" alt="IMG_1986.JPG" loading="lazy" width="517" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533461090.jpg" alt="IMG_1982.JPG" loading="lazy" width="519" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The monument itself is located at the far edge of the cemetery, and it marks the burial place of Mother Jones, who dedicated her life to fighting for the rights of coal miners. At the same time, the monument also stands as a memorial to those miners whose lives were lost in the Battle of Virden and similar deadly conflicts over union miners’ rights.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>While traditional religious language is often expected at cemeteries, it is prominent at this site in unexpected ways. For example, those who “have given their lives to the cause of clean unionism in America” are labeled as “martyrs” on a monument placard; Mother Jones is given the title, “The Miner’s Angel” on an engraved stone; and a Mother Jones quotation at the bottom of a list of donors borrows language of Christian atonement theology, stating, “I learned in the early part of my career that labor must bear the cross for others’ sins, must be the vicarious sufferer for the wrongs that others do.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533517431.jpg" alt="IMG_1996.JPG" loading="lazy" width="610" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533560088.jpg" alt="IMG_2013.JPG" loading="lazy" width="613" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533580684.jpg" alt="IMG_1998.JPG" loading="lazy" width="629" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533615677.jpg" alt="IMG_2031.JPG" loading="lazy" width="625" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>While Mother Jones was raised in the Roman Catholic tradition, she is said to have later rejected organized religion which she believed caused workers to become complacent in this world because of its focus on the afterlife. Still, Mother Jones believed Jesus to be a revolutionary and a model for workers in their fight against wealthy “parasites,” and she befriended several clergymen with similar activist values (Rosemary Feurer, “Mother Jones: A Global History of Struggle and Remembrance, from Cork, Ireland to Illinois,” <em>Illinois Heritage,</em> May 2013, 30).</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The Mother Jones Monument stands as a witness to the life of a woman who was committed to the struggle for labor rights and class justice, while at the same time revealing a background tension between an opioid religion and an activist religion, made apparent throughout the site in characteristic quotations from Mother Jones such as, “Pray for the dead and fight like hell for the living!”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533655531.jpg" alt="IMG_2016.JPG" loading="lazy" width="665" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533690344.jpg" alt="IMG_2017.JPG" loading="lazy" width="661" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533709059.jpg" alt="IMG_2019.JPG" loading="lazy" width="661" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>Sources:&nbsp;</p><p>Rosemary Feurer, “Mother Jones: A Global History of Struggle and Remembrance, from Cork, Ireland to Illinois,” <em>Illinois Heritage,</em> May 2013, 28-33.&nbsp;</p><p>“Who Was Mother Jones?” Mother Jones Museum, accessed July 29, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.motherjonesmuseum.org/who-was-mother-jones</a>.</p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	39.08101	-89.7319255	5585 Mt Olive Rd, Mt Olive, IL 62069, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "Monument", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Cemetery", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Activism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "LaborUnions", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-10 22:30:59.119	2024-08-01 17:44:30.362	2024-11-22 20:04:13.489	\N
