66c162b5ee271ee786d831e0	4th of July Parade, 2022 	<p>For nearly 40 years, breaking only for the pandemic, members of my extended family have been gathering with food and drinks at the North Avenue bridge over the Menomonee River to celebrate and watch the Fourth of July Parade in Wauwatosa, Wisconsin. “Tosa,” as it is familiarly called, is a <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.wauwatosa.net/discover-tosa/about-wauwatosa">predominately white middle-class suburb</a> of Milwaukee (81.4% of residents are “non-Hispanic White”). As Wisconsin’s 14<sup>th</sup> largest city, Wauwatosa is relatively younger (median age of 37.9 years) and more educated (58.6% of adults hold a bachelor’s degree or higher) than both the state and national averages. Although Wisconsin is politically considered to be a “swing state,” like <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.milwaukeemag.com/comparing-how-the-city-and-every-milwaukee-county-suburb-voted-in-2016-and-2020/">most of the cities in Milwaukee County</a>, the majority of Wauwatosa residents reliably vote democrat in both <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.wauwatosa.net/government/departments/city-clerk/elections-voting/election-results">local and national elections</a>, with at least one-third still solidly republican. &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>While the mood was jubilant and celebratory, the crowd was considerably more sparse in 2022 than those from my childhood days, when designated family members would have to camp out for hours to secure our coveted spot on the North Avenue bridge. In contrast, this year parking was ample and good parade-watching spots were plentiful, as the crowd was too thin to fill both sides of the street. Still, those in attendance were decked out in patriotic gear as many united with friends and family of all ages alongside their bloody mary bars and tables of food.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948571650.jpg" alt="7.4.22_1 crowd.JPG" loading="lazy" width="324" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>Before the parade began, a cheery gentleman was walking along the sidewalk handing something out to every person he saw. It was a fake “million dollar bill,” and on the back it said “In God We Trust,” and a paragraph that begins with, “THE MILLION DOLLAR QUESTION: Will you go to Heaven when you die?...” The paragraph ends by directing readers to visit “<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">NeedGod.com</a>” and “<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">LivingWaters.com</a>.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948608094.jpg" alt="7.4.22_2 million.JPG" loading="lazy" width="331" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948625433.jpg" alt="7.4.22_3 million.JPG" loading="lazy" width="332" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The unofficial start to the parade was a group of Harley Davidson motorcycles with large American flags on the back. This was followed by a man and a woman who appeared to have jumped into a gap in the lineup carrying a sign that simply said “Jesus.” The man shouted, “Many signers of the Constitution were pastors who gave their lives and honor so that we could have our freedoms today. Trust in Jesus…” Hearing this, my aunt (a retired nurse) made a quick comment to me about how rights and freedoms were also taken away from indigenous people who had originally resided in the land; a comment that gave voice to the unexpressed tension of celebrating independence on stolen land named after its indigenous inhabitants.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948678303.jpg" alt="7.4.22_3.5 harley.JPG" loading="lazy" width="343" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948701974.jpg" alt="7.4.22_4 Jesus.JPG" loading="lazy" width="343" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The parade officially began with police motorcycles, a fire truck, and a float with Tripoli Shriner Veterans with about 10 American flags and a sign stating, “Honoring all Veterans” and another sign stating, “Please Stand For Your Flag.” Attendees pridefully stood and clapped. In full swing, the parade included high school bands, color guards, and dance teams; politicians; civic organizations; veterans’ organizations; businesses; and local media personalities.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948741086.jpg" alt="7.4.22_5 Shriners.JPG" loading="lazy" width="347" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>Taking place just ten days after the overturning of Roe v. Wade in the Supreme Court Dobbs decision, there were subtle signs of patriotic protest in the parade. For example, one woman marching with the Girl Scouts wore a dress with an American flag design and peace sign earrings. She was carrying a small American flag, and written on her left arm were the words “Protect Women.” I saw this woman again later on as she was walking back from the end of the parade, and I noticed on her right arm was written, “I dissent.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948775298.jpg" alt="7.4.22_6 girlscouts1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="350" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948794672.jpg" alt="7.4.22_6 girlscouts2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="270" style="aspect-ratio: 0.476761 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">The phrase “I dissent” also appeared on a t-shirt worn by a man marching with the group that garnered the loudest applause from the crowd, called “Tosa Together.” Leading the group was a banner which stated, “Growing Equity &amp; Diversity in Wauwatosa.” Other t-shirt statements included “Black Lives Matter - Wauwatosa” and “AntiRacist – Wauwatosa.” In addition to an American flag were rainbow flags and a Black Lives Matter flag. One person carried a sign written in English, Spanish, and Arabic which said: “No matter where you are from, we’re glad you’re our neighbor.” Another group passed by later on similarly promoting LGBT equality, led by a vehicle with a rainbow sign that read, “Free Mom Hugs.”</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948869539.jpg" alt="7.4.22_7 inclusion1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="574" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3335 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948883672.jpg" alt="7.4.22_8 inclusion2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="579" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33398 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948901731.jpg" alt="7.4.22_9 inclusion3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="439" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948939745.jpg" alt="7.4.22_91 inclusion4.JPG" loading="lazy" width="441" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948917573.jpg" alt="7.4.22_92 inclusion5.JPG" loading="lazy" width="445" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>About halfway through the parade, two men were walking near the sidewalk, unaffiliated with the parade, handing out material to people. One of them was pushing a dolly with a box strapped to it which had a picture of the US on the front with a cross and the words, “This Great Nation was Founded on the GOSPEL of Jesus Christ.” On the side it read, “‘It is impossible to govern a nation without God and the Bible.’ - President George Washington.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948976029.jpg" alt="7.4.22_93 shalom1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="452" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948998482.jpg" alt="7.4.22_94 shalom2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="459" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><p>On the outside of the folder that contained the materials was written, “US Presidents Declare Christianity” with a list of alleged quotes from former presidents to defend this statement. The other side included claims that the United States was founded solely on the basis of Christianity, and the inside included a plea for America to return to its “Christian heritage.” The envelope inside the folder indicated that the church distributing the materials was Shalom Baptist Church in Brown Deer (another Milwaukee suburb). Also printed in red letters on the envelope were statements condemning homosexuality and abortion. Inside the envelope contained a slew of pamphlets, including several Chick tracts, one of which stoked fears that Islam is overtaking the United States and will bring an end to Christianity in America if not stopped.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949033347.jpg" alt="7.4.22_95 shalom3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="324" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949054233.jpg" alt="7.4.22_96 shalom4.JPG" loading="lazy" width="321" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949071516.jpg" alt="7.4.22_97 shalom5.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949104887.jpg" alt="7.4.22_99 shalom7.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949120433.jpg" alt="7.4.22_98 shalom6.JPG" loading="lazy" width="321" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">While the representatives from Shalom Baptist Church had just inserted themselves into a gap in the parade, there were two churches that officially marched as participants in the parade. My aunt notified me early about the first group, suggesting to be on the lookout for “the Baptists” who always have the largest float in the parade. She was right. Before the arrival of the semi-truck carrying the float, were two women in their late teens wearing long skirts carrying a sign that said, “Big Dig,” which is the title of the upcoming Vacation Bible School at nearby Falls Baptist Church. The semi passed with loud and dramatic music playing, and on the semi was an archeological scene with several people on the float waving and an American flag in the center. As the float was driving past, I heard a man in the group next to us say, “Now that’s big church money.”</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949161078.jpg" alt="7.4.22_991 baptist1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="491" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949184628.jpg" alt="7.4.22_992 baptist2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="490" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">In comparison to the big money at Falls Baptist Church, the other church represented in the parade seemed pintsize. Wauwatosa Avenue United Methodist Church was one of the last organizations to march in the parade, carrying a sign that read, “Welcome Pastor Kate,” with children riding bikes adorned with flags. They handed out cards which provided information about the church, including that it is an inclusive “reconciling congregation” that welcomes all.</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949225979.jpg" alt="7.4.22_994 UMC2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="443" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949244024.jpg" alt="7.4.22_995 UMC3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="440" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The parade ended somewhat abruptly with a car that carried two local TV news anchors. My family and I were confused about whether or not it was over, and we all were in agreement that this year’s parade was rather underwhelming compared to pre-pandemic years.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Despite its small size, the 2022 Wauwatosa Fourth of July parade still had some big things to say about religion, patriotism, and ideology in a midwestern swing state. While Christian nationalism was on full display by those who unofficially inserted themselves into the lineup, it appears to have been excluded by those city volunteers who organized the parade. This ultimately regulated the messaging to represent Wauwatosa as an open and inclusive community, which countered the anti-abortion, anti-LGBTQIA+, and anti-Muslim sentiments included in materials from sideline entries like Shalom Baptist Church.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>While patriotism was everywhere, it seemed to carry different meanings for different organizations. For some it was pride in storied origins and an argument to return to better days, while for others it was pride in progress and fuel for positive change, and it was clear that Wauwatosa city leaders were driving for the latter. Still, I am left wondering about the effects of those who entered into the parade on the margins – did their messages of Christian nationalism appeal to, or even radicalize, any parade-goers who may have felt concerned about the declining attendance and rising progressive tone of this beloved annual tradition?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Sources&nbsp;</strong></p><p>“About Wauwatosa,” City of Wauwatosa, WI, accessed August 10, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.wauwatosa.net/discover-tosa/about-wauwatosa</a>.&nbsp;</p><p>“Election Results,” City of Wauwatosa, WI, accessed August 10, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.wauwatosa.net/government/departments/city-clerk/elections-voting/election-results</a>.&nbsp;</p><p>Allie Habeck, “Comparing How the City and Every Milwaukee County Suburb Voted in 2016 and 2020,” <em>Milwaukee Magazine</em>, November 4, 2020, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.milwaukeemag.com/comparing-how-the-city-and-every-milwaukee-county-suburb-voted-in-2016-and-2020/</a>.</p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949644457.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949732271.mp4">Video 2</a></p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	43.0604448	-88.03201469999999	North & Menomonee, Wauwatosa, WI 53226, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "Patriotism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "ChristianNationalism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Parade", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-04 14:00:21.269	2024-08-18 02:55:49.868	2024-11-22 20:03:38.181	\N
66c16203986eb9dd86ee83a9	4th of July Parade, 2022 	<p>For nearly 40 years, breaking only for the pandemic, members of my extended family have been gathering with food and drinks at the North Avenue bridge over the Menomonee River to celebrate and watch the Fourth of July Parade in Wauwatosa, Wisconsin. “Tosa,” as it is familiarly called, is a <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.wauwatosa.net/discover-tosa/about-wauwatosa">predominately white middle-class suburb</a> of Milwaukee (81.4% of residents are “non-Hispanic White”). As Wisconsin’s 14<sup>th</sup> largest city, Wauwatosa is relatively younger (median age of 37.9 years) and more educated (58.6% of adults hold a bachelor’s degree or higher) than both the state and national averages. Although Wisconsin is politically considered to be a “swing state,” like <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.milwaukeemag.com/comparing-how-the-city-and-every-milwaukee-county-suburb-voted-in-2016-and-2020/">most of the cities in Milwaukee County</a>, the majority of Wauwatosa residents reliably vote democrat in both <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.wauwatosa.net/government/departments/city-clerk/elections-voting/election-results">local and national elections</a>, with at least one-third still solidly republican. <span>&nbsp;</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>While the mood was jubilant and celebratory, the crowd was considerably more sparse in 2022 than those from my childhood days, when designated family members would have to camp out for hours to secure our coveted spot on the North Avenue bridge. In contrast, this year parking was ample and good parade-watching spots were plentiful, as the crowd was too thin to fill both sides of the street. Still, those in attendance were decked out in patriotic gear as many united with friends and family of all ages alongside their bloody mary bars and tables of food.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948571650.jpg" alt="7.4.22_1 crowd.JPG" loading="lazy" width="324" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Before the parade began, a cheery gentleman was walking along the sidewalk handing something out to every person he saw. It was a fake “million dollar bill,” and on the back it said “In God We Trust,” and a paragraph that begins with, “THE MILLION DOLLAR QUESTION: Will you go to Heaven when you die?...” The paragraph ends by directing readers to visit “<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">NeedGod.com</a>” and “<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">LivingWaters.com</a>.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948608094.jpg" alt="7.4.22_2 million.JPG" loading="lazy" width="331" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948625433.jpg" alt="7.4.22_3 million.JPG" loading="lazy" width="332" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The unofficial start to the parade was a group of Harley Davidson motorcycles with large American flags on the back. This was followed by a man and a woman who appeared to have jumped into a gap in the lineup carrying a sign that simply said “Jesus.” The man shouted, “Many signers of the Constitution were pastors who gave their lives and honor so that we could have our freedoms today. Trust in Jesus…” Hearing this, my aunt (a retired nurse) made a quick comment to me about how rights and freedoms were also taken away from indigenous people who had originally resided in the land; a comment that gave voice to the unexpressed tension of celebrating independence on stolen land named after its indigenous inhabitants.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948678303.jpg" alt="7.4.22_3.5 harley.JPG" loading="lazy" width="343" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948701974.jpg" alt="7.4.22_4 Jesus.JPG" loading="lazy" width="343" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The parade officially began with police motorcycles, a fire truck, and a float with Tripoli Shriner Veterans with about 10 American flags and a sign stating, “Honoring all Veterans” and another sign stating, “Please Stand For Your Flag.” Attendees pridefully stood and clapped. In full swing, the parade included high school bands, color guards, and dance teams; politicians; civic organizations; veterans’ organizations; businesses; and local media personalities.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948741086.jpg" alt="7.4.22_5 Shriners.JPG" loading="lazy" width="347" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Taking place just ten days after the overturning of Roe v. Wade in the Supreme Court Dobbs decision, there were subtle signs of patriotic protest in the parade. For example, one woman marching with the Girl Scouts wore a dress with an American flag design and peace sign earrings. She was carrying a small American flag, and written on her left arm were the words “Protect Women.” I saw this woman again later on as she was walking back from the end of the parade, and I noticed on her right arm was written, “I dissent.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948775298.jpg" alt="7.4.22_6 girlscouts1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="350" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948794672.jpg" alt="7.4.22_6 girlscouts2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="270" style="aspect-ratio: 0.47676056338028167"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">The phrase “I dissent” also appeared on a t-shirt worn by a man marching with the group that garnered the loudest applause from the crowd, called “Tosa Together.” Leading the group was a banner which stated, “Growing Equity &amp; Diversity in Wauwatosa.” Other t-shirt statements included “Black Lives Matter - Wauwatosa” and “AntiRacist – Wauwatosa.” In addition to an American flag were rainbow flags and a Black Lives Matter flag. One person carried a sign written in English, Spanish, and Arabic which said: “No matter where you are from, we’re glad you’re our neighbor.” Another group passed by later on similarly promoting LGBT equality, led by a vehicle with a rainbow sign that read, “Free Mom Hugs.”</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948869539.jpg" alt="7.4.22_7 inclusion1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="574" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3334997503744384"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948883672.jpg" alt="7.4.22_8 inclusion2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="579" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3339793281653747"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948901731.jpg" alt="7.4.22_9 inclusion3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="439" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948939745.jpg" alt="7.4.22_91 inclusion4.JPG" loading="lazy" width="441" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948917573.jpg" alt="7.4.22_92 inclusion5.JPG" loading="lazy" width="445" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>About halfway through the parade, two men were walking near the sidewalk, unaffiliated with the parade, handing out material to people. One of them was pushing a dolly with a box strapped to it which had a picture of the US on the front with a cross and the words, “This Great Nation was Founded on the GOSPEL of Jesus Christ.” On the side it read, “‘It is impossible to govern a nation without God and the Bible.’ - President George Washington.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948976029.jpg" alt="7.4.22_93 shalom1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="452" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948998482.jpg" alt="7.4.22_94 shalom2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="459" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p>On the outside of the folder that contained the materials was written, “US Presidents Declare Christianity” with a list of alleged quotes from former presidents to defend this statement. The other side included claims that the United States was founded solely on the basis of Christianity, and the inside included a plea for America to return to its “Christian heritage.” The envelope inside the folder indicated that the church distributing the materials was Shalom Baptist Church in Brown Deer (another Milwaukee suburb). Also printed in red letters on the envelope were statements condemning homosexuality and abortion. Inside the envelope contained a slew of pamphlets, including several Chick tracts, one of which stoked fears that Islam is overtaking the United States and will bring an end to Christianity in America if not stopped.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949033347.jpg" alt="7.4.22_95 shalom3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="324" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949054233.jpg" alt="7.4.22_96 shalom4.JPG" loading="lazy" width="321" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949071516.jpg" alt="7.4.22_97 shalom5.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949104887.jpg" alt="7.4.22_99 shalom7.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949120433.jpg" alt="7.4.22_98 shalom6.JPG" loading="lazy" width="321" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">While the representatives from Shalom Baptist Church had just inserted themselves into a gap in the parade, there were two churches that officially marched as participants in the parade. My aunt notified me early about the first group, suggesting to be on the lookout for “the Baptists” who always have the largest float in the parade. She was right. Before the arrival of the semi-truck carrying the float, were two women in their late teens wearing long skirts carrying a sign that said, “Big Dig,” which is the title of the upcoming Vacation Bible School at nearby Falls Baptist Church. The semi passed with loud and dramatic music playing, and on the semi was an archeological scene with several people on the float waving and an American flag in the center. As the float was driving past, I heard a man in the group next to us say, “Now that’s big church money.”</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949161078.jpg" alt="7.4.22_991 baptist1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="491" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949184628.jpg" alt="7.4.22_992 baptist2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="490" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">In comparison to the big money at Falls Baptist Church, the other church represented in the parade seemed pintsize. Wauwatosa Avenue United Methodist Church was one of the last organizations to march in the parade, carrying a sign that read, “Welcome Pastor Kate,” with children riding bikes adorned with flags. They handed out cards which provided information about the church, including that it is an inclusive “reconciling congregation” that welcomes all.</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949225979.jpg" alt="7.4.22_994 UMC2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="443" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949244024.jpg" alt="7.4.22_995 UMC3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="440" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The parade ended somewhat abruptly with a car that carried two local TV news anchors. My family and I were confused about whether or not it was over, and we all were in agreement that this year’s parade was rather underwhelming compared to pre-pandemic years.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Despite its small size, the 2022 Wauwatosa Fourth of July parade still had some big things to say about religion, patriotism, and ideology in a midwestern swing state. While Christian nationalism was on full display by those who unofficially inserted themselves into the lineup, it appears to have been excluded by those city volunteers who organized the parade. This ultimately regulated the messaging to represent Wauwatosa as an open and inclusive community, which countered the anti-abortion, anti-LGBTQIA+, and anti-Muslim sentiments included in materials from sideline entries like Shalom Baptist Church.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>While patriotism was everywhere, it seemed to carry different meanings for different organizations. For some it was pride in storied origins and an argument to return to better days, while for others it was pride in progress and fuel for positive change, and it was clear that Wauwatosa city leaders were driving for the latter. Still, I am left wondering about the effects of those who entered into the parade on the margins – did their messages of Christian nationalism appeal to, or even radicalize, any parade-goers who may have felt concerned about the declining attendance and rising progressive tone of this beloved annual tradition?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Sources&nbsp;</strong></p><p>“About Wauwatosa,” City of Wauwatosa, WI, accessed August 10, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.wauwatosa.net/discover-tosa/about-wauwatosa</a>.&nbsp;</p><p>“Election Results,” City of Wauwatosa, WI, accessed August 10, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.wauwatosa.net/government/departments/city-clerk/elections-voting/election-results</a>.&nbsp;</p><p>Allie Habeck, “Comparing How the City and Every Milwaukee County Suburb Voted in 2016 and 2020,” <em>Milwaukee Magazine</em>, November 4, 2020, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.milwaukeemag.com/comparing-how-the-city-and-every-milwaukee-county-suburb-voted-in-2016-and-2020/</a>.</p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	43.0604448	-88.03201469999999	North & Menomonee, Wauwatosa, WI 53226, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "Patriotism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "ChristianNationalism", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-04 14:00:21.269	2024-08-18 02:52:51.032	2026-04-15 13:48:36.969	\N
