uto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948571650.jpg" alt="7.4.22_1 crowd.JPG" loading="lazy" width="324" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Before the parade began, a cheery gentleman was walking along the sidewalk handing something out to every person he saw. It was a fake “million dollar bill,” and on the back it said “In God We Trust,” and a paragraph that begins with, “THE MILLION DOLLAR QUESTION: Will you go to Heaven when you die?...” The paragraph ends by directing readers to visit “<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">NeedGod.com</a>” and “<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">LivingWaters.com</a>.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948608094.jpg" alt="7.4.22_2 million.JPG" loading="lazy" width="331" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948625433.jpg" alt="7.4.22_3 million.JPG" loading="lazy" width="332" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The unofficial start to the parade was a group of Harley Davidson motorcycles with large American flags on the back. This was followed by a man and a woman who appeared to have jumped into a gap in the lineup carrying a sign that simply said “Jesus.” The man shouted, “Many signers of the Constitution were pastors who gave their lives and honor so that we could have our freedoms today. Trust in Jesus…” Hearing this, my aunt (a retired nurse) made a quick comment to me about how rights and freedoms were also taken away from indigenous people who had originally resided in the land; a comment that gave voice to the unexpressed tension of celebrating independence on stolen land named after its indigenous inhabitants.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948678303.jpg" alt="7.4.22_3.5 harley.JPG" loading="lazy" width="343" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948701974.jpg" alt="7.4.22_4 Jesus.JPG" loading="lazy" width="343" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The parade officially began with police motorcycles, a fire truck, and a float with Tripoli Shriner Veterans with about 10 American flags and a sign stating, “Honoring all Veterans” and another sign stating, “Please Stand For Your Flag.” Attendees pridefully stood and clapped. In full swing, the parade included high school bands, color guards, and dance teams; politicians; civic organizations; veterans’ organizations; businesses; and local media personalities.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948741086.jpg" alt="7.4.22_5 Shriners.JPG" loading="lazy" width="347" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Taking place just ten days after the overturning of Roe v. Wade in the Supreme Court Dobbs decision, there were subtle signs of patriotic protest in the parade. For example, one woman marching with the Girl Scouts wore a dress with an American flag design and peace sign earrings. She was carrying a small American flag, and written on her left arm were the words “Protect Women.” I saw this woman again later on as she was walking back from the end of the parade, and I noticed on her right arm was written, “I dissent.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948775298.jpg" alt="7.4.22_6 girlscouts1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="350" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948794672.jpg" alt="7.4.22_6 girlscouts2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="270" style="aspect-ratio: 0.47676056338028167"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">The phrase “I dissent” also appeared on a t-shirt worn by a man marching with the group that garnered the loudest applause from the crowd, called “Tosa Together.” Leading the group was a banner which stated, “Growing Equity &amp; Diversity in Wauwatosa.” Other t-shirt statements included “Black Lives Matter - Wauwatosa” and “AntiRacist – Wauwatosa.” In addition to an American flag were rainbow flags and a Black Lives Matter flag. One person carried a sign written in English, Spanish, and Arabic which said: “No matter where you are from, we’re glad you’re our neighbor.” Another group passed by later on similarly promoting LGBT equality, led by a vehicle with a rainbow sign that read, “Free Mom Hugs.”</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948869539.jpg" alt="7.4.22_7 inclusion1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="574" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3334997503744384"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948883672.jpg" alt="7.4.22_8 inclusion2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="579" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3339793281653747"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948901731.jpg" alt="7.4.22_9 inclusion3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="439" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948939745.jpg" alt="7.4.22_91 inclusion4.JPG" loading="lazy" width="441" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948917573.jpg" alt="7.4.22_92 inclusion5.JPG" loading="lazy" width="445" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>About halfway through the parade, two men were walking near the sidewalk, unaffiliated with the parade, handing out material to people. One of them was pushing a dolly with a box strapped to it which had a picture of the US on the front with a cross and the words, “This Great Nation was Founded on the GOSPEL of Jesus Christ.” On the side it read, “‘It is impossible to govern a nation without God and the Bible.’ - President George Washington.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948976029.jpg" alt="7.4.22_93 shalom1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="452" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948998482.jpg" alt="7.4.22_94 shalom2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="459" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p>On the outside of the folder that contained the materials was written, “US Presidents Declare Christianity” with a list of alleged quotes from former presidents to defend this statement. The other side included claims that the United States was founded solely on the basis of Christianity, and the inside included a plea for America to return to its “Christian heritage.” The envelope inside the folder indicated that the church distributing the materials was Shalom Baptist Church in Brown Deer (another Milwaukee suburb). Also printed in red letters on the envelope were statements condemning homosexuality and abortion. Inside the envelope contained a slew of pamphlets, including several Chick tracts, one of which stoked fears that Islam is overtaking the United States and will bring an end to Christianity in America if not stopped.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949033347.jpg" alt="7.4.22_95 shalom3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="324" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949054233.jpg" alt="7.4.22_96 shalom4.JPG" loading="lazy" width="321" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949071516.jpg" alt="7.4.22_97 shalom5.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949104887.jpg" alt="7.4.22_99 shalom7.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949120433.jpg" alt="7.4.22_98 shalom6.JPG" loading="lazy" width="321" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">While the representatives from Shalom Baptist Church had just inserted themselves into a gap in the parade, there were two churches that officially marched as participants in the parade. My aunt notified me early about the first group, suggesting to be on the lookout for “the Baptists” who always have the largest float in the parade. She was right. Before the arrival of the semi-truck carrying the float, were two women in their late teens wearing long skirts carrying a sign that said, “Big Dig,” which is the title of the upcoming Vacation Bible School at nearby Falls Baptist Church. The semi passed with loud and dramatic music playing, and on the semi was an archeological scene with several people on the float waving and an American flag in the center. As the float was driving past, I heard a man in the group next to us say, “Now that’s big church money.”</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949161078.jpg" alt="7.4.22_991 baptist1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="491" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949184628.jpg" alt="7.4.22_992 baptist2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="490" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">In comparison to the big money at Falls Baptist Church, the other church represented in the parade seemed pintsize. Wauwatosa Avenue United Methodist Church was one of the last organizations to march in the parade, carrying a sign that read, “Welcome Pastor Kate,” with children riding bikes adorned with flags. They handed out cards which provided information about the church, including that it is an inclusive “reconciling congregation” that welcomes all.</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949225979.jpg" alt="7.4.22_994 UMC2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="443" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949244024.jpg" alt="7.4.22_995 UMC3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="440" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The parade ended somewhat abruptly with a car that carried two local TV news anchors. My family and I were confused about whether or not it was over, and we all were in agreement that this year’s parade was rather underwhelming compared to pre-pandemic years.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Despite its small size, the 2022 Wauwatosa Fourth of July parade still had some big things to say about religion, patriotism, and ideology in a midwestern swing state. While Christian nationalism was on full display by those who unofficially inserted themselves into the lineup, it appears to have been excluded by those city volunteers who organized the parade. This ultimately regulated the messaging to represent Wauwatosa as an open and inclusive community, which countered the anti-abortion, anti-LGBTQIA+, and anti-Muslim sentiments included in materials from sideline entries like Shalom Baptist Church.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>While patriotism was everywhere, it seemed to carry different meanings for different organizations. For some it was pride in storied origins and an argument to return to better days, while for others it was pride in progress and fuel for positive change, and it was clear that Wauwatosa city leaders were driving for the latter. Still, I am left wondering about the effects of those who entered into the parade on the margins – did their messages of Christian nationalism appeal to, or even radicalize, any parade-goers who may have felt concerned about the declining attendance and rising progressive tone of this beloved annual tradition?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Sources&nbsp;</strong></p><p>“About Wauwatosa,” City of Wauwatosa, WI, accessed August 10, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.wauwatosa.net/discover-tosa/about-wauwatosa</a>.&nbsp;</p><p>“Election Results,” City of Wauwatosa, WI, accessed August 10, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.wauwatosa.net/government/departments/city-clerk/elections-voting/election-results</a>.&nbsp;</p><p>Allie Habeck, “Comparing How the City and Every Milwaukee County Suburb Voted in 2016 and 2020,” <em>Milwaukee Magazine</em>, November 4, 2020, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.milwaukeemag.com/comparing-how-the-city-and-every-milwaukee-county-suburb-voted-in-2016-and-2020/</a>.</p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	43.0604448	-88.03201469999999	North & Menomonee, Wauwatosa, WI 53226, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "Patriotism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "ChristianNationalism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Parade", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-04 14:00:21.269	2024-08-18 02:53:21.116	2024-11-22 20:05:32.144	\N
66c2a83970ac713135a76220	Wynwood Street Art	<p>Driving down I-95 in Miami, Florida, just two miles south of Little Haiti, it is nearly impossible to miss the now iconic image of three young people of color towering over the trendy Miami neighborhood of Wynwood. The mural entirely covers two adjacent walls of the Wynwood 25 building (a large apartment complex), and snakes around the corner, spilling onto a third wall. The scene is tranquil, as the two outside individuals are each pictured holding a rose with a halo-like ring of roses around their faces, and the third has his hands raised in a prayer-like position.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724031837037.jpg" alt="1Wyn_1 Saints.JPG" loading="lazy" width="456" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>This mural, one of the largest permanent murals in Wynwood, gives the passerby a small taste of what they would encounter if they exited the freeway and entered into this Instagrammable art district. Walking the streets of Wynwood can quickly bring about a feeling of sensory overload. The area smells of stale beer, cigarettes, weed, and street food; there is a constant chattering among drivers through the vocalization of their car horns; Latin and hip-hop music can be heard from every direction; and every inch of building, sidewalk, and light post is covered with murals, graffiti, stickers, and stencils.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724031883840.jpg" alt="1Wyn_2 streets.JPG" loading="lazy" width="445" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724031901998.jpg" alt="1Wyn_3 streets.JPG" loading="lazy" width="447" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724031932761.jpg" alt="1Wyn_4 streets.JPG" loading="lazy" width="335" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">The style and subject of the art varies, with many neutral murals seemingly commissioned by local business owners to add style to their buildings.</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032005371.jpg" alt="1Wyn_6 generic.JPG" loading="lazy" width="416" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032005373.jpg" alt="1Wyn_7 generic.JPG" loading="lazy" width="418" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032005376.jpg" alt="1Wyn_8 generic.JPG" loading="lazy" width="419" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>More overt religious imagery can be seen throughout the area, including a Bible verse accompanying the commemoration of a life lost, stencils of rosaries and doves, and a transmuting image that connects the concepts of protest and prayer.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032146968.jpg" alt="1Wyn_9 religion.JPG" loading="lazy" width="604" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032146970.jpg" alt="1Wyn_91 religion.JPG" loading="lazy" width="327" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032146973.jpg" alt="1Wyn_92 religion.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032146977.jpg" alt="1Wyn_93 religion.JPG" loading="lazy" width="475" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Overt cultural commentary is also often depicted, with critiques of consumerism, capitalism, and gun violence.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032229288.jpg" alt="1Wyn_94 critique.JPG" loading="lazy" width="516" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032229291.jpg" alt="1Wyn_95 critique.JPG" loading="lazy" width="518" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032229293.jpg" alt="1Wyn_96 critique.JPG" loading="lazy" width="519" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The artist who spraypainted the earlier-referenced iconic mural that beckons onlookers from I-95, El Mac, offers some insight into the mind of a Wynwood street artist in his <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://mac-arte.blogspot.com/2020/05/new-el-mac-murals-in-miami-love-supreme.html">blog post</a> written about the mural. The piece is titled “A Love Supreme (Wynwood Saints),” and the young folks in the mural depict three local figures who El Mac chose as expressive of the neighborhood, portraying “monumental, everyday saints—prayerful, resilient, and representative of upliftment, beauty, and loving kindness.”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>El Mac describes the act of art and painting as an act of prayer, and he states that he hopes his art can ultimately be a force for good. Further, El Mac states, “I strongly feel that the creation of art can be, at its best, a spiritual vocation, a means of responding to the world in service of truth, beauty, and goodness.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032536953.jpg" alt="1Wyn_97 saints.JPG" loading="lazy" width="687" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>El Mac stated that he wanted his mural to be “something representative of this place, its history, and its people, while conveying strength, dignity, balance, solidarity, and love.” El Mac wrote about how much Wynwood had changed since his first mural in the area back in 2007, but he stopped short of using the word that many critics have come to use to describe Wynwood – gentrification.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A quick glance at local news and editorial stories reveals a common narrative about Wynwood: What was once a largely Puerto Rican community known as “Little San Juan,” the area was hit by poverty and began to fall into disrepair. Taking advantage of the cheap building costs, artists began to settle in the area at the turn of the twenty-first century and galleries were assembled, forming a vibrant artistic community. Noticing the rising popularity of the area, developers swooped in, building luxury apartments and condos, and bringing in upscale restaurants and nightclubs. Rising rents have now priced out many of the area’s former inhabitants, including many artists, and Wynwood is now a popular stop on Miami bus tours, with upwards of $40 cover charges at many of its crowded bars and clubs.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032647969.jpg" alt="1Wyn_97.5 bus.JPG" loading="lazy" width="520" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3334997503744384"><p></p><p>There is a complex and uncomfortable relationship between street art and gentrification, particularly in the age of Instagram, which can quickly popularize sites of beauty, insight, and oddity. This brings a sort of irony to those murals critiquing capitalism, as the very existence of these murals plays a role in perpetuating gentrification, which exemplifies the capitalist impact on urban areas. The entanglement of art and capitalism, much like that of religion and capitalism, seems almost inescapable; despite occasional noble intentions, the outcomes of these intersections are often problematic and far-reaching.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>As for El Mac, it is interesting to note that his iconic Wynwood mural was commissioned by The Related Group – a Miami development company owned by Jorge Pérez, who is <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/17/arts/design/friends-no-more-jorge-perez-and-donald-trump.html">often called</a> the “luxury-condo king of Miami” and the “Donald Trump of the tropics.” Still, El Mac has stated his commitment to the Wynwood community. Although he does not use the term “gentrification” explicitly, in his blog post, he links a <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://vimeo.com/110682099">video</a> which documents the neighborhood's gentrification. Further, El Mac seems to offer a veiled acknowledgement of his own discomfort with his developer alliance and the irony of his mural’s role in Wynwood’s gentrification through his choice of quote to accompany the mural’s description, taken from Leonard Cohen’s novel, <em>Beautiful Losers</em>:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>"What is a saint? A saint is someone who has achieved a remote human possibility. It is impossible to say what that possibility is. I think it has something to do with the energy of love. Contact with this energy results in the exercise of a kind of balance in the chaos of existence. A saint does not dissolve the chaos; if he did the world would have changed long ago. I do not think that a saint dissolves the chaos even for himself, for there is something arrogant and warlike in the notion of a man setting the universe in order. It is a kind of balance that is his glory... Something in him so loves the world that he gives himself to the laws of gravity and chance. Far from flying with the angels, he traces with the fidelity of a seismograph needle the state of the solid bloody landscape."</em></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032787262.jpg" alt="1Wyn_98 saints.JPG" loading="lazy" width="359" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p><strong>Sources</strong></p><p>Álvarez, Camila and Natalie Edgar. “Right to Wynwood.” November 10, 2013. Miami: R17 and Meraki Media. Vimeo. <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://vimeo.com/110682099</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Drake, Carolina Ana. “Miami-Based Artists and Arts Organisations Grapple with Gentrification.” <em>The Art Newspaper</em>. December 8, 2023, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2023/12/08/miami-based-artists-and-arts-organisations-grapple-with-gentrification</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>El Mac. “New El Mac Murals in Miami: ‘A Love Supreme (Wynwood Saints).’” Blogspot. May 18, 2020, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://mac-arte.blogspot.com/2020/05/new-el-mac-murals-in-miami-love-supreme.html</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Pasols, Aaliyah. “Gentrification Complete: Will Wynwood's Progress Be Its Downfall?” <em>Miami New Times</em>. October 5, 2021, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.miaminewtimes.com/music/history-wynwood-miami-gentrification-13066720</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Patel, Shaan. “Wynwood, A Story of Instagram Gentrification.” <em>Miami Grid</em>. March 3, 2019, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://miami-grid.com/2019/03/03/wynwood-instagram-gentrification/</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Sokol, Brett. “Friends No More? Jorge Pérez and Donald Trump.” <em>The New York Times. </em>February 17, 2017, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/17/arts/design/friends-no-more-jorge-perez-and-donald-trump.html</a>.</p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	25.8006136	-80.1900876	212 NE 25th St #101, Miami, FL 33137, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "StreetArt", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Gentrification", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Saints", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-07 23:00:07.748	2024-08-19 02:04:41.257	2024-11-22 20:05:20.914	\N
66c2a817ee271ee786d831e1	Wynwood Street Art	<p>Driving down I-95 in Miami, Florida, just two miles south of Little Haiti, it is nearly impossible to miss the now iconic image of three young people of color towering over the trendy Miami neighborhood of Wynwood. The mural entirely covers two adjacent walls of the Wynwood 25 building (a large apartment complex), and snakes around the corner, spilling onto a third wall. The scene is tranquil, as the two outside individuals are each pictured holding a rose with a halo-like ring of roses around their faces, and the third has his hands raised in a prayer-like position.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724031837037.jpg" alt="1Wyn_1 Saints.JPG" loading="lazy" width="456" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>This mural, one of the largest permanent murals in Wynwood, gives the passerby a small taste of what they would encounter if they exited the freeway and entered into this Instagrammable art district. Walking the streets of Wynwood can quickly bring about a feeling of sensory overload. The area smells of stale beer, cigarettes, weed, and street food; there is a constant chattering among drivers through the vocalization of their car horns; Latin and hip-hop music can be heard from every direction; and every inch of building, sidewalk, and light post is covered with murals, graffiti, stickers, and stencils.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724031883840.jpg" alt="1Wyn_2 streets.JPG" loading="lazy" width="445" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724031901998.jpg" alt="1Wyn_3 streets.JPG" loading="lazy" width="447" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724031932761.jpg" alt="1Wyn_4 streets.JPG" loading="lazy" width="335" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p><span style="font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">The style and subject of the art varies, with many neutral murals seemingly commissioned by local business owners to add style to their buildings.</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032005371.jpg" alt="1Wyn_6 generic.JPG" loading="lazy" width="416" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032005373.jpg" alt="1Wyn_7 generic.JPG" loading="lazy" width="418" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032005376.jpg" alt="1Wyn_8 generic.JPG" loading="lazy" width="419" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>More overt religious imagery can be seen throughout the area, including a Bible verse accompanying the commemoration of a life lost, stencils of rosaries and doves, and a transmuting image that connects the concepts of protest and prayer.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032146968.jpg" alt="1Wyn_9 religion.JPG" loading="lazy" width="604" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032146970.jpg" alt="1Wyn_91 religion.JPG" loading="lazy" width="327" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032146973.jpg" alt="1Wyn_92 religion.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032146977.jpg" alt="1Wyn_93 religion.JPG" loading="lazy" width="475" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Overt cultural commentary is also often depicted, with critiques of consumerism, capitalism, and gun violence.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032229288.jpg" alt="1Wyn_94 critique.JPG" loading="lazy" width="516" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032229291.jpg" alt="1Wyn_95 critique.JPG" loading="lazy" width="518" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032229293.jpg" alt="1Wyn_96 critique.JPG" loading="lazy" width="519" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The artist who spraypainted the earlier-referenced iconic mural that beckons onlookers from I-95, El Mac, offers some insight into the mind of a Wynwood street artist in his <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://mac-arte.blogspot.com/2020/05/new-el-mac-murals-in-miami-love-supreme.html">blog post</a> written about the mural. The piece is titled “A Love Supreme (Wynwood Saints),” and the young folks in the mural depict three local figures who El Mac chose as expressive of the neighborhood, portraying “monumental, everyday saints—prayerful, resilient, and representative of upliftment, beauty, and loving kindness.”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>El Mac describes the act of art and painting as an act of prayer, and he states that he hopes his art can ultimately be a force for good. Further, El Mac states, “I strongly feel that the creation of art can be, at its best, a spiritual vocation, a means of responding to the world in service of truth, beauty, and goodness.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032536953.jpg" alt="1Wyn_97 saints.JPG" loading="lazy" width="687" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>El Mac stated that he wanted his mural to be “something representative of this place, its history, and its people, while conveying strength, dignity, balance, solidarity, and love.” El Mac wrote about how much Wynwood had changed since his first mural in the area back in 2007, but he stopped short of using the word that many critics have come to use to describe Wynwood – gentrification.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>A quick glance at local news and editorial stories reveals a common narrative about Wynwood: What was once a largely Puerto Rican community known as “Little San Juan,” the area was hit by poverty and began to fall into disrepair. Taking advantage of the cheap building costs, artists began to settle in the area at the turn of the twenty-first century and galleries were assembled, forming a vibrant artistic community. Noticing the rising popularity of the area, developers swooped in, building luxury apartments and condos, and bringing in upscale restaurants and nightclubs. Rising rents have now priced out many of the area’s former inhabitants, including many artists, and Wynwood is now a popular stop on Miami bus tours, with upwards of $40 cover charges at many of its crowded bars and clubs.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032647969.jpg" alt="1Wyn_97.5 bus.JPG" loading="lazy" width="520" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3334997503744384"><p></p><p>There is a complex and uncomfortable relationship between street art and gentrification, particularly in the age of Instagram, which can quickly popularize sites of beauty, insight, and oddity. This brings a sort of irony to those murals critiquing capitalism, as the very existence of these murals plays a role in perpetuating gentrification, which exemplifies the capitalist impact on urban areas. The entanglement of art and capitalism, much like that of religion and capitalism, seems almost inescapable; despite occasional noble intentions, the outcomes of these intersections are often problematic and far-reaching.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>As for El Mac, it is interesting to note that his iconic Wynwood mural was commissioned by The Related Group – a Miami development company owned by Jorge Pérez, who is <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/17/arts/design/friends-no-more-jorge-perez-and-donald-trump.html">often called</a> the “luxury-condo king of Miami” and the “Donald Trump of the tropics.” Still, El Mac has stated his commitment to the Wynwood community. Although he does not use the term “gentrification” explicitly, in his blog post, he links a <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://vimeo.com/110682099">video</a> which documents the neighborhood's gentrification. Further, El Mac seems to offer a veiled acknowledgement of his own discomfort with his developer alliance and the irony of his mural’s role in Wynwood’s gentrification through his choice of quote to accompany the mural’s description, taken from Leonard Cohen’s novel, <em>Beautiful Losers</em>:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><em>"What is a saint? A saint is someone who has achieved a remote human possibility. It is impossible to say what that possibility is. I think it has something to do with the energy of love. Contact with this energy results in the exercise of a kind of balance in the chaos of existence. A saint does not dissolve the chaos; if he did the world would have changed long ago. I do not think that a saint dissolves the chaos even for himself, for there is something arrogant and warlike in the notion of a man setting the universe in order. It is a kind of balance that is his glory... Something in him so loves the world that he gives himself to the laws of gravity and chance. Far from flying with the angels, he traces with the fidelity of a seismograph needle the state of the solid bloody landscape."</em></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1724032787262.jpg" alt="1Wyn_98 saints.JPG" loading="lazy" width="359" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p><strong>Sources</strong></p><p>Álvarez, Camila and Natalie Edgar. “Right to Wynwood.” November 10, 2013. Miami: R17 and Meraki Media. Vimeo. <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://vimeo.com/110682099</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Drake, Carolina Ana. “Miami-Based Artists and Arts Organisations Grapple with Gentrification.” <em>The Art Newspaper</em>. December 8, 2023, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2023/12/08/miami-based-artists-and-arts-organisations-grapple-with-gentrification</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>El Mac. “New El Mac Murals in Miami: ‘A Love Supreme (Wynwood Saints).’” Blogspot. May 18, 2020, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://mac-arte.blogspot.com/2020/05/new-el-mac-murals-in-miami-love-supreme.html</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Pasols, Aaliyah. “Gentrification Complete: Will Wynwood's Progress Be Its Downfall?” <em>Miami New Times</em>. October 5, 2021, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.miaminewtimes.com/music/history-wynwood-miami-gentrification-13066720</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Patel, Shaan. “Wynwood, A Story of Instagram Gentrification.” <em>Miami Grid</em>. March 3, 2019, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://miami-grid.com/2019/03/03/wynwood-instagram-gentrification/</a>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Sokol, Brett. “Friends No More? Jorge Pérez and Donald Trump.” <em>The New York Times. </em>February 17, 2017, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/17/arts/design/friends-no-more-jorge-perez-and-donald-trump.html</a>.</p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	25.8006136	-80.1900876	212 NE 25th St #101, Miami, FL 33137, USA	t	f	f	[]	2022-07-07 23:00:07.748	2024-08-19 02:04:07.769	2026-04-15 13:48:37.011	\N
66c16296ae8382984e15126f	4th of July Parade, 2022 	<p>For nearly 40 years, breaking only for the pandemic, members of my extended family have been gathering with food and drinks at the North Avenue bridge over the Menomonee River to celebrate and watch the Fourth of July Parade in Wauwatosa, Wisconsin. “Tosa,” as it is familiarly called, is a <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.wauwatosa.net/discover-tosa/about-wauwatosa">predominately white middle-class suburb</a> of Milwaukee (81.4% of residents are “non-Hispanic White”). As Wisconsin’s 14<sup>th</sup> largest city, Wauwatosa is relatively younger (median age of 37.9 years) and more educated (58.6% of adults hold a bachelor’s degree or higher) than both the state and national averages. Although Wisconsin is politically considered to be a “swing state,” like <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.milwaukeemag.com/comparing-how-the-city-and-every-milwaukee-county-suburb-voted-in-2016-and-2020/">most of the cities in Milwaukee County</a>, the majority of Wauwatosa residents reliably vote democrat in both <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.wauwatosa.net/government/departments/city-clerk/elections-voting/election-results">local and national elections</a>, with at least one-third still solidly republican. &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>While the mood was jubilant and celebratory, the crowd was considerably more sparse in 2022 than those from my childhood days, when designated family members would have to camp out for hours to secure our coveted spot on the North Avenue bridge. In contrast, this year parking was ample and good parade-watching spots were plentiful, as the crowd was too thin to fill both sides of the street. Still, those in attendance were decked out in patriotic gear as many united with friends and family of all ages alongside their bloody mary bars and tables of food.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948571650.jpg" alt="7.4.22_1 crowd.JPG" loading="lazy" width="324" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>Before the parade began, a cheery gentleman was walking along the sidewalk handing something out to every person he saw. It was a fake “million dollar bill,” and on the back it said “In God We Trust,” and a paragraph that begins with, “THE MILLION DOLLAR QUESTION: Will you go to Heaven when you die?...” The paragraph ends by directing readers to visit “<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">NeedGod.com</a>” and “<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">LivingWaters.com</a>.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948608094.jpg" alt="7.4.22_2 million.JPG" loading="lazy" width="331" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948625433.jpg" alt="7.4.22_3 million.JPG" loading="lazy" width="332" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The unofficial start to the parade was a group of Harley Davidson motorcycles with large American flags on the back. This was followed by a man and a woman who appeared to have jumped into a gap in the lineup carrying a sign that simply said “Jesus.” The man shouted, “Many signers of the Constitution were pastors who gave their lives and honor so that we could have our freedoms today. Trust in Jesus…” Hearing this, my aunt (a retired nurse) made a quick comment to me about how rights and freedoms were also taken away from indigenous people who had originally resided in the land; a comment that gave voice to the unexpressed tension of celebrating independence on stolen land named after its indigenous inhabitants.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948678303.jpg" alt="7.4.22_3.5 harley.JPG" loading="lazy" width="343" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948701974.jpg" alt="7.4.22_4 Jesus.JPG" loading="lazy" width="343" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The parade officially began with police motorcycles, a fire truck, and a float with Tripoli Shriner Veterans with about 10 American flags and a sign stating, “Honoring all Veterans” and another sign stating, “Please Stand For Your Flag.” Attendees pridefully stood and clapped. In full swing, the parade included high school bands, color guards, and dance teams; politicians; civic organizations; veterans’ organizations; businesses; and local media personalities.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948741086.jpg" alt="7.4.22_5 Shriners.JPG" loading="lazy" width="347" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>Taking place just ten days after the overturning of Roe v. Wade in the Supreme Court Dobbs decision, there were subtle signs of patriotic protest in the parade. For example, one woman marching with the Girl Scouts wore a dress with an American flag design and peace sign earrings. She was carrying a small American flag, and written on her left arm were the words “Protect Women.” I saw this woman again later on as she was walking back from the end of the parade, and I noticed on her right arm was written, “I dissent.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948775298.jpg" alt="7.4.22_6 girlscouts1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="350" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948794672.jpg" alt="7.4.22_6 girlscouts2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="270" style="aspect-ratio: 0.476761 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">The phrase “I dissent” also appeared on a t-shirt worn by a man marching with the group that garnered the loudest applause from the crowd, called “Tosa Together.” Leading the group was a banner which stated, “Growing Equity &amp; Diversity in Wauwatosa.” Other t-shirt statements included “Black Lives Matter - Wauwatosa” and “AntiRacist – Wauwatosa.” In addition to an American flag were rainbow flags and a Black Lives Matter flag. One person carried a sign written in English, Spanish, and Arabic which said: “No matter where you are from, we’re glad you’re our neighbor.” Another group passed by later on similarly promoting LGBT equality, led by a vehicle with a rainbow sign that read, “Free Mom Hugs.”</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948869539.jpg" alt="7.4.22_7 inclusion1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="574" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3335 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948883672.jpg" alt="7.4.22_8 inclusion2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="579" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33398 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948901731.jpg" alt="7.4.22_9 inclusion3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="439" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948939745.jpg" alt="7.4.22_91 inclusion4.JPG" loading="lazy" width="441" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948917573.jpg" alt="7.4.22_92 inclusion5.JPG" loading="lazy" width="445" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>About halfway through the parade, two men were walking near the sidewalk, unaffiliated with the parade, handing out material to people. One of them was pushing a dolly with a box strapped to it which had a picture of the US on the front with a cross and the words, “This Great Nation was Founded on the GOSPEL of Jesus Christ.” On the side it read, “‘It is impossible to govern a nation without God and the Bible.’ - President George Washington.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948976029.jpg" alt="7.4.22_93 shalom1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="452" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723948998482.jpg" alt="7.4.22_94 shalom2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="459" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><p>On the outside of the folder that contained the materials was written, “US Presidents Declare Christianity” with a list of alleged quotes from former presidents to defend this statement. The other side included claims that the United States was founded solely on the basis of Christianity, and the inside included a plea for America to return to its “Christian heritage.” The envelope inside the folder indicated that the church distributing the materials was Shalom Baptist Church in Brown Deer (another Milwaukee suburb). Also printed in red letters on the envelope were statements condemning homosexuality and abortion. Inside the envelope contained a slew of pamphlets, including several Chick tracts, one of which stoked fears that Islam is overtaking the United States and will bring an end to Christianity in America if not stopped.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949033347.jpg" alt="7.4.22_95 shalom3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="324" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949054233.jpg" alt="7.4.22_96 shalom4.JPG" loading="lazy" width="321" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949071516.jpg" alt="7.4.22_97 shalom5.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949104887.jpg" alt="7.4.22_99 shalom7.JPG" loading="lazy" width="320" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949120433.jpg" alt="7.4.22_98 shalom6.JPG" loading="lazy" width="321" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">While the representatives from Shalom Baptist Church had just inserted themselves into a gap in the parade, there were two churches that officially marched as participants in the parade. My aunt notified me early about the first group, suggesting to be on the lookout for “the Baptists” who always have the largest float in the parade. She was right. Before the arrival of the semi-truck carrying the float, were two women in their late teens wearing long skirts carrying a sign that said, “Big Dig,” which is the title of the upcoming Vacation Bible School at nearby Falls Baptist Church. The semi passed with loud and dramatic music playing, and on the semi was an archeological scene with several people on the float waving and an American flag in the center. As the float was driving past, I heard a man in the group next to us say, “Now that’s big church money.”</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949161078.jpg" alt="7.4.22_991 baptist1.JPG" loading="lazy" width="491" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949184628.jpg" alt="7.4.22_992 baptist2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="490" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 12pt">In comparison to the big money at Falls Baptist Church, the other church represented in the parade seemed pintsize. Wauwatosa Avenue United Methodist Church was one of the last organizations to march in the parade, carrying a sign that read, “Welcome Pastor Kate,” with children riding bikes adorned with flags. They handed out cards which provided information about the church, including that it is an inclusive “reconciling congregation” that welcomes all.</span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949225979.jpg" alt="7.4.22_994 UMC2.JPG" loading="lazy" width="443" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949244024.jpg" alt="7.4.22_995 UMC3.JPG" loading="lazy" width="440" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The parade ended somewhat abruptly with a car that carried two local TV news anchors. My family and I were confused about whether or not it was over, and we all were in agreement that this year’s parade was rather underwhelming compared to pre-pandemic years.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Despite its small size, the 2022 Wauwatosa Fourth of July parade still had some big things to say about religion, patriotism, and ideology in a midwestern swing state. While Christian nationalism was on full display by those who unofficially inserted themselves into the lineup, it appears to have been excluded by those city volunteers who organized the parade. This ultimately regulated the messaging to represent Wauwatosa as an open and inclusive community, which countered the anti-abortion, anti-LGBTQIA+, and anti-Muslim sentiments included in materials from sideline entries like Shalom Baptist Church.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>While patriotism was everywhere, it seemed to carry different meanings for different organizations. For some it was pride in storied origins and an argument to return to better days, while for others it was pride in progress and fuel for positive change, and it was clear that Wauwatosa city leaders were driving for the latter. Still, I am left wondering about the effects of those who entered into the parade on the margins – did their messages of Christian nationalism appeal to, or even radicalize, any parade-goers who may have felt concerned about the declining attendance and rising progressive tone of this beloved annual tradition?</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Sources&nbsp;</strong></p><p>“About Wauwatosa,” City of Wauwatosa, WI, accessed August 10, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.wauwatosa.net/discover-tosa/about-wauwatosa</a>.&nbsp;</p><p>“Election Results,” City of Wauwatosa, WI, accessed August 10, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.wauwatosa.net/government/departments/city-clerk/elections-voting/election-results</a>.&nbsp;</p><p>Allie Habeck, “Comparing How the City and Every Milwaukee County Suburb Voted in 2016 and 2020,” <em>Milwaukee Magazine</em>, November 4, 2020, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.milwaukeemag.com/comparing-how-the-city-and-every-milwaukee-county-suburb-voted-in-2016-and-2020/</a>.</p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1723949644457.mp4">Video 1</a></p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	43.0604448	-88.03201469999999	North & Menomonee, Wauwatosa, WI 53226, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "Patriotism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "ChristianNationalism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Parade", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-04 14:00:21.269	2024-08-18 02:55:18.07	2024-11-22 20:03:45.987	\N
