677c389d9552c3a3b8321133	Council Oak (Mvskoke)	<p><span style="font-size: 18px"><strong>Mvskoke Council Oak</strong></span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736186734780.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="600" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>The Council Oak in Tulsa, Oklahoma is a sacred site to the Mvskoke (Muscogee Creek) people. The Mvskoke were forced to relocate to what is now Oklahoma from their ancestral lands in the southeastern United States during the early 19th century through the violent tactics of the US Government remembered as the Trail of Tears. This site along the Arkansas River was established as sacred grounds by the Locv Pokv (Locapoga) tribal town of the Mvskoke in 1836. During the twentieth century, the site became incorporated into Tulsa, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.muscogeenation.com/2018/10/24/reflects-arrival-to-indian-territory-history-of-council-oak-tree/">a city whose name derives from the Mvskoke word Tvlvhasse, meaning "old town</a>, and was added to the Register of National Historical Places in 1976. During the November 2024 annual commemoration of Council Oak Tree Day, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.mvskokemedia.com/council-oak-tree-to-be-transferred-to-the-muscogee-creek-nation/">the City of Tulsa declared an intention</a> to return governance of the site to the Muscogee Creek Nation.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736188777388.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="609" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>The Council Oak Tree is now fully enmeshed in the cityscape of Tulsa, Oklahoma, a fixture in the Riverview residential neighborhood and adjacent to the downtown commercial district.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736192917351.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="650" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>The Council Oak sits atop a hill to the east of the Arkansas River, which is just barely visible here through the development along 18th Street and Riverside Drive. </p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736188952736.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="605" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>On the grounds of the Council Oak is a monument to the Mvskoke peoples who endured forced relocation and those who died from the brutal conditions en route from Alabama to Indian Territory.</p><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 18px"><strong>"Morning Prayer" Monument</strong></span></p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736189756019.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="611" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><p>The monument is entitled "Morning Prayer" and was <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://tulsahomeforsale.net/creek-nation-council-oak-park-1750-south-cheyenne-avenue-tulsa-oklahoma-midtown-tulsa/">commissioned by the Muscogee Creek Nation</a> and completed in 2009. "Morning Prayer" was designed by Richard Thornton, a Yuchi-Creek, and sculpted and installed by Dan Brook, also Creek. The monument features a stylized flame that symbolizes the sacred fires brought from ancestral lands to Indian Territory that were used to sacralize the site of the Council Oak and create continuity through the ritual life of survivors and their descendants. It also includes text panels that tell the story of forced migration, resettlement, and the rituals that animate this site.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736190349074.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="627" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><p>Panel text: "Each year at Council Oak we celebrate the historic meeting held here after the United States Government removal to re-establish our tribal government. We remember the struggles and celebrate the successes of our Nation. We honor those tribal towns and all Mvskoke men and women who perpetuate the traditional ways and keep the ceremonial fires burning."</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736190546765.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="627" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><p>Panel text: "Assimilation into the dominant society, U.S. Government policies, and the loss of elders knowing the old ways contributed to the extinction of many tribal towns. Of the 44 original tribal towns removed, only a few Mvskoke and Euchee tribal grounds actively continue with ancient ceremonies. These remaining ceremonial grounds keep our sacred fires 'alive' to this day."</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736190891116.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="635" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p>Panel text: "Fire is a revered element of many sacred rituals of the Mvskoke (Muscogee Creek People). The sacred fire represents the divine masculine of the 'Epofvnkv' (the Creator) to which all things are connected."</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736191041053.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="857" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p>Text panel: "During the rituals, dances and prayers, the smoke rises from the sacred fires and it fades into the skies. The smoke carries the messages of the Mvskoke people to the Creator. These ceremonies are not primarily for personal benefit, but rather the benefit of the entire community or nation."</p><p></p><p>Text panel: "The sacred fires are built with logs pointing to the east, west, north and south; the four cardinal directions. This is the basis of many traditional Mvskoke concepts. The earliest Mvskoke teachings instruct that our sacred fires are to burn eternally. If extinguished, the Mvskoke will perish."</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736192529089.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="661" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p>Text panel: "By 1834, our forefathers were forced to walk to the Indian Territory (Oklahoma) on what is known today as the Trail of Tears from our ancestral tribal lands in the southeastern United States. However, our Mvskoke leaders ensured that our traditional legacy was continued and protected here."</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736192782521.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="663" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p>Text panel: "The sacred fires were integral to the religious, political and social systems long before the first European contact. The ashes of our ceremonial fires from each of the tribal towns were carried over the long march to be rekindled here."</p>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	36.1361908	-95.9899502	Creek Nation Council Oak Park, 1750 S Cheyenne Ave, Tulsa, OK 74119, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "Muscogee", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Creek", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "indigenous", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "streetscape", "origin": "user"}]	2025-01-03 17:58:54.102	2025-01-06 20:10:05.443	2025-04-10 17:13:48.065	\N
67816d639ab7f3dd87966881	Hobby Lobby Religion	<p>Hobby Lobby is a well-known home decor store. This store is in almost all states and has been extremely successful in their business. David Green and his family are the founders of Hobby Lobby and are devout Christians who continued their beliefs into their work. Before walking in, I noticed that this store is in a pretty busy strip mall. There is a grocery store, restaurants, and a consignment store nearby that are quite busy. The first thing I noticed when I walked in was the hours placed on the door. It states that they are open Monday-Saturday and are off on Sunday to give their workers a break to spend with family and worship. I had never read their hours before, so I was quite shocked when I saw this. I knew they were closed on Sundays but did not know they said, "Give time for employees to worship" on their doors.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736535302696.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>After I entered the store, I noticed how friendly the workers are. They welcomed me and asked me if I needed help finding anything. I saw many individuals in the store, most being white middle-aged women. Most were shopping alone and taking their time in each aisle, slowly viewing all the items the store had. There were many workers in the store who were walking around and making sure everything was tidy, and all customers were finding what they needed. I walked around the store for a little while until I came across my first piece of Christian decor. It was a small piece of wall art, most likely for a children's bedroom, that stated, "God is within her, she will not fall." This is a commonly known Bible verse in the Christian community and is used frequently for decor, Bible covers, motivational quotes, etc. This specific verse is not quoted on the art, but as a Christian myself, I noticed it from the Bible and knew it was a verse. </p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736535319280.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>The next piece of wall art I encountered was another Bible verse: "She is clothed with strength and dignity, and she laughs without fear of the future. Proverbs 31:25." This piece seemed more for adults than the first piece did since it was white and tan.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736535335131.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>After leaving the wall art section, I came across a small shelf of crosses, all varying in color, size, and shape. Most of them had Bible verses on them, and some didn't. The ones with Bible verses were mostly stating values each believer should have, such as hope, faith, trust, and love.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736535350093.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>Lastly, I came across a section of devotionals and Bible's. I was not expecting to see these since this was a home decor store, but they had a pretty significant collection of them. These devotionals ranged from Jesus Calling, which is an extremely common devotional book in the Christian community, to Walking with God. The books were very pretty and varied in color and size.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736535364874.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>All in all, Hobby Lobby utilizes their religion to expand their merchandise. They have opened up many doors by using religion in their merchandise, such as selling Bible's and other religious home decor like the wall art. I talked to some employees, and they said that the corporation is devoted to serving their community and ensuring individuals have access to home decor that relates to the Christian religion. The store was not very homey, though. I was expecting a very cozy and warm environment, but the bright fluorescent lights and busy crowd did not allow that to happen; however, the workers were extremely friendly and helpful. Hobby Lobby shows how common the Christian religion is here in Saint Louis, especially in the suburbs. The store was busy and many people were viewing the religious items the store had.</p>	eNlFMkamG3dhAy0n4WcBAcZECOm2	38.5945441	-90.5635606	15425 Manchester Rd, Ballwin, MO 63011, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "store", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Christian", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "decor", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "home", "origin": "user"}]	2025-01-09 18:13:08.769	2025-01-10 18:56:35.548	2025-01-11 03:08:39.437	\N
660da3df1507945c2a4c32bc	Muslim Women Group Excercise Flyer	<div>A flyer on <span style="font-size: 1em;">a public bulletin board in the D Wing of Wells Hall at Michigan State University advertising a Muslim Women Group Excercise event on campus.</span><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1712169928552.jpg" style="font-size: 1em; max-width: 50%; height: auto;"><span style="font-size: 1em;">&nbsp;</span></div><br>	6ZqvcIG3sLQzpuExn7UsB1QWDQh2	42.7265386423494	-84.48186172180294	Wells Hall, 734 Red Cedar Rd, East Lansing, MI 48824, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "MSU", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Islam", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Flyer", "origin": "user"}]	2024-04-03 18:40:25.268	2024-04-03 18:45:51.927	2024-06-21 21:25:27.337	\N
6781eb471132ef2b3c8f1d1c	St. Camillus Parish 	<p>St. Camillus Parish is located in Silver Spring Maryland within a very diverse neighborhood this is seen in the congregation who attend the masses. I attended the multicultural Christmas Eve mass. This mass was very powerful as many different cultures can come and enjoy mass as one congregation. This diversity allows for there to be no boundaries of entry to attend mass. St. Camillus Parish is very large with brick walls and dim lighting. There is a small entrance which has double doors where there were many greeters waiting to hand out programs. The congregation is made up of all walks of people those stems from the diverse surrounding neighborhood where many immigrants have settled. There was some talking before and after the service but mostly joyous celebration as Christmas was coming the next morning. There were different languages spoken before and after mass between families and friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The mass was in three different languages this entailed three different homilies all done in their respective language and music done in all three languages. This provides a sense of belonging within a diverse group all connecting by their religious beliefs. This sense of community stems from everyone respecting the other languages as they all have their moments of practice. St. Camillus Parish is also made up of a lot of families with little kids who were full of excitement for the Christmas morning to come. This was seen as after the service many kids could be seen yelling and running back to their cars in order to get home for bed.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; St. Camillus Parish does a wonderful job making all feel welcome I think this stems from the congregation which is made up of many different ways of life. There is someone from all walks of life with their own life experiences, but the Parish provides a place where all can come and enjoy service as one. I talked to the older lady next to me in the middle of the service as she was trying to ask me a question about the order of the homilies. This conversation took a moment due to her only speaking French. I took French for many years through high school which helped me slowly explain the order. This gave me a sense of belonging in the church as the older lady trusted she could turn to her side and ask me a question.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Reflecting back upon my time at the church I believe that if I were looking for a regular church to attend, I would give a lot of consideration to St. Camillus Parish as the community is unmatched. The church gives off a welcoming feeling to all who step through the door no matter who they are.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736567585231.jpg"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736567598225.jpg"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736567614678.jpg"><p><br></p>	b4GD3PejFOXBgLEzgOBazwi5uyg2	\N	\N	\N	t	f	f	[]	2025-01-11 03:53:42.929	2025-01-11 03:53:43.642	2025-01-11 03:53:51.295	\N
