6781f1d09ab7f3dd87966886	Penn State Basketball	<p>The Penn State basketball games are a huge attraction for families and students of Penn State. This is a place where many come together from all walks of life to support the same team. This is a place where it does not matter who you are or where you’re from as long as you are supporting Penn State. There is a sense of belonging to the community as all are cheering in their own way for Penn State to win. Every fan is watching waiting expectantly for a big play. This event brings people together who would never cross paths. The seating force you to sit next to someone you would never get to meet anywhere else. The fan to fan interactions come from a mutual excitement over the game and a desire for the team to well. There is a supportive culture where there is a common goal of victory. After the game I reflected upon the moments I had shared with others. I had watched as people celebrated together and met new people through high fives. This support for a school team brings many different cultures together allowing for common ground to be found.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736569292907.jpg"><p><br></p>	b4GD3PejFOXBgLEzgOBazwi5uyg2	38.95743188766581	-77.0758211997844	3808 Huntington St NW, Washington, DC 20015, USA	t	f	f	[]	2025-01-11 04:21:36.308	2025-01-11 04:21:36.817	2025-01-11 04:21:51.06	\N
6781edbf7128162601586a27	President Carters Lying in State 	<p>President Carters passing crossed many Americans news the past week. This provided an interesting opportunity to go to the Capital Building to President Carters Lying in State. This was a very mournful event, when waiting in line there was an air of respect with many talking very quietly or not talking at all. When inside the capital there was no photography allowed and no phones were out. All kept their eyes low and many said a prayer or took a knee. These prayers were all a private moment between the prayer and God wishing the best for former President Carter. The respectful mourners all kept former president Carter in their prayers in their respective religions. The prayers were all meaningful holding value within every religion. There was no visible judging for how people mourned the formers presidents passing. There was respect for all in a moment which was bigger than any singular person. After I left the hall I had a deep appreciation for the people around me as we had all gone to pay respects to a man none of us ever met and this was all done in each persons personal way.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736568246107.jpg"><p><br></p>	b4GD3PejFOXBgLEzgOBazwi5uyg2	38.95743188766581	-77.0758211997844	3808 Huntington St NW, Washington, DC 20015, USA	t	f	f	[]	2025-01-11 04:04:14.607	2025-01-11 04:04:15.085	2025-01-11 04:04:30.556	\N
