68269b1bcfd83da30865d4df	Statuary in Colored Marbles for the New Cathedral- The Cathedral Basilica of St. Louis	<img height="auto" src="https://lh7-rt.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXeqNm-hFpjcTnNu86xB6AahfYsa7jXbqJfBVg9vycapuUdN2OYp43sU2hzZk1HQTHhGVqlCHoMhmWFs2Rh0PDvOMq7lo-YBJOVgwaX5zpWw1pZLogVa3EApkhn74n7UXxjVeqWbNw?key=3MsIV_IskFe34Z10TxtHMZmP" loading="lazy" width="391" style="aspect-ratio: 0.623125"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">This small newspaper article highlights four statues being made in Italy for the cathedral. The statues include two adorning angels, Mary, and St. John. George D. Barnett, the head architect, had the sculptor illustrate these statues under his direction.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Title: Statuary in Colored Marbles for the New Cathedral</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Creator: Unknown</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Publisher: The Post-Dispatch St. Louis, MO</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Contributor: Maura Hill&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Date: September 19th, 1915</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Rights: Uncertain?</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Source: </span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="http://Newspaper.com"><span style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt"><u>Newspaper.com</u></span></a><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">, The Post-Dispatch St. Louis, MO</span></p>	zHwslkIPrdaiaeuXvaSbHh0JIHt1	\N	\N	