695de9a6e501b2dec4d9df53	Holy Trinity Serbian Chruch	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767763727084.jpg" alt="Image description" width="130" style="aspect-ratio: 0.849437 / 1;"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt;"><strong>So, this is my church, 1/6 we held our evening service we call Vespers, for Christmas Eve. I wanted to show the altar and the various iconography which may differ from a lot of other churches. One can see the tree in the corner which we bless the branches of and burn to symbolize the fire keeping Jesus warm as a baby in the manger, the tree of life, and the cross. All this happens in preparation for Nativity. One may see the Priest talking to fellow parishioners which may have a different set up than some churches with traditional attire for these big events and holidays. Here the culture is definitely Serbian so the majority of white people with some people of color attending as well. In this picture you can only see men but there are many women as well. Since he does a lot of the songs and liturgies in Serbian being American that only speaks English may be difficult to follow along with, but he does try and do his speeches at the end of Liturgy in English and Serbian. Looking at these photos, I get a bit nostalgic of occasionally coming here for the holidays as a child, but now I more so feel at home and at peace being with this amazing, loving community. I often wait until after service and just sit there in the presence of the icons symbolic of the Trinity, Jesus, Mary, the Saints, etc. Doing so gives me such clarity and peace that surpasses understanding. I sit and spend time there feeling very reflective in prayer, in and out of Liturgy. I would say it is very small in relation to the amount of people that come during the holidays. There are lines out the door of people coming for Badnjak (branches that the priest blessed). I also would say that if you're unfamiliar with the church and come during a holiday you may be shocked with how many people are there so I might recommend you find a person to stand or sit with or to come during the normal Liturgical services first. This church is very religious and is important to not only members of the church, but to the entire Serbian population here in St. Louis. There aren't as many Serbians as there are Bosnians here and so having this Church where everyone gets together to catch up and meet is extremely important and vital in preserving our culture here. Also this being the only Eastern Orthodox Serbian Church in Missouri makes it a necessary place for this people group to worship and get together!</strong></span></p><p></p>	ELmnJCsV4WelPIrBXROIBvydYW02	38.6114111	-90.21673419999999	1910 Serbian Dr, St. Louis, MO 63104, USA	f	t	f	[]	2026-01-07 05:05:03.165	2026-01-07 05:05:42.037	2026-01-08 18:41:09.374	\N
