6960b978e17a265e32abba55	SLU Ramadan 	<p>I chose Saint Louis University during Ramadan because it is a familiar space where religion becomes visible in subtle but meaningful ways. Walking around campus during this time, the physical environment looks mostly the same, but the rhythms of daily life feel different. I noticed people carrying water bottles but not drinking from them, sitting quietly during lunch hours, or checking their phones around sunset. There were flyers posted for iftar events, reminders of prayer times, and conversations about fasting that blended naturally into campus life. These details were easy to miss unless you were paying attention, but together they showed how religion shapes everyday routines.</p><p>The people in this space were diverse in terms of race, gender, and background, which made the religious presence feel integrated rather than separate. One of my closest friends is Muslim and she thought me a lot when it was my first time experiencing and seeing Ramadan hapenning. One of the biggest insigths is that some students fasts openly, while others practices quietly. And paying more atention to that I could see there was a sense of mutual awareness and respect, even among those who were not participating in Ramadan. Food spaces on campus felt different during the day and more social in the evening, especially when people gathered to break their fast together. This highlighted how religion can influence time, space, and social interaction without needing a formal worship setting.</p><p>Emotionally, this observation made me feel more aware and reflective. At first, I felt unsure about what counted as “religion” in this space, but the longer I observed, the clearer it became that religion was embedded in daily life rather than confined to prayer rooms or services. The overall mood felt calm and intentional, shaped by discipline, patience, and community.</p><p>This site matters because it challenges the idea that religion only exists in sacred buildings. Ramadan at SLU shows how religion interacts with education, culture, and social life. It demonstrates lived religion in action, where belief influences behavior, schedules, and relationships. Understanding this helps explain how religion continues to shape modern life, even in secular or academic spaces.</p><p>Digital media for this fieldnote included a photo of campus flyers and notes taken during observation.</p><p></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767978540671.jpg" width="1200" style="aspect-ratio: 1.45631 / 1;"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Tinos; font-size: 15px;">Attendees socialize and take a few moments to reflect ahead of breaking their fasts at the collab iftar in the St. Louis Room of the Busch Student Center on March 18. (The University News / Nejla Hodzic)</span><br><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://unewsonline.com/2025/03/ramadan-celebrations-at-slu-put-community-building-at-the-forefront/">https://unewsonline.com/2025/03/ramadan-celebrations-at-slu-put-community-building-at-the-forefront/</a></p><p></p><p><br></p>	bgTpAnBRelfhaolDseUJ9wx1dqM2	38.6358876	-90.23412309999999	3630 W Pine Blvd, St. Louis, MO 63108, USA	f	t	f	[]	2026-01-09 08:17:08.19	2026-01-09 08:16:56.482	2026-01-09 21:03:12.626	\N
