66ad4ff2ee271ee786d83122	Ermita de la Caridad	<p>Just five miles south of Miami’s Little Havana, on the coast of Biscayne Bay, sits the conical-shaped National Shrine of Our Lady of Charity – what Thomas Tweed once described as "the sacred center of the Cuban Catholic community in exile” (Thomas Tweed, <em>Our Lady of the Exile: Diasporic Religion at a Cuban Catholic Shrine in Miami</em>, 1997, pg. 3).</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633422319.jpg" alt="IMG_1709.JPG" loading="lazy" width="875" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633465902.jpg" alt="IMG_1694.JPG" loading="lazy" width="875" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633521957.jpg" alt="IMG_1721.JPG" loading="lazy" width="876" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633556150.jpg" alt="IMG_1754.JPG" loading="lazy" width="883" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>The centerpiece of the site is located in the sanctuary, where behind the altar rests a doll-size figurine of Our Lady of Charity, wearing a fanned-out golden dress and illuminated from below. The figurine is positioned in a way that incorporates her into the stunning mural painted on the wall behind the altar, which extends from the floor of the altar to the ceiling. According to a brochure available at the shrine, the mural narrates the history of Cuban Catholicism. The mural includes 44 important figures in this history from 1492-1977, beginning with Christopher Columbus in the lower left corner, and culminating in an image of two angels carrying a Cuban flag at the top, which the brochure describes as a representation of “carrying the homeland to God.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633601663.jpg" alt="IMG_1748.JPG" loading="lazy" width="894" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633622483.jpg" alt="IMG_1723.JPG" loading="lazy" width="891" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633671279.jpg" alt="IMG_1728.JPG" loading="lazy" width="880" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633711366.jpg" alt="IMG_1725.JPG" loading="lazy" width="892" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p>In 1997 Thomas Tweed published his extensive ethnographic study of the shrine to Our Lady of Charity, which offers in-depth information on the history of the shrine and its devotees in the 1990s. That being said, the shrine is not frozen in time, and messaging added to the exterior grounds of the site has been updated to include slogans from the 2021 protests in both Cuba and Miami, criticizing the Cuban socialist government. For example, the hashtag #SOSCuba, used to spread awareness of the protests on social media, is printed on a banner below the petition, <em>Virgen de la Caridad, Salva a Cuba!</em> (Virgin of Charity, Save Cuba!). Further, attached to the fence on the opposite side of the grounds is a phrase that can be seen on many flags hanging from balconies in Little Havana: <em>Patria y Vida. </em>This phrase comes from a reggaeton song which criticized the Cuban government and was adapted as the unofficial slogan of the 2021 protests. On the grounds of the shrine, this slogan has been further modified to include the word <em>Dios</em>, as it is written above an image of the Cuban flag and American flag, connected in the center by Our Lady of Charity: <em>Dios: Patria y Vida</em> (God: Homeland and Life).</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633761010.jpg" alt="IMG_1698.JPG" loading="lazy" width="909" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633827166.jpg" alt="IMG_1703.JPG" loading="lazy" width="876" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633849479.jpg" alt="IMG_1712.JPG" loading="lazy" width="879" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633865260.jpg" alt="IMG_1711.JPG" loading="lazy" width="890" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722633883753.jpg" alt="IMG_1773.JPG" loading="lazy" width="893" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Sources:</p><p>Anamaria Artemisa Sayre, “Explaining 'Patria Y Vida,' The Song That's Defined The Uprising In Cuba,” <em>NPR</em>, July 20, 2021, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.npr.org/sections/altlatino/2021/07/19/1017887993/explaining-patria-y-vida-the-cuban-song-defying-an-evil-revolution</a>.</p><p><em>The Mural at the Shrine</em> (Miami: National Shrine of Our Lady of Charity).</p><p>Thomas Tweed, <em>Our Lady of the Exile: Diasporic Religion at a Cuban Catholic Shrine in Miami</em> (New York: Oxford University Press), 1997.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	25.7413124	-80.2115432	3609 S Miami Ave, Miami, FL 33129, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "Shrine", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Catholicism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Cuba", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-07 21:15:29.276	2024-08-02 21:30:26.513	2024-11-22 20:04:23.429	\N
69622142e5cae7d05d1f4f6b	basilica venice 	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768040621501.jpg" alt="Image description" width="1536" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768040576381.jpg" alt="Image description" width="1536" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768040606404.jpg" alt="Image description" width="1536" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768040564503.jpg" alt="Image description" width="1536" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768040548715.jpg" alt="Image description" width="1078" style="aspect-ratio: 0.749131 / 1;"><p>This is the Basilica Santa Maria della Salute in Venice, Italy. The first thing I noticed when I walked in was the statues. I was amazed by how beautifully preserved they were and by the skill required to build them so long ago. The ceilings were very high, and many windows surrounded the space. It was a rainy day in Venice, and looking up at the cloudy sky through the windows made me feel as though I was no longer on earth but instead in the clouds. The white walls reflected this light, and as I stood there my chest felt lighter.</p><p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">The architectural layout of the basilica is circular, with arches leading to different sections filled with paintings, statues, candles, and religious artwork. A few benches are placed in the middle of the space, positioned toward the altar. White marble dominates the interior with gold detailing, and the colors complement each other in a way that feels intentional and calming. Overall, the atmosphere felt peaceful, and clean.</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0);">Many religious objects immediately caught my attention. Large crosses and arches are built directly into the architecture, with statues positioned high above, making them appear almost like angels. Angelic figures and religious symbolism appear in nearly every corner. Long candles are placed at the altar facing the benches, and near the entrance there is a small metal table where visitors can purchase a candle for one Euro and light it. Aside from candlelight, the space relies almost entirely on natural lighting. The paintings use blue, white, red, and neutral tones, blending seamlessly with the architecture and light to create a sense of sanctuary and peace.</span></p><p><br>The basilica was not busy , and most of the people inside appeared to be older. Many visitors entered quietly, sat down to pray, lit a candle, and then left. Others stayed longer, sitting silently or taking photographs. The space remained quiet, with only a few whispers, and it was clear that everyone treated the basilica with respect. After I began to feel more relaxed and comfortable. I decided to light one myself. lighting the candle made me feel optimistic and hopeful.The basilica encourages religious behavior through its architecture, artwork, light, and silence, it creates an environment that makes reflection feel free. The artwork shows religious values visually, emphasizing themes like family, innocence and purity . While the basilica is clearly a religious space, its history and location also make it touristic, which is evident in visitors who come mainly to observe and take a photo. The use of expensive marble and gold shows the Church’s historical wealth and power throughout history&nbsp;<br></p>	yr8rTfUHgEdOOFgJSsIuMbY6Ntr2	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[]	2026-01-10 10:26:35.469	2026-01-10 09:52:02.688	2026-01-11 05:28:55.589	\N
69615a6de501b2dec4d9df59	St. Barbara Church of Saburtalo	<p>I was here a few days ago to celebrate Orthodox Christmas. I was invited by a friend of my grandmother that regularly attends this church.</p><p></p><p>As I walked in I noticed around 6-7 small buildings and a church in one chunk of land. Some of them serve as confession booths, storage, etc.</p><p>I got there early so it was relatively empty. The air around me was so peaceful and clear. It truly felt like the land that I was walking on was holy. I even saw a flock of birds flying overhead as soon as I walked in.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767990037207.jpg" alt="Image description" width="422" style="aspect-ratio: 0.856651 / 1;"><p>Once the people started gathering it was lively and so friendly. Everyone greeting each other as if they were life long friends (which some of them were), celebrating Christmas and slowly but surely gathering around to start the ceremony. But before we get to that let me talk a bit more about the priests there. I got to talk to two of them, one was older and was labeled as more experienced while the other one was relatively young. Talking to them felt like I was presented with an endless pit of wisdom, and they shared a small portion of that said wisdom with me. The elder priest actually took offered to take my confession which I immediately agreed to. We stepped in to one of the smaller buildings I mentioned earlier (That's how I came to find out the purpose of these buildings) away from the people. The building consisted of a single room, dark, no lights with a simple heater to warm the place up. It was also used as storage from what I could see. Before I stepped in, he set out two chairs one for him and for me facing directly in front of his. The priest sat me down and spoke to me. I won't get into the details of the confession, but I'll say this much: it was one of the most relieving and peaceful experiences I have had.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767990013484.jpg" alt="Image description" width="429" style="aspect-ratio: 0.852874 / 1;"><p>As the service began, the people and the priest started chanting inside and outside the church, marking the start of Divine Liturgy (I know these rituals by their Georgian names, and I describe them here based on my experience of their sequence and practice.I might get the names of these services wrong since I only know them in Georgian. I apologize in advance). Divine melody of chants filled the area. Later, passages from the Gospel were read aloud. Lit candles were held all around me as the people listened and occasionally marked themselves with the sign of the cross. Toward the end, bread dipped in wine was distributed, symbolizing the body and blood, a practice that made the ritual tangible and communal rather than purely symbolic. Because this took place on Orthodox Christmas, the service felt especially charged, blending celebration with reverence. Religion here functioned less as something explained and more as something enacted, learned through participation, repetition, and shared timing.</p><p></p><p>What struck me the most was how inseparable religion and community felt in this space. This was not just a site for individual belief, but a place where relationships, memory, and identity were reinforced. The church did not feel separate from everyday life, instead it structured it. I heard people discuss verses and passages with each other, sharing their slightly elevated or different understanding of them to which the group rejoiced. Something like this was something that I have only ever seen in a church happen.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767990097527.jpg" alt="Image description" width="431" style="aspect-ratio: 0.866744 / 1;">	MnFDBFJqBrbcnAHKMcrBSGGu6HS2	41.71464196319862	44.775449555210145	14 Simon Chikovani St, T'bilisi, Georgia	t	f	f	[]	2026-01-09 19:43:30.913	2026-01-09 19:43:41.514	2026-01-13 23:47:39.254	\N
69616037e501b2dec4d9df5a	Crave Coffee House	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767991997252.jpg" alt="Image description" width="100" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767991598872.jpg" alt="Image description" width="100" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><p></p><p>Walking by the Crave Coffeehouse, you do not see the traditional coffee shop exterior. You might think it is a church, which it is. They have a worship service on Sunday mornings. But during the rest of the week, the floor is set up with tables and chairs, and the Coffeehouse is open. You will hear music playing and see the speakers in the upper corners surrounding the stage. There is a podium and a cross hung in the center. There are 7 arched stained glass windows, and a door that was put in where the 8th one once was. That door leads to their outside seating, which is currently empty because of the cold weather. There are 5 chandeliers that provide additional soft lighting. You can hear the espresso machine whistle and the workers talking as you sit and enjoy your order. People sit around the tables working on their computers, talking on the phone, or catching up with friends around a cup of coffee. You can smell the sandwiches that they make on the grill. The environment is soft and inviting, but allows for people to keep their space and focus on their work. Today, the seats are mostly empty, and the workers are talking about how it will be busier next week when the students are back in school as the spring semester starts. They still have a Christmas tree, a wreath, and strands of garland hanging.</p><p>What about this church is different from others?</p><p>Why did they decide to not only have a coffee bar on Sundays, but also open a coffee shop throughout the week?</p><p>Do the profits from Crave support the church's continued operation?</p><p>Was the addition of the coffee shop to bring exposure to the church despite its smaller capacity?</p><p>Has the addition of Crave Coffeehouse increased the number of constituents in the church’s congregation?</p><p>Does the coffeehouse being in a church positively or negatively affect the number of customers?</p><p>People are sitting, but remain focused on the technology in front of them. The workers’ faces change from smiles when talking with the customers to staying quiet and looking in the back. The customers who are talking do so in a quieted voice, and there is no loud laughter or harsh tones. The music reflects this with softer voices and smoother rhythms, promoting a relaxing environment.</p><p>As I sit here, I am not thinking about the fact that this is a church. I am thinking it is a nice place to sit and enjoy a meal with company, or sit with my computer and work while sipping on a hot beverage. I wonder how many people actually see this as a place of religious worship and how many simply view it as another coffee shop where they can sit and be productive.</p><p></p>	9BZ6Ms5BpnQNZcWVGt0zZcc8brB2	38.6220608	-90.2353045	3500 Caroline St, St. Louis, MO 63104, USA	f	t	f	[{"label": "coffee", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "food", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "christianity", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "church", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Students", "origin": "user"}]	2026-01-09 20:07:45.351	2026-01-09 20:08:23.824	2026-01-09 20:54:47.229	\N
65e60ed91d442f7c715c9c44	Sheikh Abdul Qadir al-Jilani Shrine	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709575832107.jpg" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p>&nbsp;<br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>	IP0ariWjZ2gcDCGHDn7jmvHifwn2	33.336731263994906	44.40834426849967	8CP5+P83, Baghdad, Baghdad Governorate, Iraq	f	f	f	[]	2024-03-04 18:09:15.534	2024-03-04 18:11:37.658	2024-11-22 19:12:25.045	\N
6961e370e17a265e32abba58	Jvari (Cross) Monastery	<p>Jvari Monastery sits overlooking the intersection of the Mtkvari and Aragvi rivers, a place of high significance. The river Mtkvari that splits Georgia has played a pivotal role in the history of the country. Aragvi is also one of the most important rivers in the country's history, so it sense why their intersection is regarded as so significant. The elevated location of the Monastery makes it visible from a distance. </p><p></p><p>The grounds are calm but not silent. Visitors move slowly, often pausing to look outward rather than inward, drawn to the beautiful view below. At some point it felt as if the Monastery was placed there to keep watch on both the people and the land. Just like the rest of the churches, candles and icons fill your vision if you step inside.</p><p></p><p>What stands out to me with the Jvari Monastery is how power and religion feel connected here. Jvari does not hide itself, or sit in a secluded corner. It feels as if it is declaring itself, "I am here, this land is under my protection". To me, the monastery presents religion as something that marks territory, history, and collective memory.</p>	MnFDBFJqBrbcnAHKMcrBSGGu6HS2	41.838530451796245	44.73324358463287	RPQM+89V Jvari Monastery, Mtskheta, Georgia	t	f	f	[]	2026-01-04 05:28:13.063	2026-01-10 05:28:16.666	2026-01-13 23:48:43.119	\N
6962c077e17a265e32abba5a	Midway Airport	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768078692234.jpg">The Midway International Airport in Chicago is the scrappy sibling of O’Hare and the city’s major hub for Southwest Airlines. When I landed at the airport ahead of the 2026 American Historical Association meeting, I noticed signage for both a Chapel and a Yoga Room. I made a mental note to scope out these sacred spaces before my return flight. These photos were taken on a Saturday afternoon in early January. The Bears will face the Packers a few hours later in an NFL playoff game a few miles east of the airport at Soldier Field. Walking through the concourse, lots of people wear Bears clothing, suggesting another unexpected sacred space.<br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079158253.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079170586.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079181167.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079192577.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079198867.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079206702.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079215023.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079219650.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079224308.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079229979.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079235660.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079239893.jpg"><br>“Chapel” is an “interdenominational” sacred space (unpack this!) that is most visibly Protestant, catholic, and Muslim. No obvious markers of Judaism, Buddhism, Hinduism, Orthodox Christianity, indigenous, or other religious traditions. I sat in the room for about 20 minutes before my flight and counted 3 other people who used the space, two for afternoon prayers (Muslim) and another for unspecified prayer or reflection. 	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	41.78821065052975	-87.74382626196	Concourse B, Chicago O'Hare International Airport, Gate B7, Chicago, IL 60638, USA	f	f	f	[]	2026-01-10 21:11:18.907	2026-01-10 21:11:19.293	2026-04-15 13:48:42.215	\N
