65bbd25d1d442f7c715c9b8c	Ellen Clark Sculpture Park	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706808133695.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190616_EllenClarkSculpturePark_Riley_001.JPG" width="473" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706808174606.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190616_EllenClarkSculpturePark_Riley_002.JPG" width="474" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706808209658.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_EllenClarkSculpturePark_Riley_001.JPG" width="473" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706808242463.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_EllenClarkSculpturePark_Riley_002.JPG" width="468" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706808286377.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_EllenClarkSculpturePark_Riley_005.JPG" width="602" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706808316625.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_EllenClarkSculpturePark_Riley_006.JPG" width="603" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The sound of the creak as you walk through the wrought-iron gates of the Sculpture and Dog Park signifies an entire new environment. Although the loudest sound is still the constant traffic on the intersection between Grand and Lindell, the park has a completely different atmosphere than the bustling cityscape surrounding it. Covered in bright green grass and shady trees with birds chirping loudly overhead, the park acts as a resort from the hectic chaos that lies just beyond its gates. The natural landscape differs immensely from the brick buildings that surround the park. While the surrounding city has sounds of cars in traffic, the park has sounds of dogs’ collars jingling.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The difference in landscape rings true in even the social aspect of the park: most conversations start with a simple “what is your dog’s name?” or “what breed is it?”. Because of the automatic shared interest of everyone that walks through those gates, social interaction comes incredibly natural to the park’s attendees. While the crowd ranges from young couples, students, families, and older individuals, conversations flow all over the park. The park’s dog population varies just the same: small dogs, big dogs, old dogs, and every different shade of fur imaginable.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While the original purpose of the park was to be a display of colorful and vibrant sculptures made by Ellen Clark, the park’s attendance is almost entirely from dog owners coming to let their dogs play freely in the city.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The most interesting aspect of the park lies in the religious undertones. While dogs are not typically allowed in religious buildings but they still remain a huge part of many peoples’ lives, the dog park is a place that has religious aspects that includes them. Dogs and their owners visit the park every week or multiple times a week at the same times, visitors quickly learn the names of the regulars, and everyone has a common interest once they arrive.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While Ellen Clark Sculpture and Dog Park resembles a religious location for its visitors and their beloved pets, Saint Francis Xavier, the church for some of the park’s attendants, stands just adjacent to the front gate.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Does this site require further investigating? Yes.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Where do most of the attendees live?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">How often do most of them come? What times of day are most popular?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What do they normally do at the park? Do they consider it a retreat for themselves, or just their dogs?</span></p><p></p><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Sounds of traffic and honking are the loudest noises within the park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A crisp wind blows through and leaves rustle on all of the trees within the gates</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Sounds of dogs running through the grass, the tags on their collars hitting together to make bell noises&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Breaks screech at the stoplight between Grand and Lindell</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In one of the many lined up cars at the stoplight, a radio blasts music in the distance and grows further away as the car speeds off</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As a pack of dogs running together grows closer, the sounds of their heavy breathing gets louder</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The colorful statues in the park seem much brighter in the sunlight, with colors of red, blue, green, silver, and purple</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When new groups arrive, the iron gate squeals open and slams shut behind them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Every dog runs up to the new dog to smell them every time the gate opens</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">An airplane cuts through the sky above, drowning out the traffic noises for a moment</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The park is on the northeast side of SLU’s campus and is located in front of the Marvin and Harlene Wool Center&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Adjacent to the Park is Saint Francis Xavier Church, which looks photoshopped in because of the deep blue sky behind it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A bus pulls up to the stop on the side of the Park and shouts out the location for the bus’ passengers</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In the distance, the people in the Park can hear the fountain’s streams splashing down into the pool below it (fountain behind the blue Saint Louis University sign on Grand Blvd)</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As time goes on, the park becomes more shady as the sun disappears behind the Jesuit Hall building</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Park’s attendants are a wide variety of people: young couples, students, families, and older individuals</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While the type of people vary, the Park’s dog population has a variety just the same: small dogs, big dogs, old dogs, and every different shade of fur imaginable</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Every few moments, a couple dogs break off from the group and begin to wrestle on their own</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There is a lot of greenery in the park for the very contrasting city-scape surrounding it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">We are framed on three sides by brick buildings of all different shades</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One side of the Park gives an open view to the University, the huge church being the first thing you see</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Fox Theater is just within view</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Park is very colorful with the sculptures and the surrounding city palate -- both with the actual colors of the Park and the environment</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The SLU Church bells ring every hour</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Park attendants seem to spread out because of the design of the seating -- benches are spread on different ends of the Park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Some attendants seem to hover over their dog, watching carefully to stop any problems their dog creates -- this creates conversation as people begin to talk to the wandering owners</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Conversations usually begin with “What’s your dog’s name?” or “What is their breed?”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As it gets later and people begin to come home from work, more dogs arrive at the Park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Although it is firstly named a “Sculpture Park” it seems as though not one person arrives simply for the sculptures, but instead, with their dogs</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Birds chirp loudly overhead</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Park is located at the periphery of Grand Center, creating a great view from the Park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While many people use the Park to sit on their phone while their dog plays, many others use it start up conversation with other dog owners, talk with the company they came with, or to simply sit in the sun and relax</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Many of the Park’s attendants know other owners or other dogs’ names because they come to the Park every week/many times a week</span><br></p></li></ul><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709242056781.mp4">Video 1</a></p>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.637589835614	-90.23210763931274	300-312 N Grand Blvd, St. Louis, MO 63103, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "dogs", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "animals", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "public", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "park", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "urban", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "architecture", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "sculptures", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Catholicism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "outdoors", "origin": "user"}]	2019-06-16 17:00:26.311	2024-02-01 17:18:21.369	2025-01-28 18:22:08.973	\N
