6601bfae1507945c2a4c3260	Laughing Bear Bakery	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711390462728.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Buddah_Safa.jpg.JPG" width="566" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711390473978.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Safa.jpg.JPG" width="570" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711390485398.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Sanctuary_Safa.jpg.JPG" width="547" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711390503444.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_StainedGlass_Safa.jpg.JPG" width="535" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><p></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">The entrance to this hidden bakery was the same one for the old Methodist Church that housed it-ridden with trespasser warnings and accessible only to those whom the church staff let in. The hot air summer air remained unchanged as we entered the aged building-the air conditioning had apparently stopped working weeks ago. That explained the loud, creaky, and rhythmic whirring sound that came from a space in front and to our left. As we got closer, the soft scent of baked goods wafted and sliced through the otherwise muddy and concrete-like stench. Two open doors served as the starting point of what was a large and empty hall. A podium all the way in the back once again reminded me that this wasn’t a stand-alone bakery and was indeed now a part of the church and its attendees. Nonetheless, no one was actually present on the space or amongst the three to four empty round tables and chairs except for the owner, Ms. Kalen, and two of her kitchen staff. From the surfaces of the tables to the old wood polishing across the room, everything had an air of ancient owing to the visible dust, sound of creaks, and simply an aged scent.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">We were shown around the meditation room, the old sanctuary of the church and finally, the bakery’s main kitchen. Compared to the church’s environment, the kitchen was very clean. The two employees, who were former inmates, were extremely focused, diligent, and so very enthusiastic about their work. The way they timed their baking, organized utensils, washed up surfaces, and cooked their goods to perfection showcased the passion these men had for what they were creating. As Ms. Kalen said, baking gave the bakers a place to exhibit their pride and creativity. The bakery as a whole also gave them a place to find others just like themselves along with an environment that, instead of putting them down, lifted them up and promised them a second chance.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Questions:</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">1.What makes Laughing Bear Bakery different from other bakeries such as the one you had mentioned on Cherokee Street?</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">2. Do you notice any differences between male and female bakers when it comes to rehabilitation at the workplace?</span></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711390831124.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711390879737.mp4">Video 2</a></p>	h5IM6ZhPGxVEAdSpwywH4C1OZLB2	38.59836297144364	-90.2533882856369	4001-4005 Utah St, St. Louis, MO 63116, USA	f	f	f	[]	2019-07-18 18:12:27.469	2024-03-25 18:17:18.052	2024-06-21 21:25:20.557	\N
