674f5e3a38f26c9105983665	Temple 1 Henro Store	<p>At Ryōzenji, designated as Temple 1 on the Shikoku Pilgrimage, there is a well-stocked <em>henro</em> (pilgrim) store catering specifically to those beginning their spiritual journey. This store offers essential items for the pilgrimage, including the white coat or vest (<em>hakui</em>), a conical hat (<em>sugegasa</em>), the signature walking stick (<em>kongō-zue</em>), and a stamp book (<em>nōkyō-chō</em>) or scroll (<em>kakejiku</em>) used to collect stamps from each temple visited along the route. These items not only identify the wearer as a pilgrim but also hold symbolic and practical significance throughout the pilgrimage.</p><p></p><p>While many temples on the Shikoku Pilgrimage have small shops offering basic supplies and souvenirs, the <em>henro</em> store at Temple 1 stands out as one of the largest and most comprehensive. It serves as a central point for pilgrims to gather the necessary gear and receive guidance, making it an ideal starting place for their journey.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1733250692259.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="883" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1">	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	\N	\N	\N	t	f	f	[]	2024-12-03 18:27:16.055	2024-12-03 19:38:34.612	2025-03-18 18:13:57.584	\N
674e081a187a21d9a2a59f51	Temple 4 - Dainichi	<p>Temple 4 (<em>Dainichiji</em>) is located at the end of the first sustained climb of the Shikoku Pilgrimage path, nestled in a tranquil upland valley. During our 2019 visit, our team spoke with the head monk, who has spearheaded efforts to make the temple more accessible to the increasing number of visitors. These updates include improved restrooms and an expanded parking area, ensuring greater comfort for pilgrims and other visitors.</p><p>While the temple warmly welcomes everyone, the monk and temple staff stress the importance of respect for the sacred site. Visitors are encouraged to read the posted signs for guidance and, whenever possible, to use Japanese when conversing, fostering an atmosphere of cultural understanding and reverence.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1733166945994.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="951" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1">	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[]	2024-12-02 19:12:41.654	2024-12-02 19:18:50.225	2024-12-03 20:37:06.596	\N
67c771f56d35d4138c2fd585	The Virgin Mary in Astoria	<p>The statues are facing out into the street rather than inside the home. This might be for several reasons: to remind passersby of something divine, to ward off people who come to harm, to ward off evil spirits, to identify oneself as a devout person to the customers.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1741123830946.jpg"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1741123852214.jpg"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1741123871430.jpg"><p><br></p>	d2v7JCxLlFT9jkQlvayOD4Z3HAL2	40.69161968379623	-73.91410070813502	166 Cornelia St, Brooklyn, NY 11221, USA	t	f	f	[]	2025-03-04 21:34:44.213	2025-03-04 21:34:45.157	2025-03-11 04:26:25.202	\N
678484e21132ef2b3c8f1d1f	Modern Cross?	<p>On Gravois, right before highway 44 there's a lopsided cross. There's nothing detailing why it's there or if the uneven nature of it indicates anything. Modern cross or abstract art and a statement? </p>	GHHBmf2Y5RQTXN1drNatuiZUqf63	38.60739977893956	-90.21491409176025	2114 Lemp Ave, St. Louis, MO 63104, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-01-13 03:04:57.635	2025-01-13 03:13:38.642	2026-04-15 13:48:38.766	\N
67c739280433403af519366d	Guadalupe in Maspeth 	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1741108693997.jpg"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1741108879372.jpg"><p>Wayfinding in Maspeth Queens, I came across this fixture immediately after opening the door to the diner. <br></p>	d2v7JCxLlFT9jkQlvayOD4Z3HAL2	40.69161968379623	-73.91410070813504	166 Cornelia St, Brooklyn, NY 11221, USA	t	f	f	[]	2025-03-04 17:32:22.868	2025-03-04 17:32:24.13	2025-03-11 04:26:49.347	\N
674688222a34da641188e3c1	Car/walking Directional Sign	<p>As the number of henro participating in the Shikoku Pilgrimage has increased, signage in Japanese and English (and sometimes other languages) has been installed in many areas to try to direct pilgrims and visitors unfamiliar with the area.</p><p></p><p>This sign, on the journey between Temple 4 and Temple, directs both walking henro and those traveling by car.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1732675472502.jpg" alt="IMG_2018.jpg" loading="lazy" width="870" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333">	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	34.1466480068149	134.46824360793457	Kameyamashita-20 Ōtera, Itano, Itano District, Tokushima 779-0105, Japan	f	f	f	[]	2024-11-27 02:41:00.854	2024-11-27 02:46:58.217	2026-04-15 13:48:38.817	\N
6782ea5c9dcf3e02b0f6d80c	Basilica of the National Shrine of Immaculate Conception	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736636018629.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="462" style="aspect-ratio: 0.860708782742681"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736636035781.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="454" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736636064777.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="451" style="aspect-ratio: 1.2029257314328583"><p>The Basilica of the National Shrine of the Immaculate Conception is located in Washington D.C. It serves as a minor basilica in the Catholic faith and acts as a national shrine. It is also the largest church building in North America and the tallest habitable building in Washington D.C. Walking into the basilica for the first time can be intimidating. The building is huge and the ceiling is covered in a collection of artwork. The first mosaic that most people's eyes are drawn to is the Christ in Majesty Mosaic. The goal of the mosaic is to remind the people who see it that Christ reigns above all. The mosaic shows Christ with his arms raised and three tongues of flames coming from his head. There are many fine details in the image, such as his crown of jewels to show he is above all, the look on his face that shows he is in judgment, and his throne which is a symbol of pardon and reconciliation. Moving on from the mural there is a large number of other things to do. The basilica has numerous chapels and there is almost always a mass happening in one of them. There is also a cafeteria where visitors can get food. Lastly, there is a gift shop that sells different religious goods. All proceeds from the gift shop contribute back to the basilica. Moving on from things to do at the Basilica, there is a lot of feelings and emotions that happen while inside. All the times I have been there it has always been packed full of people. The lights are low in the entirety of the church and there is always quiet music playing in the background. The air smells of candle wax and incense. Even though I was there to sightsee, it was obvious that the feeling in the air was one of worship. Numerous tours are being given simultaneously, and it is difficult to find a quiet and private spot to pray. The church is a large and beautiful example of religion and architecture in the United States.</p>	vkXhcfUpNogQCZia3cvpO8pJ44G3	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[]	2025-01-11 22:01:14.256	2025-01-11 22:02:04.502	2025-01-11 22:54:42.99	\N
68269b1b56ac5d90d588cce2	New Cathedral- The Cathedral Basilica of St. Louis	<img height="auto" src="https://lh7-rt.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXcVQ72zXBzvZsPLUCy4WSlU2AzU8pho5x2Tr8wxjVHeqYTeM99pJhQIuYDkG6q1OGTfHAmHElWJg2FN0VCrDDRkACS6pj1JGVKG0QR3eC2DF-rGdRr3yz7_PnP5YwLviGbZKFwU?key=3MsIV_IskFe34Z10TxtHMZmP" loading="lazy" width="624" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5984095427435387"><p>This postcard showcases the cathedral in 1914, the year it opened to the public. The mosaics inside had not yet been completed, but this postcard showcases the splendor of the Romanesque exterior which factors into the artistic draw of the cathedral. Creation of a postcard immediately after opening shows this place was anticipated to be popular and well known. </p><p>Title: New Cathedral</p><p>Creator: Unknown</p><p>Contributor: Maura Hill </p><p>Date: 1914 ca. </p><p>Type: Colorized photo postcard </p><p>Rights: No Copyright - United States</p><p>Source: The State Historical Society of Missouri, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://digital.shsmo.org/digital/collection/imc/id/69323/rec/2">https://digital.shsmo.org/digital/collection/imc/id/69323/rec/2</a></p>	zHwslkIPrdaiaeuXvaSbHh0JIHt1	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[]	2025-05-16 01:55:13.921	2025-05-16 01:55:39.134	2025-05-16 04:56:22.888	\N
