696136e2e501b2dec4d9df56	Hospital	The hospital waiting room is a space where religion appears quietly during moments of uncertainty and stress. The physical environment is neutral and clinical, but emotional intensity is high. I’ve been to the hospital a couple of times and had to wait that stressful time to the doctor come back with news and I observed people sitting silently, holding hands, whispering prayers, or bowing their heads. Some wore religious jewelry or held prayer beads, while others interacted with chaplains passing through the space. Religion here was not loud or organized, but deeply personal.I felt a sense of heaviness and vulnerability while observing this space. The mood was tense but hopeful, and religion seemed to offer comfort and structure in moments when people felt powerless. What stood out to me was how religion functioned as emotional support rather than formal practice. This matters because it shows religion responding to human needs in real time. In this setting, religion becomes about coping, hope, and meaning rather than doctrine or ritual, highlighting its role in everyday life during moments of crisis.	bgTpAnBRelfhaolDseUJ9wx1dqM2	-16.6768109	-49.2470761	R. 239, 206 - Setor Leste Universitário, Goiânia - GO, 74605-070, Brazil	f	t	f	[]	2026-01-09 17:12:02.067	2026-01-09 17:12:02.708	2026-01-09 20:54:59.685	\N
6961ca2ee17a265e32abba57	Providence Friends: Quaker Meeting House	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768019122931.jpg" alt="Image description" width="100" style="aspect-ratio: 0.5625 / 1;"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt;">Providence Friends: Quaker Meeting House</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt;">For my first field note, I wanted to start out by mentioning that I had a personal experience to this specific place as I had attended Moses Brown for several years before transferring for high school. Moses Brown is a Quaker school that does in fact have a meeting house on the schools campus. As well this meeting house has several ceremonies each week and is mandatory for school students to attend every few weeks. Though technically it is considered a private place and / or locations, the meeting house is open 24/7 and 7 days a week for people to visit and come into. As well in regards to determining whether or not I had needed to figure out if I needed to seek permission to take pictures, video, or audio recordings, I had spoken to the director of the meeting house at Moses Brown School and had been told I am allowed to take pictures from the outside. I had intended to stay as respectful and had sought out clarification as much as possible in the process and therefore took a picture of the outside of the building. Although I had wished to have had the opportunity to have taken advantage more of the inside of the meeting house, I was still able to capture and paint a good picture and form a deeper understanding of Quakerism. Although there were not many people at this specific locations, I was able to see two people sitting inside in silence. And though they were not directly have any interactions with each other or me, I would say that they were interacting with the meeting house. And in regards to cultural signals while spending my time at the meeting house and not having any personal interactions with the two people who I had witnessed there I did not get any cultural signals in this place, object, site, etc. And after my experience back here at the meeting house at Moses Brown school, it allowed me to think more deeply and gain a little bit more context of the importance and what it means in Quakerism. And a question that kept coming up to mind while I had been at this specific place, was how do quakers in society live their lives today in current times? And why they continue to practice their religious practices even though in current times Quakerism is commonly forgotten about and even looked down upon?</span></p>	2kxgy7B5BJUhMl53tziRi5QIU4O2	41.83561	-71.397533	87 Morris Ave, Providence, RI 02906, USA	f	t	f	[]	2026-01-10 03:40:26.302	2026-01-10 03:40:30.717	2026-01-10 04:25:27.338	\N
6961e288e501b2dec4d9df5e	Gergeti Trinity Church	<p>Gergeti Trinity Church is unique in its isolation. Sitting high above the surrounding landscape, it appears almost detached from everyday life. The path to the church makes it feel as if you are being detached and separated from the world below. By the time you reach it, there is only silence and open space around you. The view is amazing from up there with mountains covering the horizon in every direction.</p><p></p><p>The atmosphere as I said before is quiet, restrained. People speak quietly, softly, taking in the view as much as the church itself. Much like the rest of the churches, candles and icons appear all around you once you step inside. What's so surreal about the church and its location is that the natural surroundings feel just as sacred as the church itself. Wind, clouds, distance, they all work together to shape one of the most peaceful experiences you could ever have. </p><p></p><p>Gergeti reflects the image and history of Georgia well. Endurance, geography, ancientness, these are all common themes almost everywhere in Georgia. Gergeti also shows that even while disconnected from the world faith persists in isolation. </p><p></p>	MnFDBFJqBrbcnAHKMcrBSGGu6HS2	42.66221869380565	44.620255529880524	MJ7C+75C, Stepantsminda, Georgia	t	f	f	[]	2026-01-02 05:24:18.581	2026-01-10 05:24:24.246	2026-01-13 23:48:50.485	\N
