rear end of the church had a rusty teal and reddish wheelbarrow that lay against the church wall. It faced bushes of dry, wheat-like weeds in the soil patches that encased the church. The air was crisp and clear and allowed for the bells of nearby churches to accent the appearance of this one, for no other indications besides a metal cross on the roof and a metal plaque on the front appeared to give this structure a church-like appearance. Moreover, the presence of people at this church was not to worship inside of it (they could not), but to treat it as a tourist site and simply document it through pictures.</span></p><p></p><ol><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 12pt">Does the interior space play a really important role in the religious significance of a church?</span></p></li></ol>	h5IM6ZhPGxVEAdSpwywH4C1OZLB2	38.778691989849754	-90.4830846190453	401 S Main St, St Charles, MO 63301, USA	f	f	f	[]	2019-06-17 17:00:40.291	2024-02-23 19:49:28.884	2024-06-21 21:25:18.717	\N
