6625ca9a5cc3848690279ebe	The Church of Baseball	<p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1714157457108.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="4032" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Each week on Sunday mornings, Little Leaguers in Southern California pray to the gods of baseball as they recite the Little League Pledge: "I trust in God, I love my country, and will defend its laws. I will play fair, and strive to win. But win or lose, I will always play my best. PLAY BALL!"</p><p></p><p>The ritual begins about five minutes before the game is scheduled to start. The players come out on the field as the coaches yell "don't step on the lines!" Home team lines up along the third base line, and the visiting team lines up along the first place line. Then the manager from the home team invites one of his players to help led the two teams in the Pledge of Allegiance. A second player helps lead the Little League pledge. Between these two recitations, these Southern California seekers have recognized God two times before the game can begin. Play Ball!</p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1713800804921.mp4">Video 1</a></p>	to6gNGICQ1X1EHBDN6KQVeykKas2	34.26996484131353	-118.52066859774467	17723 Horace St, Granada Hills, CA 91344, USA	t	f	f	[]	2024-04-22 02:20:04.151	2024-04-22 02:25:30.665	2024-06-21 20:54:15.798	\N
66283096e8afc731e3b58e54	Melrose Abbey	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1713909729069.jpg" loading="lazy" width="4032" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p>The monastery is partly ruined, yet still serene. The surrounding area is scattered with tombstones, some eligible and clean, and others sunken and barely visible. It is said that Alexander II and the heart of Robert the Bruce are among those buried here. There is greenery throughout the ruins that flows with the walkways of the abbey. The size of the monastery is large, even without its full architecture present. I’m 5’5’’ and my head didn’t meet the bottom of some of the window openings.&nbsp; The stone used is configured into many archways, sculptures, and crosses, reflecting the trends in medieval architecture. The people are quiet and observant, taking time to look at and touch the remaining stone. I was with my family members, some of whom are Christian and felt moved being in this space. I found myself intrigued with how the monks lived in the monastery at its peak, imagining the walkways and water-wells being used. I wonder what it must have been like to live here with the monks. There is a visiting center and museum about the history of Melrose Abbey. The monastery was the first of its kind (Cistercian) in Scotland, founded in 1136 but was involved in many conflicts with England, leading to reconstruction and eventually ruin.</p>	F9PYL1B1L8VwDllARwcSTpBDCkY2	55.5990683	-2.7190314	9 Buccleuch St, Melrose TD6 9LB, UK	f	f	f	[]	2019-07-12 20:23:00	2024-04-23 22:05:10.705	2024-06-21 20:55:12.543	\N
