66e8724f02b8c4ed7f950770	Bando Station - Start of the Henro Michi	<p>This is an updated note for Bando Station.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731435215288.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="579" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33314 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731435215289.jpg" alt="greenLineRoad.jpg" loading="lazy" width="426" style="aspect-ratio: 0.750108 / 1"><p>This small train station is the main starting point for the "Green Line," a green painted line on the pavement guiding henro (pilgrims) from the station to the main gate of Ryozen-ji, Temple No. 1 on the Shikoku Pilgrimage. Henro in their white gear are a common sight on the local trains coming from Tokushima Station, especially during the spring pilgrimage season. As more visitors from countries outside Japan come to Shikoku to participate in the henro michi, support in the form of signs in English and other languages is growing, but it still can be difficult to navigate comfortably without some knowledge of Japanese. Both local people and other pilgrims are generally helpful, and will try to assist with guidance to the correct pathways and temple entrances.</p><p></p><p></p><p></p>	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	34.155435	134.5068805	Tsujimidō-65-1 Ōasachō Bandō, Naruto, Tokushima 779-0232, Japan	f	f	f	[]	2024-09-16 17:57:11.158	2024-09-16 18:00:47.892	2024-11-20 02:07:18.161	\N
6781e3dd9ab7f3dd87966885	Holy Trinity Catholic Church 	<p>Holy Trinity is a Catholic church located in a very wealthy neighborhood in Washington DC. The church is in the heart of Georgetown surrounded by between rowhouses. The church is beautiful with pillars lining the entrance giving off a very formal feeling presence. This is further reinforced by the clothes of those who attend. Many of those who attend Sunday service are in suits and formal dresses including the kids, many of which are dressed up in khakis and semi formal attire. The congregation is made up of many families with young kids. I observed many families talking with each other before and after the Sunday service. The congregation was clearly a very tight knit group with many having connections with each other outside of the church. The community is also seen as the church is connected to a school which many of the families send their kids to. The inside of the church was very well lit with a large seating area and some balcony seating space. The church has tall ceilings with artwork carved into the wall depicting the day that Jesus was crucified. I was welcomed by ushers at the entrance handing out pan flips and welcoming me into the service. They had smiles on their face and a welcoming tone to their voice, this set the tone for my visit as from the start I was welcomed in.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; While the congregation was very welcoming the congregation was made up of predominantly white families. This comes from the economic status of the surrounding area as the church is deep inside the upper class area of Washington DC. The congregation may be welcoming but due to their location they predominantly attract white families. The location of the church limits the accessibility for those outside of Georgetown and the surrounding neighborhoods. This lack of diversity within the church plays a role in the up bringing of the kids who attend the church and its Sunday school program. There is a need for the diversification within the upper class DC area.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; After the conclusion of the service, I enjoyed a donut from the school cafeteria. This allowed me to further see the interactions of the community as parents were talking and kids were running around with their friends. This gave me the feeling of a very family friendly congregation that welcoming growing families. This openness to inclusion of families is important for the church as it allows for growth through the church’s congregation. The church is pivotal for the practice of Christianity because without an accessible church in the heart of Georgetown many would be left without a place to practice as traffic can become an issue. I thought the service was very respectful and there was an undertone of the conservative nature of the church within the priest’s homily. This conservative undertone plays to the location of the church as many of the upper class families are conservative leaning.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736565677192.jpg"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736565684548.jpg"><p><br></p>	b4GD3PejFOXBgLEzgOBazwi5uyg2	\N	\N	\N	t	f	f	[]	2025-01-11 03:22:05.007	2025-01-11 03:22:05.495	2025-01-11 03:23:15.396	\N
675c6b1c2af43b86d55373f3	Kickin' and a Gougin'	<img src="TypeError: NetworkError when attempting to fetch resource." alt="Image description"><p>The smell of stale cigarette smoke hung low and dense as I walked through the heavily tented doors. The lighting was dim and poor. The sun stopped at the doorway. It took some effort for my eyes to adjust. Flashing neon. The unnatural greenish hue of florescent bulbs hanging overhead. Ostentatious carpets, where any stain would easily get lost in the motley of unrelated colors. A cacophony of bells, horns, whistles, and clinks from the hundreds of slot machines. Gloomy figures pumped tokens into various automated contraptions, eyes affixed, shoulders hunched. Oxygen tanks and wrist braces. An all-you-can-eat seafood buffet to the right. A panoptical tower in the middle, with double-sided glass, seeing all, seen by none. No windows. Time, day/night, was irrelevant. Outside is not welcome here. But first, a gruff security guard in an ill-fitted uniform. My ID. Fortunately, I was old enough for this. It was a beautiful Saturday. Fight night at the local casino.</p><p></p><p>I made my way to the impromptu arena. The white floor of the fight cage was encrusted with blood from untold previous encounters. Appropriate. Employees lined stackable chairs around the chain-linked hexagon. Others followed as they placed flyers advertising the forthcoming weekend’s events on each of the chairs. Midget wrestling and wet t-shirt contests. The bartender busily stocked the bar full of light domestic beers. Merchants arranged displays for their pricey wears, such that interested consumers might have the option to let anyone else know that they were once a part of this fandom. Said fandom begins to slowly enter. Affliction and Tapout shirts aplenty. Tattoos and bejeweled jeans. High heels and shorts skirts. Still plenty of time for the cash bar.</p><p></p><p>Backstage—i.e. the mobile home out back—the atmosphere was tense. Grapes, bananas, bottled water, and cheap assorted lunchmeat in the middle. The “red” and “blue” corners on opposing sides of the trailer. Combatants temporarily partitioned by inches of laminate wood paneling. Some paced nervously, exchanging apprehensive yet aggressive glances. Others sat listening to their trainers and cornermen as they got their hands taped. Others sprawled out on the black faux-leather couches, listening to ear-phoned music. Words were sparse in here. Quiet focus. The occasional laughter seemed to be born less of comedy than of a bodily need to vent, to release the overabundance of stored up energy from what was to come. Combatants in the evening’s earlier fights warmed up their kicks and punches in the back parking lot by the dumpsters. 1-2-3, 2-3-2, 1-2-10, 1-1-3-10, 2-3-2-9. Pads of various shapes and sizes absorbed the edgy strikes. Anticipation was palpable. Ring girls smoked cigarettes.</p><p></p><p>The fights went quick. Adrenaline tends not to pace its release over time. It’s not easy to train your body to curtail its natural reaction. But this made for short and exciting exchanges. Knockouts and submissions were abundant. Opponents rapidly expressed those years, months, and weeks of training and anticipation. Warm-ups became a luxury backstage, as we worked quickly to tape hands. Fighters were rushed in by the event coordinators who beckoned them by yelling last names. Each time the doors opened the sounds of the cheering audience would spill out. Sweat, blood, bruises, and hematomas visible on the exiting combatants. They did it for fun.</p><p></p><p>By the time the main fight was to commence, the bar ran dry. The audience was ready. Assigned seats were irrelevant, as most stood and hovered toward the center ring. Groups of same-shirted family and friends gathered in support of their representative. Behind thin curtains, the fighters and their respective entering entourages awaited announcement. I helped one of the fighters stay warm. Each kick, bruising my forearms behind the Thai pads. I pretended it didn’t hurt. He was ready. Less than two minutes into the first round, he took his opponent down to the ground and minced him with a flurry of punches. Blood. The referee pulled them apart, stopping the fight. The audience was rabid. They had gotten their fill of overpriced cheap beer and violent spectacles. Bloodlusty.</p><p></p><p>But not all of the casino’s bars ran dry that night. As I walked past the gruff security guard in the ill-fitted uniform to seek a more oxygen-rich environment outside, I looked back to see the two headliners sitting shoulder to shoulder, laughing over shared beers. Less than an hour after they met in the crimson-stained ring. Hallmark.</p>	OH1utgr2BKSojnHk3zaLBXxse4Z2	47.8451676	-122.5434213	7989 NE Salish Ln, Kingston, WA 98346, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "violence", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "muscularchristianity", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "evangelicalism", "origin": "user"}]	2014-05-17 16:12:59.476	2024-12-13 17:13:00.285	2025-11-21 17:25:02.575	\N
672d3fa00079e794e7c0a114	Test for android	<p>Writing a test</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731018520442.jpg"><p><br><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731018572444.mp4">https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731018572444.mp4</a><br>Url for video should work adding another video<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731018603682.mp4">https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731018603682.mp4</a>Now chance for audio<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731018633684.3gp">https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731018633684.3gp</a><br><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731018640545.3gp">https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1731018640545.3gp</a><br></p>	mPpZp21UPnZSqevTcExeLXCxhfP2	38.6509136	-90.2948419	6012 McPherson Ave, St. Louis, MO 63112, USA	f	f	f	[]	2024-11-07 22:28:29.969	2024-11-07 22:30:56.657	2024-11-09 05:31:47.924	\N
67acbfb1d3689674db51ea25	Lived Religion Example 1	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1739373675371.jpg"><p><br>I chose this tattoo because I became acquainted with a co-worker in the fall of 2023 that had one. The backstory behind this: he is a sureno that was relocated to Illinois after his prison term through a drug rehabilitation program that places the patients into jobs with the intent of integrating them into society. What he believes, and many other surenos believe, is that the “Lady of Guadalupe” is a symbol of protection and faith. She is seen as a prominent representation of Mexico in Catholicism. The Catholic religion is so deeply engrained in many Hispanic cultures that it even bleeds over into gang life. I had to get this picture from google because though I’d seen my co-worker’s tattoo, I never got a chance to take a picture of it because he left the job and program and fled back to the west coast after about 2 months of being in Illinois.</p>	cbajE8sZRhbpsfT4wGI0bppRQra2	40.12793681310618	-88.22138767235299	1604 Melrose Valley Ct, Urbana, IL 61801, USA	t	f	f	[]	2025-02-12 15:35:08.268	2025-02-12 15:35:13.842	2025-02-12 16:13:38.181	\N
678168b69ab7f3dd8796687f	Rural folk activities	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736532193417.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="960" style="aspect-ratio: 0.977597 / 1"><p>This photo shows the most lively festival of the year for the people of Shantou, which does not yet have an official name. On a certain day after the Chinese New Year every year, the rural villages in Shantou would hold a grand festival of worship on a village-by-village basis. All the idols are moved from the temple to the square for worship. The villagers would then bring all kinds of food to the square and each family would have a table. These tables are filled with offerings such as fruits, snacks, roasted meat, chickens and ducks, as well as alcohol and paper money to symbolize respect for the gods and ancestors. The villagers offer incense and worship in turn, praying for favorable weather conditions, family health and business success in the new year. The whole scene was bustling with pious worship rituals and mutual exchanges among villagers, reflecting a strong community atmosphere and family ties.</p><p></p><p>At the end of the ceremony, the offerings are taken home to be enjoyed, which is seen as a symbol of sharing the blessings of the deity. The idol, on the other hand, will be re-carried back to the temple by the village youths to end the day's activities. Although the festival has no official name, it has become a unique cultural symbol in the Shantou area, carrying not only respect for traditional beliefs, but also an important moment for villagers to reunite and pass on their culture. This kind of worship activity demonstrates the Shantou people's emotion towards their history and homeland, and at the same time allows the younger generation to feel the charm and significance of traditional culture.</p><p></p><p>Participating in such activities gives me a deep sense of belonging and respect for traditional culture. In modern society, most villagers live in cities, but everyone returns to the village for this festival. This kind of worship ceremony allows me to pause and reflect on our roots and family bonds. Every offering table and each item on it carries respect for our ancestors and deities, as well as the hopes of each family for the future. This communal ritual, involving the entire village, makes me feel the unity and warmth between people.</p><p>location：Shangtou city ,Guangdong, China</p>	nE1zFFEYadXIDMeGfvyiZLoVJTs1	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[]	2025-01-10 18:01:32.822	2025-01-10 18:36:38.202	2025-01-11 19:44:00.56	\N
674e0a6ddeae4d162f083a79	Temple 4 Area - Map	<p>Throughout the pilgrimage pathways in Tokushima Prefecture, <em>henro</em> (pilgrims) and visitors can find maps and other navigational aids designed to guide them from temple to temple and highlight additional points of interest along the route. These tools are invaluable for ensuring smooth travel and enriching the pilgrimage experience.</p><p></p><p>This particular map, written in Japanese, is located at the edge of a parking area near Temple 4 (<em>Dainichiji</em>). It serves as a practical resource for pilgrims, offering detailed information about the surrounding area and helping them navigate the sacred journey with confidence.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1733167671601.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="875" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p></p>	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	\N	\N	\N	t	f	f	[]	2024-12-02 19:21:27.666	2024-12-02 19:28:45.639	2025-03-18 19:07:49.301	\N
678160751132ef2b3c8f1d16	Rural folk activities	<p>This photo shows the traditional You Shen （Parading of Deities) event held during the Spring Festival in Shantou, a common custom in Shantou and Fujian Minnan-speaking regions. Usually, on a specific day after the Spring Festival, villages in rural Shantou organize the event as a communal ritual. Villagers bring out statues of deities from temples for worship, and then young men from the village carry these statues around the village in a parade, praying for peace and prosperity.</p><p>The deities represented by these statues are often historical figures who made significant contributions to the village, such as teachers, doctors, or officials. The You Shen event is not only a traditional ritual but also a way to commemorate the village’s history and its influential figures, fostering a sense of identity and connection among the villagers.</p><p>location Shangtou city Gunagdon China</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736532028907.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="1921" style="aspect-ratio: 1.80037 / 1">	nE1zFFEYadXIDMeGfvyiZLoVJTs1	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[]	2025-01-10 17:39:06.691	2025-01-10 18:01:25.414	2025-01-11 19:48:53.123	\N
67acec947aec613617fd88de	Lived Religion Example 2	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1739385345117.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1739385403388.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1739385415816.jpg">I took these pictures in a Rural King last week. These are belt buckles that I would say heavily represent the Evangelical faction of Christianity. I took note of the American flag in the background of two of them, one of them stating: “I stand for the flag and kneel for the cross”. I grew up interacting with Evangelical Christians and it is very on brand for them to treat Evangelical Christianity as being synonymous with patriotism. For them, American values and Christianity go hand-in-hand, even so much as to have these beliefs bleed over into politics. <br>	cbajE8sZRhbpsfT4wGI0bppRQra2	40.12793681310618	-88.22138767235299	1604 Melrose Valley Ct, Urbana, IL 61801, USA	t	f	f	[]	2025-02-12 18:46:43.824	2025-02-12 18:46:44.263	2025-02-12 18:46:55.771	\N
68113a1146646ec8cbf26473	Old Chapel or Dormitory Building, Photo of a Building in Shawnee Methodist Mission	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1745959168623.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="364" style="aspect-ratio: 1.32146 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1745959180119.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="365" style="aspect-ratio: 1.35346 / 1"><p>Picture of a building on the grounds of the Historic Shawnee Methodist Mission. The picture is either of the old chapel or a dormitory for students.</p><p>[<span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif; font-size: 12pt">Photograph of Shawnee Mission Old Chapel or Dormitory, Kain Stobbe, Photograph, This physical primary resource is accessible by the State Historical Society of Missouri solely for the purposes of research, Historical Society of Missouri Archives K0058 Box 3 F. 5]</span></p>	b9MMnezhlqTMsMA1kFtm9idZsew2	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[{"label": "building", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "shawneemission", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "methodist", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "shawneemethodistmission", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "chapel", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "photo", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "dormitory", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "mission", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "shawnee", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "laborschool", "origin": "user"}]	2025-04-29 20:19:46.978	2025-04-29 20:44:01.726	2025-05-08 21:02:27.856	\N
68269b1b56ac5d90d588cce1	Decorations in Mosaic Cathedral of St. Louis- Cathedral Basilica of St. Louis	<img height="auto" src="https://lh7-rt.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXdv0TodgmktmHVggsT7agKlXWc-UGOXQmfZV5zM-Q5TYMreuZiNYSM9FRMlOqsRXX6APuLSa3N4nCRqeWC_Sn0tSsIWoUXhV00MN2ab8D5JdUu6Qid_-oRwiHELqfZWYmyzCG3sHw?key=3MsIV_IskFe34Z10TxtHMZmP" loading="lazy" width="283" style="aspect-ratio: 0.7299630086313194"><img height="auto" src="https://lh7-rt.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXegPI1wPmEv93f-w8RQB3uNStNuLHOYjn_VoUZDNoVfLTRhhdiwaKRpUP8Yps5QmZUXkThbeaSQGql3mAgvVzlOOUl_9y_vLpA39_k79Wfr1w5yUvUR1VRU689NjQixB9DbaQWJNw?key=3MsIV_IskFe34Z10TxtHMZmP" loading="lazy" width="245" style="aspect-ratio: 0.7184822521419829"><img height="auto" src="https://lh7-rt.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXf-fOfcrFH3Y8goRyrS1UtwyDt2PkXaMvk55Y5Jz7sruMQRPi4E_3PgtBcFgBMalBAOEjrg1eiawrjC_lW0Py3wm5Z0SRD9E9QHRX-uT1iI0lyA6iXa0cu3emilMh4TjGLlKtZlQQ?key=3MsIV_IskFe34Z10TxtHMZmP" loading="lazy" width="288" style="aspect-ratio: 0.7146433041301627"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Lotus Magazine published this three page article about the mosaics of the main altar and baldacchino at the Cathedral in 1916. The main architect of the cathedral, George D. Barnett, is interviewed for this magazine and discusses his beliefs that mosaics should look like mosaics and not exact copies of the cartoons. Striving for this original appearance makes the mosaics feel like pieces from ancient times.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Title: Decorations in Mosaic Cathedral of St. Louis</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Creator: Gustav Kobbe</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Publisher: Thomas J. Watson Library, The Metropolitan Museum of Art&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Contributor: Maura Hill&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Date: February 1916</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Type: Art Journal/Magazine&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Format: pp. 215-217 (3 pages)</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Rights: To JSTOR’s knowledge, this journal issue is in the public domain and may be used accordingly. JSTOR does not make any representation or warranty regarding, and user assumes responsibility for, use of this content.</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Source: The Lotus Magazine, JSTOR, </span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/20543715"><span style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt"><u>https://www.jstor.org/stable/20543715</u></span></a></p><p><br></p>	zHwslkIPrdaiaeuXvaSbHh0JIHt1	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[]	2025-05-16 01:55:13.921	2025-05-16 01:55:39.133	2025-05-16 04:54:59.517	\N
68269b1acfd83da30865d4db	The Mosaic Beauty of the St. Louis Cathedral- The Cathedral Basilica of St. Louis	<img height="auto" src="https://lh7-rt.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXfHPWaKZtyOM-iyFXnLStgutHrXP89BU2qKAULcXsxrtK0QQ4qD1yToXuL_a_cl11KSFX7e0Nd-UNHDpT3DXa8w6zioAjm2oXV5FAaIjC8WW4_T6PLrd5zNkTG62_eROohW3fRJfQ?key=3MsIV_IskFe34Z10TxtHMZmP" loading="lazy" width="366" style="aspect-ratio: 0.814375"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In this single page of a multi-page article in the St. Louis Globe Democrat, the design process and style of the mosaics are described in detail. Learning the process of this complex art form creates a greater appreciation for the craft and the description of the St. Louis' specific details create a home-like feeling. It is also important to note that there is a connection to ancient arts and societies. Creating elaborate and ancient inspired religious spaces connects the church goer to their God more closely because the house of God is meant to be elaborate for Him.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Title: The Mosaic Beauty of the St. Louis Cathedral&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Creator: Nell Gross</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Publisher: St. Louis Globe-Democrat</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Contributor: Maura Hill&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Date: October 13th, 1962</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Type: Newspaper</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Format: Page 99</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Rights: Unknown</span></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Source: </span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="http://Newspaper.com"><span style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt"><u>Newspaper.com</u></span></a><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">, St. Louis Globe-Democrat</span></p>	zHwslkIPrdaiaeuXvaSbHh0JIHt1	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[]	2025-05-16 01:55:13.921	2025-05-16 01:55:38.782	2025-05-16 05:05:42.776	\N
