65bd8d70ce3cc6a72dfc3619	World's Fair Pavilion	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706919759160.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_Exterior_Riley.JPG" width="543" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706920158002.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_Exhibit_Riley_001.JPG" width="540" style="aspect-ratio: 0.666667 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706920055242.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_Exhibit_Riley_002.JPG" width="549" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706920208848.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_Fountain_Riley_001.JPG" width="541" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706920268679.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_Fountain_Riley_002.JPG" width="542" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706920392310.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_Fountain_Riley_003.JPG" width="537" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706920482577.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_Interior_Columns_Riley_001.JPG" width="529" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706920796020.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_Interior_Columns_Riley_002.JPG" width="527" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706921001256.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_LampPost_Riley.JPG" width="537" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706921059811.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190710_WorldsFairPavilion_TurtleSculpture_Riley.JPG" width="533" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As I walked up to the World’s Fair Pavilion, I could still hear the sounds of cars buzzing by and the presence of all the traffic near Forest Park. But when I walked inside, it was like stepping into a different world. The Pavilion felt much larger than it looked from the outside, as trees block the view of how long it actually is. It seemed like all the hectic noises got drowned out and all I could see was the green space and nature waiting for me on the other side of the Pavilion. The only noises I could hear were the Kerth Fountain on the hill below me, birds chirping overhead, and a few people around the area talking in the distance. People at the bottom of the hill kayak peacefully in a small lake and teenagers mess around in the fountain, splashing water on each other. The ambience of the Pavilion feels like an oasis from the bustling streets around it and it has a relaxing effect on its visitors. Whether it inspires religious reflection, or simply a few moments of mindfulness, it is a perfect calming destination for St. Louis residents looking to escape the city’s hecticness.&nbsp;</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Why do people normally come to the World’s Fair Pavilion?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As lots of people showed up to swim and walk through the Kerth Fountain, are they actually allowed to do this?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">What is the typical race, class, and gender of the everyday Pavilion visitor?</span></p><p></p><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The two loudest sounds at the Kerth Fountain on the bottom of the hill and people in the Pavilion talking</span></p></li></ul><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When they talk, their voices echo a little in the high ceilings of the Pavilion&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It’s a man and a woman in exercise clothes, I believe the woman is his trainer and is instructing him on what to do with the PVC pipe in his hands for their next workout</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A couple walks around the perimeter of the Pavilion, holding hands and talking. They are both wearing nice clothes and look like they’re on a date</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Leaving my car and walking up to the Pavilion, it seemed much smaller, as trees were blocking my view of how long it actually is</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As I walked up to it, I could still hear cars buzzing by and feel the presence of all the traffic near Forest Park. But when I stepped inside of the Pavilion, it seemed like that all got drowned out</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Seeing all the green space and nature waiting for me on the other side of the Pavilion was like stepping into a different universe</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">All the sudden, there were no noises of traffic or many people within sight. All I could see was how beautiful the area looked</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Birds pass by overhead and chirp, the air smells like fresh-cut grass and flowers, and the sun beats down on the pavement I’m standing on</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Inside the Pavilion, it’s nice and shady with a cool breeze passing through, as the building acts as a wind tunnel</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As I viewed out onto the hill below me from the Pavilion’s balcony, I saw a little lake with kayakers in bright orange kayaks paddling around</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Bugs buzz from different directions all around me</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Bikers pass by and ride up through the Pavilion, passing by the people exercising</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Lavender and tall grain plants line the outside of the Pavilion</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">In the Kerth Fountain, a group of four teenagers jump into the fountain, water going up to their knees</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One girl takes pictures of them as the three boys jump around, splashing in the water</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The exercising duo have finished up their session and parted ways, walking towards the exit</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There are two large archways underneath the Pavilion on the left side that are gated up, they seem to lead to an area for event-goes to meet</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">On the other side of the Pavilion is three large archways that lead out onto a ramp that goes all the way down the hill</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The teenagers in the Fountain have stopped playing in the water and having started to look at the pictures that were taken</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A child who saw the teenagers jump in has followed suit, squealing with excitement when he hopped in</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Nearby, an old sounding train horn just blew</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The roof of the inside of the Pavilion is wood slats that are the same orange color as the outside roof</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">On the balcony that looks over the hill, there is “St. Louis World’s Fair Pavilion Plaza” written in the cement blocks on the ground</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There is a plaque that reads “Kerth Fountain, Restored 2004, From the family, friends, and colleagues of Alfred H. Kerth III” that you notice right before you see the fountain below</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">On either side of the Pavilion, there are two tall towers that almost look like watch towers that stand above the rest of the roof</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">An airplane seems to be close to the ground because it loudly flies by above</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">On both sides of the entire Plaza there are rows of tall trees that make the area feel more secluded from the rest of the park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">As a nice breeze blows by again, the tall plants blow back and forth</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The environment is incredibly peaceful and a great place for reflection</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A lot of St. Louis history happened in this place and the environment feels that way</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Times New Roman, serif; font-size: 7pt">&nbsp;</span><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It feels like an oasis from the bustling streets around it and the environment has an effect that makes you want to relax and be mindful</span></p></li></ul><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709327908140.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709327915233.mp4">Video 2</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709327923962.mp4">Video 3</a></p>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.63648780298524	-90.28548896312718	1904 Concourse Dr, St. Louis, MO 63110, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "public", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "park", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "outdoors", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "architecture", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "landscaping", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "monuments", "origin": "user"}]	2019-07-10 17:00:46.258	2024-02-03 00:48:48.356	2025-01-28 18:22:10.935	\N
6753caa8187a21d9a2a59f59	V2	<p>Sample 3 from explore screen…</p>	My5Mp0RKfyfVESHkla0aZ6mywt22	37.785834	-122.406417	1800 Ellis St, San Francisco, CA 94115, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-04-04 22:28:48.095	2024-12-07 04:10:16.427	2025-04-29 15:08:59.504	\N
68102a5d54d0f2a23452987d	Bishop's Chair & Kneeler; Archbishop's Coat of Arms	<p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1747327434574.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="328" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif">The historic chair and kneeler once belonged to Bishop Joseph Rosati and was created around the time the cathedral was consecrated in 1834. The Coat of arms above the chair belong to the archbishop of the Archdiocese of Saint Louis. The coat of arms includes the inscription “Serve the Lord With Gladness”, and a design featuring the flag of Baltimore, the Archbishop's former parish, as well as his Polish ancestry.</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Title: </strong>Bishop Rosati’s Chair and Kneeler; Current Archbishop Rozanski’s Coat of Arms</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Publisher:</strong> Archdiocese of Saint Louis</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Contributor: </strong>Kathleen Jochman</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Format: </strong>JPG Image</span></p>	TrWZ9PxTspOnzWwVD6UeQTxC5s12	38.62390707487893	-90.1870648167376	241-281 Walnut St, St. Louis, MO 63102, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-04-29 01:24:45.347	2025-04-29 01:24:45.523	2025-05-16 01:53:13.889	\N
695daf75e501b2dec4d9df50	Native American Christian Dancers	<p>This field note is on a video I took of a Native American Christian Worship performance while I was at a Christian Conference in Phoenix AZ this past week. The conference is called Urbana and has been taking place once every three years over the past 50 years or so. The conference draws thousands of people from all over the country and the world, and there were people of all races and ethnicities there. An important part of the conference is to demonstrate the global church, and what Christian are doing all over the country and the world. This Native American group was sharing part of their worship, and lessons on how to work together as Christians across cultures. The Native American group emphasized the importance of each dance style. The shawl dancers are representative of butterflies, change and beauty, the jingle dancers represent healing, and the ribbon dancer was this calming presence. The purpose of this group at the conference was to emphasize how Christianity can vary in forms of worship across cultures, and how God created all of these cultures with different dances, clothes, and traditions and they are all beautiful in his eyes. I think that for me it was really wonderful to see this because while we talk about people being different from ourselves to see it lived out was really beautiful. I particularly enjoyed the shawl dancers because their movement was so free and unhindered. In white western Christianity we see a lot of stiffness and there is no movement to worship outside a slight sway or clap. When I was younger my church was very mixed and we and included a lot of Congolese immigrants, and they influenced the worship of our church in a wonderful way. Our church incorporated dance into the worship, and it was a really wonderful way to relax and connect with God. This native American dance group reminded me of that, and made me want to delve back into that style of worship.</p><p></p><p>Additionally, I think that this dance group proved how it is ideal when sharing your religion, and trying to spread Christianity, that you don't try and force your culture on the people you are sharing it with. I think that as a western culture we have taken influence from other cultures and made it our own, and that is the same here. As people become part of religions, or hold on to influence from other cultures they grow with it and adapt it to their culture. Christianity should just be about sharing God, but sometimes it has also become a form of forcing other cultures to assimilate.</p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767747522578.mp4">Video 2</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767747522578.mp4">Video 2</a></p>	GojpOownVGSB6GAphPiQJZEJXuL2	\N	\N	\N	f	f	f	[]	2026-01-07 00:50:16.463	2026-01-07 00:57:25.139	2026-01-08 17:29:18.069	\N
65bbe844f53d3372e084591f	Lafayette Park	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706812850292.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkEntrance_Riley_001.HEIC.JPG" width="483" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706812919560.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkFence_Riley.HEIC.jpg" width="481" style="aspect-ratio: 1.4370956146657081"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706812934552.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkEntrance_Riley_002.HEIC.JPG" width="481" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706812968778.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_LafayetteParkLake_Riley.JPG" width="479" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813000660.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_Riley_001.HEIC.JPG" width="481" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813044284.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_Riley_002.HEIC.JPG" width="482" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813132998.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_ThomasHartBentonStatue_Riley_004.JPG" width="479" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813089473.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_ThomasHartBentonStatue_Riley_001.JPG" width="484" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813160367.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_ThomasHartBentonStatue_Riley_002.JPG" width="479" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813222198.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_ThomasHartBentonStatue_Riley_003.JPG" width="486" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813258935.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_ThomasHartBentonStatue_Riley_006.JPG" width="482" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813302627.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_ThomasHartBentonStatue_Riley_005.JPG" width="483" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813337552.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkGrounds_ThomasHartBentonStatue_Riley_006.JPG" width="481" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813399382.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_ParkSign_Riley_001.HEIC.JPG" width="479" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1706813423101.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190624_LafayettePark_RowHouses_Riley.HEIC.jpg" width="478" style="aspect-ratio: 0.7149390243902439"><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 11pt">When walking through the wrought-iron gates that surround the 30-acre park in the middle of Lafayette Square, it’s like transporting into another area of the world. Surrounding the park is classic St. Louis three-story homes and bustling traffic, but inside the park is a green oasis complete with a lake, statues, and a lot of history. The history of the park is exemplified in the statues of historical figures that regally stand on pedestals and in the aged structures scattered all around. The main building in the park is an old-looking brick building that used to be a police station for Lafayette </span><span style="font-size: 14.6667px">Square but</span><span style="font-size: 11pt"> is used as a visitor’s center today. Next to the building is the Lafayette Park Lake, where even on a rainy day remains a hub for people and birds alike. Sitting on a mossy bench in front of the lake, I can hear the sounds of the fountain spewing straight into the air and splashing down into the water, the hum of the generator at the visitor center, and the sound of children laughing at the play area. All over the park, people walk their dogs on the concrete paths, a man plays catch with his two young sons, and a woman begins to feed the birds around the lake, seemingly a daily tradition for her. No matter the weather or the day, Lafayette Park attracts many different communities for its calming environment in the middle of the bustling city.</span></p><p><br><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Are the park’s visitors mostly attracted to it because of the location or the environment of the park?</span><br></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">How many more visitors are there on a sunny weekend day?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Are there any events in the park that cause people to attend regularly, keeping up with traditions?</span></p><p></p><p><span style="font-size: 11pt">Is the lake the part of the </span><span style="font-size: 14.6667px">park</span><span style="font-size: 11pt"> that is most attractive to visitors? Or is it a different portion of the park?</span></p><p></p><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The sounds of the fountain spewing straight into the air and splashing into the lake below it are the loudest noises within hearing distance</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Next is the hum of a generator on the building next to the lake</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Every few moments, the geese in the grass surrounding the lake start to squawk</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The rain has just lightened up, but it is still drizzling, so the rain creates tiny ripples all over the pond</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The geese, ducks, and swans don’t seem to mind the rain -- all of them are out in the open, not hidden under trees</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">They seem to have taken over the pond area -- there are no humans near the pond, only the birds</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The pond is in the middle of the park and surrounding it is the Park House, sculptures, and a play park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Surrounding those is many different areas of green space -- some fields, some secluded</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">According to their website, </span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://lafayettesquare.org/visit/lafayette-park/"><u><span style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">https://lafayettesquare.org/visit/lafayette-park/</span></u></a><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">, the park was dedicated in 1851 as the first public park in the city of St. Louis</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It is considered to be the oldest public park west of the Mississippi&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Park House used to be used as a police station for Lafayette Square, but is now used as the Parks and Recreation office</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The website also has a self-guided walking tour to print out and follow so that people can understand the history of each monument in the park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Surrounding the entire park is tall, three-story, classic St. Louis homes. They are all so colorful and exemplify the vivaciousness of the city that lies just outside the greenery of the Park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">I’m sitting on a bench just beyond the sidewalk of the pond -- on the rainy day, the wooden bench is soggy and the green moss spreading all over it seems to be really bright against the gloomy colors of the day</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The bench has a dedication on it with a metal plaque below it</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Even with the rainy weather, the park still seems to be busy -- there is a father playing catch with his two young songs in a field nearby, people walk their dogs on the cement sidewalks, and people still sit on benches by the pond</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">This place seems to be a hub for people around it, even when the weather is bad</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Immediately upon walking in the wrought-iron gates of the park, it felt like entering a calming natural environment in the middle of a bustling city</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The trees covering the sky above the park and the sounds of birds and water rushing make it feel like an enclosed nature park, when it’s actually some greenery in the middle of St. Louis city</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">There are two statues within my view</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">One is a statue of George Washington erected in 1869</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The other statue, which was the first one I saw upon walking into the park is of Thomas Hart Benton. His statue has around 10 concrete steps leading up to it. The base of the statue is surrounded by a concrete walkway and appears to stand on a pedestal to the rest of the Park</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The statue was dedicated in 1868 by the first professional female sculptor, Harriet Goodhue Hosmer, according to </span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://lafayettesquare.org/visit/lafayette-park/"><u><span style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">https://www.stlouis-mo.gov/parks/parks/browse-parks/view-park.cfm?parkID=52&amp;parkName=Lafayette%20Park&amp;amenitySubTypeID=27</span></u></a><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">A woman walked down the cement path by the Park House and walked right up to the birds on the grassy area around the pond</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It’s like they recognize her, they all start crowding near the fence before she even makes it to them</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When she reaches the fence, she starts to greet them with a baby voice like they’re her pets</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">She begins to pull out a bag of food from her purse and throw some all around the group of birds, making sure each gets some</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Seeing that woman, obviously very happy to see her ducks and geese, along with the father playing catch with his sons in the field behind her just shows how Lafayette Park is a place for all different communities to gather, no matter the weather</span></p></li></ul>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.61552919798304	-90.21526336669922	2023 Lafayette Ave, St. Louis, MO 63104, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "public", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "park", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "outdoors", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "monuments", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "architecture", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "plants", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "landscape", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "French", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "history", "origin": "user"}]	2019-06-24 17:00:17.822	2024-02-01 18:51:48.679	2025-01-28 18:22:09.676	\N
65c52a485d508d3d84952104	Laughing Bear Bakery	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419660020.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Buddah_Riley_001.JPG" width="631" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419673256.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_001.jpg.JPG" width="632" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419688451.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_002.jpg.JPG" width="630" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419706704.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_003.jpg.JPG" width="632" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419719763.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_004.jpg.JPG" width="629" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419732722.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Kitchen_Riley_006.jpg.JPG" width="642" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419747389.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_001.jpg.JPG" width="638" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419767674.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_002.jpg.JPG" width="636" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419783442.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_004.jpg.JPG" width="397" style="aspect-ratio: 0.6666666666666666"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419797170.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_005.jpg.JPG" width="397" style="aspect-ratio: 0.6666666666666666"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419815757.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_006.jpg.JPG" width="561" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419831859.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_007.jpg.JPG" width="486" style="aspect-ratio: 0.6666666666666666"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419855895.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_008.jpg.JPG" width="551" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419895852.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Sign_Riley_001.JPG" width="668" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419913281.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Riley_008.jpg.JPG" width="668" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419932429.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_Sign_Riley_001.JPG" width="669" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419977518.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_StainedGlass_Riley_001.JPG" width="695" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707419993248.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_StainedGlass_Riley_003.JPG" width="690" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707420013294.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_StainedGlass_Riley_005.JPG" width="688" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707420028251.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190718_LaughingBearBakery_Interior_StainedGlass_Riley_006.JPG" width="703" style="aspect-ratio: 1.5"><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Upon walking inside, the air smelled like desserts being baked and a humid mildew smell like from an old building</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Walking past the kitchen, the workers wearing white aprons seem to be bustling around</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Kalen McAlister greets us and welcomes us to sit down, she seems to be happy to have visitors at the bakery</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While guiding us around the Centenary Methodist Church, she seems to be proud of the location and the people she’s surrounded with. It soon becomes very obvious why, as one woman who works for the church talks about her dog treat company that hires homeless people</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While guiding us through meditation, her voice seemed to become much calmer and softer, showing a different side to us than the very friendly and bubbly Kalen she was outside the room</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">She seems to have a very strict belief system that I admire, including the ideas that: meditation is incredibly beneficial to life, the system is not kind to prisoners, and people deserve second chances</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">When discussing the reason for spending so much of her time helping prisoners have a second chance at life, she said that she’s seen the ways in which the prison system has failed&nbsp;</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It’s interesting that many religious organizations have funded the Bakery, as well. Organizations like Sisters of St. Joseph have honored Kalen and given her funds that were desperately needed to keep the Bakery running</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">It seems like her mission ties into religious messages about Jesus granting second chances and forgiving his followers, like from the bible, there is first John 1:9 “If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.”</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While there is a religious undertone to the mission of the Laughing Bear Bakery, it doesn’t seem like that is Kalen’s main reason for continuing her work. She has seen many people come out of prison and then immediately return back because they didn’t have the funds or resources to maintain a life outside again</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">She stated that in her nearly 30 employees that she’s hired since the bakery opened, only 2 have gone back to prison</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">While Kalen asked that we do not question the employees about their past crimes, she didn’t seem to question this either. She believes it doesn’t matter what the crimes were, her only judgement of the employees begins the moment she meets them, their history before that is unimportant to her</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">The Laughing Bear Bakery seems to be the definition of an organization that works for the community and cares for the wellbeing of an underserved group</span></p></li></ul>	m5zMmy4slFNMTuX7a4kn6hIFumX2	38.59839651142084	-90.25335609912872	4001 Utah St, St. Louis, MO 63116, USA	f	f	f	[]	2019-07-18 17:00:28.424	2024-02-08 19:23:52.452	2024-06-24 21:31:58.275	\N
