6641cf0cd0cdb28c4794b664	Temple No 1- Bando Station	<p>Bando Station is the closest local train station to the designated Temple 1 of the Shikoku pilgrimage, Ryozen-ji. Today the train is busy with students, locals, and other henro and visitors heading to the temple, judging by the white vests and jackets. A group of tourists from Italy is on the train as part of a guided tour group and looks to number about 20 people. There are dressed in comfortable outdoor clothing, so likely just visiting the first one or temples before heading to another destination.</p>	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	34.1553931	134.4931765	Michinokita-58-3 Ōasachōhinoki, Naruto, Tokushima 779-0225, Japan	f	f	f	[]	2024-05-13 05:49:46.913	2024-05-13 08:27:56.839	2024-11-22 17:39:13.784	\N
6643a86d5eba0abd4716ab20	Bumper Sticker	<div>lately, I've been seeing this design of Christ as bumper stickers.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1715710030777.jpg" style="\n      max-width: 50%;\n      height: auto; /* Maintain aspect ratio */\n      /* Additional CSS properties for sizing */\n    ">&nbsp;<br><br></div>	d2v7JCxLlFT9jkQlvayOD4Z3HAL2	40.69116176575486	-73.90385365120498	270 Cooper St, Brooklyn, NY 11237, USA	f	f	f	[]	2024-05-14 18:05:21.698	2024-05-14 18:07:41.386	2024-06-21 20:55:45.104	\N
6645832d5eba0abd4716ab2a	Temple 2 - Hondo	<p>The Hondo at Temple 2 is located adjacent to the Daishido, or Kobo Daishi hall on a narrow terrace above the rest of the temple complex. The Hondo can be reached either via a set of steep stairs, made of stone and concrete, or a concrete ramp that begins along the edge of the temple space at the back of the lower area and curves more gently around up to the back of the Daishido.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1733106085781.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="697" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p>The Hondo is directly at the top of the steps, with only a narrow flat space in front of the building. Directly behind the Hondo is another short set of steep steps leading to a small building that is not open to the public.</p><p></p><p></p>	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	34.1557449	134.4904774	Dannoue-12 Ōasachōhinoki, Naruto, Tokushima 779-0225, Japan	f	f	f	[{"label": "#ShikokuTemple2", "origin": "user"}]	2024-05-16 03:53:06.84	2024-05-16 03:53:17.763	2024-12-02 02:48:16.758	\N
6646fbc15eba0abd4716ab2c	Waymarker - Journey to Temple 4	<p>One small, but important detail about the journey between temples for the walking henro are the way markers that are placed along the routes most commonly used. These markers come in a variety of forms, with older wooden and stone markers mixed with laminated paper sheets and even painted markers on the ground or a nearby object (such as a guardrail).</p><p></p><p>With modern access to smartphone navigation, we observed many henro using their phones, but as our team traced the route between Temple 3 and Temple 4 on foot ourselves we found we often needed the way markers we encountered to verify we were on the path to the next temple. There are often multiple types of markers found along the route, as can be seen here with the carved sign with finger pointing the way and the laminated print sign on the guardrail.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1715927852005.jpg" alt="20240514_105713.jpg" loading="lazy" width="572" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1">	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	34.0765913	134.5590113	Japan, 〒770-0815 Tokushima, Suketōbashi, 1-chōme−２２ 鈴木商店渭水ビル	t	f	f	[{"label": "#ShikokuHenroJourney", "origin": "user"}]	2024-05-17 06:36:12.433	2024-05-17 06:40:01.257	2024-11-27 19:02:04.491	\N
664ad1160aafd2348a4e231a	Temple 16 - gate	<p>Temple 16 (Kanonji) is situated in a dense extra urban area, and walking henro coming from Temple 15 (Awa Kokubunji) must navigate along highways and then narrow streets to get to the temple gate.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1733107501738.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="625" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1733107553299.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="623" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p><br></p>	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	34.0683769	134.4744195	Kanonji-36-1 Kokufuchō, Tokushima, 779-3123, Japan	f	f	f	[]	2024-05-20 04:26:03.894	2024-05-20 04:27:02.151	2024-12-02 02:47:10.542	\N
664d43983a48ef5557d2b724	Bizan Mountain park and shrine	<p>Bizan mountain, in the heart of Tokushima, I'd accessible via ropeway and trails. Five Shinto shrines are located within the park at the summit. The main henro path passes along the base of Bizan mountain and the park offers a panoramic view of the valley. The city of Tokushima is both a starting point for henro and also a finishing point, as many who complete the pilgrimage return to Temple 1, so it is common to see pilgrims around parts of the city, especially near the train station/bus terminal.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1732734274665.jpg" alt="20240522_094318.jpg" loading="lazy" width="752" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333">	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	34.0671932	134.5377656	Mosukegahara-1-1 Bizanchō, Tokushima, 770-0908, Japan	f	f	f	[]	2024-05-22 00:56:37.548	2024-05-22 01:00:08.643	2024-12-02 01:58:55.953	\N
66512a428aad2c5255e53207	Saigyō Zakura - Kōyasan	<p>Saigyō's Cherry Tree - Famous monk and poet Saigyō planted this cherry tree in commemoration of moving the Sanmaidō Hall to its current location during the 12th century. Saigyō was a Shingon esoteric priest and some of his poems reference Shingon's Moon Disk Meditation. Saigyō's Buddhist name means West-Going, a reference to the Buddhist Pure Land and Amida.</p>	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	34.2131968	135.5809184	154 Kōyasan, Koya, Ito District, Wakayama 648-0211, Japan	t	f	f	[]	2024-05-24 23:58:39.402	2024-05-25 00:01:06.836	2024-11-26 19:53:43.211	\N
