66abc95570ac713135a7614f	Mother Jones Monument	<p>I found my way to the Mother Jones monument by the beckoning of a life-size image of Mary Harris Jones (1837-1930), located inside the Coalfield Rest Area on Interstate 55, just 25 miles south of Springfield, Illinois. Dressed in a sharp purple overcoat and positioned in front an image of a diverse group of coal miners, the stern stare of “Mother Jones” urged me to take a short detour on my drive home from Milwaukee to St. Louis to learn more about this intriguing woman and “her boys.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533320283.jpg" alt="IMG_1976.JPG" loading="lazy" width="295" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533365480.jpg" alt="IMG_1974.JPG" loading="lazy" width="413" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The Mother Jones Monument is located 20 miles south of the Coalfield Rest Area in Mt. Olive, Illinois, just a few turns off Old Route 66, behind a small neighborhood speckled with political yard signs suggestive of a small town with a conservative majority. The monument is situated within the Union Miners Cemetery, which is claimed to be “the only labor union-established and union-owned cemetery in the United States.” The cemetery was purchased in an effort to secure a burial site for miners who perished in the “Battle of Virden” (a deadly struggle that took place between miners and forces of the Chicago-Virden Coal Company in 1898), and all miners thereafter.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533424736.jpg" alt="IMG_1984.JPG" loading="lazy" width="511" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533446701.jpg" alt="IMG_1986.JPG" loading="lazy" width="517" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533461090.jpg" alt="IMG_1982.JPG" loading="lazy" width="519" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>The monument itself is located at the far edge of the cemetery, and it marks the burial place of Mother Jones, who dedicated her life to fighting for the rights of coal miners. At the same time, the monument also stands as a memorial to those miners whose lives were lost in the Battle of Virden and similar deadly conflicts over union miners’ rights.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>While traditional religious language is often expected at cemeteries, it is prominent at this site in unexpected ways. For example, those who “have given their lives to the cause of clean unionism in America” are labeled as “martyrs” on a monument placard; Mother Jones is given the title, “The Miner’s Angel” on an engraved stone; and a Mother Jones quotation at the bottom of a list of donors borrows language of Christian atonement theology, stating, “I learned in the early part of my career that labor must bear the cross for others’ sins, must be the vicarious sufferer for the wrongs that others do.”</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533517431.jpg" alt="IMG_1996.JPG" loading="lazy" width="610" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533560088.jpg" alt="IMG_2013.JPG" loading="lazy" width="613" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533580684.jpg" alt="IMG_1998.JPG" loading="lazy" width="629" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533615677.jpg" alt="IMG_2031.JPG" loading="lazy" width="625" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>While Mother Jones was raised in the Roman Catholic tradition, she is said to have later rejected organized religion which she believed caused workers to become complacent in this world because of its focus on the afterlife. Still, Mother Jones believed Jesus to be a revolutionary and a model for workers in their fight against wealthy “parasites,” and she befriended several clergymen with similar activist values (Rosemary Feurer, “Mother Jones: A Global History of Struggle and Remembrance, from Cork, Ireland to Illinois,” <em>Illinois Heritage,</em> May 2013, 30).</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The Mother Jones Monument stands as a witness to the life of a woman who was committed to the struggle for labor rights and class justice, while at the same time revealing a background tension between an opioid religion and an activist religion, made apparent throughout the site in characteristic quotations from Mother Jones such as, “Pray for the dead and fight like hell for the living!”</p><p>&nbsp;</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533655531.jpg" alt="IMG_2016.JPG" loading="lazy" width="665" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533690344.jpg" alt="IMG_2017.JPG" loading="lazy" width="661" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1722533709059.jpg" alt="IMG_2019.JPG" loading="lazy" width="661" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p>Sources:</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Rosemary Feurer, “Mother Jones: A Global History of Struggle and Remembrance, from Cork, Ireland to Illinois,” <em>Illinois Heritage,</em> May 2013, 28-33.&nbsp;</p><p>“Who Was Mother Jones?” Mother Jones Museum, accessed July 29, 2024, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="file:///C:/Users/kelly/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_filelist.xml">https://www.motherjonesmuseum.org/who-was-mother-jones</a>.</p>	F619Zx0aQZQ0b6OoHm9ORy7Hmlh1	39.08101	-89.7319255	5585 Mt Olive Rd, Mt Olive, IL 62069, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "Monument", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Cemetery", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Activism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "LaborUnions", "origin": "user"}]	2022-07-10 22:30:59.119	2024-08-01 17:43:49.255	2024-11-22 20:04:02.811	\N
