67461c1142f3d7d7c447136d	Tatsue Station - Walk to Temple 19	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1732646871484.jpg" alt="20240515_100053.jpg" loading="lazy" width="906" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p> Tatsue Station, on the JR Mugi Line, is the closest train station to Temples No. 18 (Onzan-ji) and No. 19 (Tatsue-ji). Temple 19 is quite close to the station and a short, flat walk toward the Tatsue River.The station is typical of more rural areas, as it is unmanned. </p>	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	33.9700056	134.6086971	Tatsue Station, Kabuki Tatsuechō, Komatsushima, Tokushima 773-0017, Japan	f	f	f	[]	2024-11-26 18:42:41.288	2024-11-26 19:05:53.958	2024-11-26 19:47:49.271	\N
6746286f2a34da641188e3bf	Sikhs of St. Louis w/ Starbucks Foodshare 	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1732651093475.jpg"><p></p><p>This sticker on a Starbucks pastry display case signals Sikh participation in the charitable economy in the St. Louis metro region. Sikhi developed in the Punjab region of the Indian subcontinent in the 16th century and is now a major religion throughout the world, largely through Indian diaspora communities. <em>Seva</em>, often translated as service to others without regard for personal benefit, is central to Sikh devotional practice. This photograph was taken during Starbucks’ holiday marketing season, which aligns with the western Christian religious calendar, prompting reflection on the subtle forms of religious pluralism in modern life. </p><p>For an introduction on Sikhi beliefs and practices through the experiences of an American-born Sikh, see Simran Jeet Singh, The Light We Give (2022).<br></p>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	38.62469668745299	-90.34763414782559	1909 S Brentwood Blvd, Brentwood, MO 63144, USA	t	f	f	[]	2024-11-26 19:58:39.206	2024-11-26 19:58:39.547	2024-12-07 20:48:40.632	\N
