6942e972ba8e50e22cd4ffcc	Dog Park Rosary	Plastic bag with note posted by patron or staff at dog park containing a rosary found in the grass.Dog parks can be spaces where people of different religions, political affiliations, races, and other cultural identities gather. Many faith traditions also have special blessings for pets as they increasingly become part of social lives of families and communities.Was this rosary brought to the park for a specific ritual? Was it merely on a person while they were there? While it may not be possible to know all the circumstances of this religious object, it does prompt questions about how we can better understand religion in unexpected public spaces as insights to religion in modern life.<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1765992747166.jpg"><br>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	38.61068571204096	-90.48814729469441	JG66+7P Town and Country, MO, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-12-17 17:33:37.721	2025-12-17 17:33:38.62	2026-04-15 13:48:42.055	\N
695f439ae17a265e32abba54	SSM Health Saint Louis University Hospital Chapel	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767940653985.jpg" alt="Image description" width="100" style="aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;"><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif;">Descriptive:</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif;">The Chapel at SSM Saint Louis University Hospital has a white interior with colorful stained-glass windows depicting a cross, Jesus, a city skyline, and plants. The Chapel has a white alter with a large brown wooden cross with Jesus hanging on the wall above it. There are dark wooden pews as well a dark wooden chair near the alter for the Priest.</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif;">Reflective:</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif;">It offers a place to express your faith and seek solace when facing rough situations in the hospital which I’m sure can be very helpful as lots of families and friends go through difficult times in hospitals and can feel supported through religion. There is a Sunday Mass that people can attend but the chapel itself is open 24/7 and available to patients, visitors, and staff alike, which is thoughtful because times when you need faith can happen at any time. It’s worth noting that this Chapel is specifically Catholic which can feel limiting as not everyone who may desire having a moment with their faith in a hospital may be Catholic. A large amount of American society is Christian, and this is a SLU affiliated hospital and SLU has a religious affiliation so it’s not necessarily a surprise but it’s still worth acknowledging this distinction regarding faith services at this hospital. I still think having a Chapel available can be beneficial, and perhaps they could add small rooms near the chapel that are dedicated to praying or even just moments alone when facing tough situations. Like the rooms that are designated for when doctors give bad news.</span></p>	TAYiMNo2Hbd7TVuKdbsNm44Ja8B3	38.6243966	-90.2384797	1201 S Grand Blvd, St. Louis, MO 63104, USA	f	f	f	[]	2026-01-08 05:41:33.769	2026-01-08 05:41:46.89	2026-01-09 06:52:06.108	\N
69613f0fe17a265e32abba56	Catedral de La Almudena	<p>This cathedral is located in Madrid where I’m currently studying abroad. It is directly across from where the Royal Palace is which in a way puts religion in the middle of the political life. The outside of the building is made from brick and stone and looks very historical but when I entered the inside, the design was much more modern than I had expected. There were many bright colors everywhere and beautiful stained glass. I stayed here for a long while just observing everything in the massive cathedral. There is a very clear separation between an area for prayers and an area made specifically for tourists. There were many tourists but there was also many people engaging in prayer or just sitting down silently. To me this space almost felt more like a museum than a church which in a way puts religion second to sightseeing. This had me questioning how the sacredness of something can change when religion is tied to a national monument. This church showed the way religion is a symbol of a nations identity. Catholicism here represents tradition along with history and it works even for people who don’t actively practice it.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767980724143.jpg" width="1200" style="aspect-ratio: 0.669643 / 1;"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767980739905.jpg" width="1168" style="aspect-ratio: 0.749679 / 1;"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767980756888.jpg" width="1200" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767980800583.jpg" width="1200" style="aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;"><p><br></p>	9hRMb71q0udjGm64G10Fpktd2xz2	40.425449232076744	-3.7065606706856684	Cl. de las Pozas, 16, Centro, 28004 Madrid, Spain	t	f	f	[]	2026-01-09 17:46:54.704	2026-01-09 17:46:55.691	2026-01-09 18:03:35.371	\N
69617004e501b2dec4d9df5c	Svetitskhoveli Cathedral	<p>Svetitskhoveli Cathedral feels fundamentally different from Sameba despite serving a similar religious function. Unlike the imposing scale of Holy Trinity, Svetitskhoveli carries a sense of ancientness. The worn out stone walls and give you the impression of a place that has been shaped slowly, over the centuries.</p><p></p><p>As you walk through the grounds, the atmosphere feels quieter and more reflective. People move with noticeable restraint and speak softly. The space seems less performative and more inward-focused. Lit candles and icons fill your vision.</p><p></p><p>What stood out to me was how history itself feels religious here. Svetitskhoveli does not simply house religious practice, it embodies it. The cathedral made me understand just how deep rooted Orthodox Christianity is within Georgia. It tells a story of numerous generations that have walked the same steps as you have while you are at the Cathedral.</p>	MnFDBFJqBrbcnAHKMcrBSGGu6HS2	41.842271197018306	44.72104758024216	RPRC+V9C, Narekvavi-Mtskheta-Railway Station, Mtskheta, Georgia	t	f	f	[]	2026-01-04 21:15:42.073	2026-01-09 21:15:48.633	2026-01-13 23:48:31.27	\N
6962c076e5cae7d05d1f4f6c	Midway Airport	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768078692234.jpg">The Midway International Airport in Chicago is the scrappy sibling of O’Hare and the city’s major hub for Southwest Airlines. When I landed at the airport ahead of the 2026 American Historical Association meeting, I noticed signage for both a Chapel and a Yoga Room. I made a mental note to scope out these sacred spaces before my return flight. These photos were taken on a Saturday afternoon in early January. The Bears will face the Packers a few hours later in an NFL playoff game a few miles east of the airport at Soldier Field. Walking through the concourse, lots of people wear Bears clothing, suggesting another unexpected sacred space.<br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079158253.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079170586.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079181167.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079192577.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079198867.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079206702.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079215023.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079219650.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079224308.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079229979.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079235660.jpg"><br><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768079239893.jpg"><br>“Chapel” is an “interdenominational” sacred space (unpack this!) that is most visibly Protestant, catholic, and Muslim. No obvious markers of Judaism, Buddhism, Hinduism, Orthodox Christianity, indigenous, or other religious traditions. I sat in the room for about 20 minutes before my flight and counted 3 other people who used the space, two for afternoon prayers (Muslim) and another for unspecified prayer or reflection. 	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	41.78821065052975	-87.74382626196	Concourse B, Chicago O'Hare International Airport, Gate B7, Chicago, IL 60638, USA	f	f	f	[]	2026-01-10 21:11:17.351	2026-01-10 21:11:18.177	2026-04-15 13:48:42.209	\N
