65aad7444d42fa67c3018c7d	Wat Pa Ha	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1705695775369.jpg" alt="Wat Pa Ha Nang Lae 2.JPG" width="1307" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>Principle Image constructed by Sala Khaek</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1705694908406.jpg" alt="Wat Pa HA Nang Lae 6.JPG" width="1211" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>Sala Khaek himself standing before smaller image flanking principle image (seen at the right) during the renovation of the temple's main prayer hall. As is typical the building was taken down leaving the Buddha images in tact, and then a new building constructed around them.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1713466105686.jpg" alt="DSC07519.JPG" width="4896" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p>The two smaller images that once flanked the principle image. Both built by Sala Khaek. They have been relocated to the office of the temple's Abbot. </p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1713466403321.jpg" alt="DSC07509.JPG" width="3672" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p>Smaller image (left) detail.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1713466436194.jpg" alt="DSC07511.JPG" width="3672" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p>Smaller image (right) detail.</p>	mvYjmNueigWqx1hs4tP3RvX7IUG2	19.9861115	99.86803139999999	XVP8+9RH ถนนบ้านป่าห้า Tambon Nang Lae, Amphoe Mueang Chiang Rai, Chang Wat Chiang Rai 57100, Thailand	t	f	f	[{"label": "SalaKhaek", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Buddhism", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "ChiangRai", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "BuildingBuddhism", "origin": "user"}]	2024-01-19 20:06:35.631	2024-01-19 20:10:44.137	2025-01-28 18:22:02.543	\N
65c7f0cfce3cc6a72dfc3625	All Star Tattoo 	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707600061511.jpg" loading="lazy" width="745" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p></p><p>Tattoos are deeply personal, deeply meaningful rituals. Or not. They can also be vanity impulses that we regret as soon as the sun rises. Walking into the All Star Tattoo shop in Maplewood, Mo., on a gray Saturday afternoon in February 2024, I would probably split the difference in my own decision-making process. This is not my first tattoo. In fact, it is one I've been saving to mark a milestone in my life. But I wasn't expecting to be here today. Sometimes you can see milestones from, well, miles away. Other times they mark their own time.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Alyssa Maldonado-Estrada writes about tattooing among Catholic men in the Bronx as a kind of pageantry of masculinity. The sample artworks covering nearly every inch of wall space when you walk into All Star, like many commercial tattoo parlors in the US, display predominately female forms among the figurative designs. Bare breasts and flappers in the American traditional style. Kimono clad "Japanese" and indigenous American women in painted faces look out as you wait for your artist from the bar stools and leather couch arranged inside the garage door that would be open to the streetscape on a warmer day. But of the dozen or so people waiting for their turn to take a chair, only 3 seem to present as male. Women's bodies are on display both as artworks and as canvases.&nbsp;<br></p><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707673025445.jpg" loading="lazy" width="677" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p>&nbsp;/<br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707673045071.jpg" loading="lazy" width="680" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707673096648.jpg" loading="lazy" width="678" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br><br></p><img height="auto" src="blob:null/5cbe0327-1ef3-4626-a4f8-f7e84d86202c" loading="lazy"><p><br></p><p>Looking over the souls waiting for their turn with the needle and ink is the Infant Jesus of Prague, holding the world in one hand and blessing humanity with the other.&nbsp;</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707600075437.jpg" loading="lazy" width="680" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707602030347.jpg" loading="lazy" width="681" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br><br></p><p></p><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707601705008.jpg" loading="lazy" width="676" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707601736983.jpg" loading="lazy" width="681" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707601758976.jpg" loading="lazy" width="681" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br><br></p><p><br></p>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	32.70324195262341	-117.15875101320763	1 Park Blvd, San Diego, CA 92101, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "Tattoo", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Catholic", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Ritual object", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Gender", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Jesús", "origin": "user"}]	2024-02-10 21:20:24.121	2024-02-10 21:55:27.469	2024-11-26 20:09:52.926	\N
65c90a025d508d3d84952109	My notes	<div><b>And I can modify text, too.</b></div><div><br></div><blockquote style="margin: 0 0 0 40px; border: none; padding: 0px;"><div>I can type notes directly into this field.&nbsp;</div></blockquote><div><br></div><blockquote style="margin: 0 0 0 40px; border: none; padding: 0px;"><div>I can <u>underline</u>&nbsp;for emphasis by toggling the button above or by using keyboard commands. Same with <i>italics</i>.</div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>When I want to quote a source, I can emphasize it by using the quote button above:</div><div><br></div><blockquote>Here is my quote!</blockquote><div>I can use the tab function on my keyboard or I can use the functions above to change the paragraph positions.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707673656719.jpg" style="\n      max-width: 50%;\n      height: auto; /* Maintain aspect ratio */\n      /* Additional CSS properties for sizing */\n    ">&nbsp;<br><br><div>I can add a camera shot directly into the text flow (note that the picture will load above my current text position.)</div><div><br></div><div>Or I can add photos from my device library:</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1707673728939.jpg" style="\n      max-width: 50%;\n      height: auto; /* Maintain aspect ratio */\n      /* Additional CSS properties for sizing */\n    ">&nbsp;<br><br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for looking at my camera roll, lol.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>I can add video:</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>But it looks like right now the video isn't loading into the note flow! We'll change that ASAP.</div><div><br></div><div><span style="font-size: 1em;">And if I want to list items or make notes for myself, I can use the bullet point function.</span><br></div><div><ul><li>think about time and place</li><li>think about what I am feeling at this site</li><li>consider the five senses, not just my own</li><li>xyzzy</li></ul><div>And then I can go back and edit here or on the desktop app.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>If l have a stylus and want to handwrite notes, I can do that, too.&nbsp;</div></div><div><br></div><div>And when I want to add multimedia, I can do that in different ways.</div><div><br></div><div><b>Time and Place</b></div><div><b><br></b></div><div>I add time and place using the functions on the main tool bar at the top of the screen. I can also adjust time and place if I'm updating notes later from a different location.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div><b>Tags</b></div><div><b><br></b></div><div>Right now, tags are entered at the full discretion of the researcher/user. This is to allow the broadest latitutde to researchers to classify and position their research. But we encourage users to use a combination of specific and general tags so your entries can connect with others that are published to the global map.</div><div><br></div><div><b>Publishing</b></div><div><b><br></b></div><div>When I'm ready to publish my note, I just click the arrow at the top of the screen. Before I do that, though, I'm going to check my own notes and posts.</div><div><b><br></b></div>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	38.610732010182666	-90.32013239501852	2812 Sutton Blvd, Maplewood, MO 63143, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "Coffee shop", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "App Demo", "origin": "user"}]	2024-02-11 17:41:26.884	2024-02-11 17:55:14.413	2024-06-21 20:55:10.406	\N
65ea1ab35d508d3d849521d3	Wat Cham Bon	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1713465811662.jpg" alt="DSC03335.JPG" width="4896" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p>Principle Buddha IMage made by Sala Khaek</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1713465682052.jpg" alt="DSC03337.JPG" width="3672" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p>Principle image detail</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1713465765528.jpg" alt="DSC03342.JPG" width="3672" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p>Head of old image, most likely one of the two smaller images that flanked the principle image made by Sala Khaek</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1713465916182.jpg" alt="DSC03347.JPG" width="3672" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p>Second head.</p>	mvYjmNueigWqx1hs4tP3RvX7IUG2	19.6933932747011	99.91870840890503	MWV9+9F4, Doi Lan, Mueang Chiang Rai District, Chiang Rai 57000, Thailand	t	f	f	[{"label": "SalaKhaek", "origin": "user"}]	2024-03-07 19:48:43.892	2024-03-07 19:51:15.343	2024-06-21 20:54:26.067	\N
65eb69e81d442f7c715c9d7a	Hindu Temple - Saint Louis	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709927078099.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_HinduTempleStLouis_Interior_Safa_002.JPG" width="787" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709927092026.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_HinduTempleStLouis_Interior_Safa_001.JPG" width="792" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1709927109704.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190712_HinduTempleStLouis_Interior_Safa_003.JPG" width="618" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Intricate floor markings line every step from the grey rubber stairs at the entrance to the shiny black marble ones leading up to the glamorous deities at their pedestals. The air smelled crisp and clean owing to the wide-open build of the space. Sure, the space was all indoors, but the plethora of windows and the continuous off-white carpet gave it an air of vastness. “No cameras, videos, or phones” read a sign that led up to this main prayer hall. It was apparent why- halfway up the steps I heard continuous and rhythmic chanting that got louder as I made my way up. Men, women and little children all sang the same hymn in a South Indian Language. The arrhythmic clinging of bells here and there was the only thing that broke the consistency in the atmosphere. The feeling of the soft carpet under my bare feet reminded me of what a prayer space would feel like at home, and perhaps this is what the temple’s intent was.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><ul><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Does the presence of a largely South Indian crowd make the space and practice more different than that of the average temple? Would you extend this effect to the identities of your religious priests and staff as well?</span></p></li><li><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Does the restriction on phone usage actually force people to concentrate on prayer or have you noticed that they seem to practice just fine without it?</span></p></li></ul><p><br></p>	h5IM6ZhPGxVEAdSpwywH4C1OZLB2	38.614825023321444	-90.49910545349121	723 Weidman Rd, Ballwin, MO 63011, USA	f	f	f	[]	2019-07-12 17:00:48.323	2024-03-08 19:41:28.377	2024-06-21 21:25:18.654	\N
