67ec3d9dec47227b05fb5bd0	Centennial Establishment of the Society of Saint Vincent de Paul 	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1747373266671.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="330" style="aspect-ratio: 0.7770618556701031"><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif">The Society of Saint Vincent de Paul is a Catholic laypersons’ organization which promotes and facilitates volunteer-led efforts to address material conditions of the poor and needy. The first conference of this organization in the US met at the Cathedral of Saint Louis, King of France in 1845. Saint Vicnent de Paul is considered a Patron Saint of the City of St. Louis.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Title: </strong>Plaque Commemorating the Society of St. Vincent dePaul</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Publisher</strong>: Self-created photograph</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Contributor</strong>: Kathleen Jochman</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Date: </strong>1845, 1945</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Format: </strong>JPG, Bronze Plaque</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif; font-size: 12pt"><strong>Source:</strong> </span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://ssvpusa.org/about-us/history/"><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif; font-size: 12pt">https://ssvpusa.org/about-us/history/</span></a></p>	TrWZ9PxTspOnzWwVD6UeQTxC5s12	38.711496860509214	-90.31085287717478	25 Lucas Rd, St. Louis, MO 63121, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-04-01 19:25:16.525	2025-04-01 19:25:17.398	2025-05-16 05:30:13.814	\N
6781623e9ab7f3dd8796687e	Religion Yard Decor	<p>It is uncommon for individuals to showcase their religious beliefs through yard decor; however, this specific individual does this by propping up a sign that has a cross and says Jesus in all capitals. This signifies the individual(s) living in this home are most likely Christian since this religion believes in Jesus as the son of God and knows Jesus died on the cross. I noticed how the sign looked a little dirty, meaning it has been there for a decent amount of time. The sign is propped up against an outhouse, which is an old-fashioned bathroom. This property is located in Wildwood, MO, which is a predominately white area. Wildwood is a suburb of Saint Louis that is west of downtown. Also, there is a large population of Christians within this area because of the copious amounts of Christian churches nearby. The yard also had loads of Christmas decor up, showing the individual(s) celebrate Christmas as well. I felt happy to see this sign because I am a Christian myself, and seeing others in my community believing in the same thing as me makes me happy.</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736532380206.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="1410" style="aspect-ratio: 0.461992 / 1">	eNlFMkamG3dhAy0n4WcBAcZECOm2	38.61123989209351	-90.71272941466064	J76P+FW Wildwood, MO, USA	f	f	f	[{"label": "christian", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "Jesus", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "God", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "cross", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "sign", "origin": "user"}]	2025-01-09 17:14:56.849	2025-01-10 18:09:02.266	2025-01-11 03:08:47.395	\N
677e0bab9ab7f3dd87966878	Willmore Park - St. Louis	<p>This park is closer to an urban area, surrounded by large fields of grass. The park itself has a large playground, with many swing sets. It’s a popular park so there are always many people around.There’s also a small hockey area, where many people come to play hockey, soccer, or even use their skateboards. There’s a dog park as well as a pond where many people fish or sit around. The people who come here are usually children or teenagers. Of course, there’s also a lot of parents and families who come here, and I have especially seen many parties (such as birthday or graduation parties) come and be hosted. People who come here seem to stay for an hour or two, and parks like this are usually the most popular mid-afternoon. Evidence of human interaction would be the run-down equipment, clearly a playground and pavement that have been used for years. There’s always people around somewhere, and all the benches are run-down as well. The grass is patchy, especially around areas where there are the frisbee nets and benches to sit on. There’s constant chatter and you can always see a grill with used coal underneath. There's a lot of human evidence at a place like this, since it’s such a popular and large area. Since it is closer to an Urban area, there are a diverse group of races and ethnicities who come to this park. There doesn’t seem to be a dominant religion/ethnicity/race, it seems very diverse with all different cultures coming to this place. I’ve seen many Bosnian and Hispanic families here—but still, it’s very diverse. It’s not an incredibly rich area, so I would say the visitors are anywhere from low to middle class. It gives a very “cozy” and welcoming vibe to the park. My own feelings and thoughts that have to do with this park is, that it holds a lot of memories from my childhood up to my teenage years. There have been many upgrades to this park, so it doesn’t look as old as it actually is. There are many places within it, such as the hockey and soccer fields, tennis, and a dog park area. There’s a pond and this has always been my favorite park to walk my dogs at. I’ve come here not only to play as a toddler, but to take homecoming and dance pictures in my teens. So, there’s so many memories that make this place special and notable to me. I’ve made new friends here, and hung out with old friends as well. There’s so much to do so I believe it to be an overall positive, notable place in my own life. I believe when it comes to this place, it’s so diverse and many families of various religions come to visit. Because of this, it is very welcoming and an overall positive vibe, with no religion feeling “dominant” over another. This makes it especially welcoming to minority religions. I remember many Islamic Bosnians coming to this park, and there are still many Bosnians who come and visit. Because of this, you will see many families with hijabs or families that speak different languages.</p>	gb9lsunG6oXMjsxmLnXgzmYcXG82	38.63375136862252	-90.2331131190918	Marchetti Towers West, 3530 Laclede Ave, St. Louis, MO 63103, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-01-08 05:22:51.294	2025-01-08 05:22:51.939	2025-01-09 20:17:33.227	\N
