69613c49e501b2dec4d9df57	Iglesias de San Jeronimo el Real	<p>This is a church that is in Madrid and is huge and sit a bit higher than the regular street level which in a way separates it from everything around it. It’s beautiful and made from stone with interesting brick architecture all alone the outside walls. It looks very old and also historic, and I noticed it has a very symmetrical design. Inside the building it was very silent and also a bit dark but has very high ceilings along with arches all along the inside. The lighting was low and most of the light was natural coming from the windows. The space felt very serious on the inside. There were signs to be quiet and respectful which is why I felt the serious vibe. The first thing that caught my attention was the alter all the way in the front. There is a large painting and statues on the walls next to it. It is the center of attention at the church. There are also many crosses, candles, statues all around and very clearly a catholic kind of space. To me the space felt very calm but also was a bit intimidating due to the silence and the massive size. I felt as though I had to be very careful of the way I acted. As someone who isn’t religious I felt a bit unsure if I was okay to just look around or if I should participate. I saw myself as a visitor I and not a participant because while I was walking around, observing, and taking pictures there was people next to me engaging in prayer.There were many tourists like me in alongside people who were worshipping which was a bit intimidating. This church is both a site to practice religion on and also a historical monument. In Spain, Catholicism isn’t just a belief system but also just a cultural presence that is connected to all of Spanish history and also power. By the mix of people praying and tourists in a way shows that religion can still be influential even when not everyone there is religious. This shows the way religion continues to shape the public life through tradition, space, and in a way authority.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767980059226.jpg" width="1200" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767980063316.jpg" width="1199" style="aspect-ratio: 1.330744 / 1;"><p><br></p>	9hRMb71q0udjGm64G10Fpktd2xz2	40.425449244976285	-3.706560674873789	Cl. de las Pozas, 16, Centro, 28004 Madrid, Spain	t	f	f	[]	2026-01-09 17:35:04.285	2026-01-09 17:35:05.163	2026-01-09 18:03:06.915	\N
64af43d139ed08b8df098941	Saint Louis CITY Soccer Club	<p>STL CITY SC matches are opportunities to reflect on the similarities of civic and cultural practices to those commonly classified as “religion”. At this May 20 match against "derby" rival Sporting Kansas City, the club and fans marked sacred space through performances such as a parade and chanting and through physical markers such as the installation of Damon Davis’s “Pillars of the Valley,” which memorializes and makes visible the erasure of a thriving black community to make way for “urban improvement” on the site the stadium now stands. Clothing, devotion-based subcultures, and fan-created iconographies of players are other ways that CITY SC can challenge and illuminate the ways we think about and understand religion.</p>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	38.63292245432704	-90.23935912228679	3730 Foundry Way Suite 140, St. Louis, MO 63110, USA	t	f	f	[]	2023-07-13 00:22:41.565	2023-07-13 00:22:41.565	2025-01-28 18:22:13.712	\N
64fa57f13e3748bad735fd92	Busch Stadium	<p>Busch Stadium in St. Louis, Missouri, is the home of Major League Baseball's St. Louis Cardinals. The team dates to the late 19th century and has a robust, dedicated fan base in St. Louis and beyond. A statue of Stan Musial greets visitors and passersby in front of the main gate where<span style="font-size: 1em">stadium tour ticket holders gather. On the day I visited with my graduate seminar, people from all over the country--and fans of other MLB teams--gathered to tour this cathedral of baseball.&nbsp;</span></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1698360858281.jpg" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br></p><p>In addition to informing tourists of the team's history, the tour guide recited the names of legendary players (including Musial) and permitted tourists' entrance into areas of the stadium otherwise restricted to ticket holders, including owners boxes, media rooms, and trophy rooms. Throughout, the tour created a sense of sacred space that was embodied in tour goers' reverence for images and objects on display.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1698361658206.jpg" width="4032" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1698361686262.jpg" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br></p><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1698361750027.jpg" width="4032" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1698361806343.jpg" width="4032" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p><br></p><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1698361844628.jpg" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br></p><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1698361883907.jpg" width="4032" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p><br></p><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1698362053107.jpg" width="4032" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	38.623508052963864	-90.1936712146292	8th St. at, Clark Ave, St. Louis, MO 63102, USA	t	f	f	[]	2022-09-19 23:12:00	2023-09-07 23:08:33.496	2025-01-28 18:22:18.408	\N
