a_005.JPG" width="757" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711649604910.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190724_ContemporaryArtMuseumStLouis_Interior_Gallery_Upstairs_Safa_001.JPG" width="742" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711649637913.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190724_ContemporaryArtMuseumStLouis_ShirinNeshatFacingHistory_Safa.JPG.JPG" width="792" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711649620890.jpg" alt="LRDA_Location_20190724_ContemporaryArtMuseumStLouis_Interior_Gallery_Upstairs_Safa_002.JPG" width="778" style="aspect-ratio: 1.33333 / 1"><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Two large glass doors served as the entrance to this vast, wide, and gray space. The architecture inside was nothing more than a monotonous arrangement of contemporary art frames against gray and white walls. Everything was so basic yet inviting in its simplicity and fine detail. Though not empty, the museum was full of echoes of even the smallest sounds including the faint buzzing and whirring of it’s café’s only coffee maker. Only three to four exhibitions were on display, each of them in the same grey and white space with a sole painted wall text offering context to otherwise banal objects and arrangements. This was a contemporary art museum, so nothing less of an empty, geometric and minimalistic space was expected. Nonetheless, it managed to tell powerful stories thorough the very little that it used to portray them. The most intriguing story was that told by Lawrence Abu Hamdan’s exhibit in what was called the “Earwitness Theatre”. Part of the exhibit was a pitch-black room was the source of the continuous echo in the museum. Abu Hamdan spoke about the Syrian refugee story and used vivid screams and cries to portray the fear and angst amongst these communities.&nbsp;</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt">Even if this space is not explicitly religious, do religious people find themselves drawn to certain works of art/exhibits?</span></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711649742225.mp4">Video 1</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711649784072.mp4">Video 2</a></p><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1711649826902.mp4">Video 3</a></p>	h5IM6ZhPGxVEAdSpwywH4C1OZLB2	38.640623480498924	-90.23483276367188	3750 Washington Ave, St. Louis, MO 63108, USA	f	f	f	[]	2019-07-24 17:00:28.505	2024-03-28 18:14:27.666	2024-06-21 21:25:22.079	\N
6639126ed0cdb28c4794b5ea	Wat Saeng Phrathat	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1715016044099.jpg" alt="DSC04336.JPG" loading="lazy" width="411" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p>Buddha image by Sala Khaek. This at one point was the principle image of the temple, but has since been replaced with a newer image. Now this image is in an auxiliary building, to the left of another, newer Buddha image. Notice the inset mirror on the altar.  to the left of the principle image, which is a newer image that has replaced the one made by Sala Khaek. </p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1715016276715.jpg" alt="DSC04340.JPG" loading="lazy" width="515" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1715016249828.jpg" alt="DSC04345.JPG" loading="lazy" width="517" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p>The image in context.</p><p></p>	mvYjmNueigWqx1hs4tP3RvX7IUG2	20.0006086	99.8960401	89 หมู่ 16 บ้านป่าซางหัวฝาย ต.แม่ข้าวต้มหลวง อ.เมือง Tambon Mae Kao Tom, Amphoe Mueang Chiang Rai, Chang Wat Chiang Rai 57100, Thailand	f	f	f	[{"label": "SalaKhaek", "origin": "user"}]	2024-05-06 17:14:59.197	2024-05-06 17:25:02.503	2024-06-21 20:54:27.871	\N
66317a28c280f76c161d1f7b	Agia Triada Monastery	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1714518507482.jpg" loading="lazy" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>&nbsp;</p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1714518536788.jpg" loading="lazy" width="2048" style="aspect-ratio: 1.333333 / 1"><p>The Holy Trinity monastery is an active Greek Orthodox monastery and is a museum for a multitude of religious objects. The courtyard is filled with plotted plants and trees surrounding benches. There are also cats that roam the monastery. The architecture has Venetian and Byzantine influence, and the monastery was built in the 17<sup>th</sup> century. (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.rental-center-crete.com/blog/agia-triada-tzagaroli/">https://www.rental-center-crete.com/blog/agia-triada-tzagaroli/</a>) The architecture includes multiple archways, but they are more rounded than the pointed archways seen at the Melrose Abbey. The monastery also has a lot of domes on the roofs, with small crosses extending through the top. This dome style is similar to that of Islamic mosques, which showcases the influence of Arabic culture in Greece. Inside the center building with the tallest dome, there is a mural of Jesus with a halo formed around His head. The room is adorned with various gold artifacts and crosses, further decorated with more images of religious Christian figures. There is a dress code, and it is to cover the shoulders and areas above the knees for both men and women. The people are mostly quiet, talking amongst the others in their group. Many visitors have their phones out and are photographing the main room. The museum contains objects ranging from important outfits and fittings to small coins and buttons. There is also a gift shop, which sells food, artifact copies, lotions, and oils among other things. The monastery is also a winery, which does wine tastings and tours.</p><p><br><br></p>	F9PYL1B1L8VwDllARwcSTpBDCkY2	35.5606673	24.1352285	H46P+73, Moni Agias Triados ton Tzagkarolon 731 00, Greece	f	f	f	[]	2023-07-24 15:50:14.793	2024-04-30 23:09:28.81	2024-06-21 20:55:12.75	\N
664ff5a55eba0abd4716ab43	Okunuin Cemetery - Corporate monuments 	<p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1732072729677.jpg" alt="20240524_094757.jpg" loading="lazy" width="438" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1"><p>The newer section of Okunoin includes many corporate monuments, and among the most recognizable is the rocket shaped monument of aerospace company Shin Meiwa Kogyo. Panasonic, Nissan, Honda, and Toyota are among the many other corporations represented. For centuries, Okunoin has been one of the most sacred and desired burial places in Japan, with emperors, samurai, monks, politicians and other prominent Japanese people vying for the chance to be interred here. The corporate graves are an opportunity for employees of the company and their families (who purchase plots within the larger grave) to be memorializing in this sacred space. The streaming crowds of visitors on most days walk past these corporate graves on their way to the inner sanctum of the cemetery and Kukai's mausoleum.</p><p></p><p>As they pause at the monuments lining the pathway, visitors take a few moments to take photos or read inscriptions, but rarely move into the rows behind the main path. There are fewer trees in this portion of Okunoin, giving it a brighter, more open feel than the shaded walkways and graves in the older sections of the cemetery.</p><p><br></p>	KHAHfSWMJ6SshJHmk81TXGOPUzI3	34.2212927	135.6062548	6JC4+GG Koya, Wakayama, Japan	t	f	f	[]	2024-05-24 00:44:21.539	2024-05-24 02:04:21.377	2024-12-02 01:46:13.994	\N
67223de10079e794e7c0a0ea	Disney Animal Kingdom "Asian Religion"	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1730295951130.jpg" alt="IMG_7687.jpeg"><p>"Asia" exhibits at Walt Disney World's Animal Kingdom theme park outside of Orlando, Florida.</p><p></p><p>There is a long and fraught history of representing religion in American exhibitions and theme parks. Late-nineteenth and early-twentieth-century worlds fairs, for instance, were hotbeds of colonialist representations of Asian and indigenous religions, often casting them as "primitive" or otherwise as inferior to "enlightened" Protestantism. Disney World has long participated in representations of religion that are built on appropriated imagery and racialized tropes. The representations of Tibetan and Nepalese religion at Disney's Animal Kingdom theme park are presented as authentic replicas of religious objects. This performative authenticity of Buddhas, stupas, and puja shrines are nevertheless situated in an environment that is created for consumerist entertainment--it is a theme park, after all--not Buddhist contemplation or religious practice. Why was it important to park designers--"imagineers"--to include religious iconography in the queues for roller coasters and water rides? What work are these objects doing to and upon patrons and employees?</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1730296915255.jpg" alt="IMG_7661.jpeg"><p></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1730296986493.jpg" alt="IMG_7665.jpeg"><p></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1730295951131.jpg" alt="IMG_7669.jpeg"><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1730295951132.jpg" alt="IMG_7668.jpeg"><p></p><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1730297131534.mp4">Video 1</a></p>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	28.3574423	-81.5905788	1213 Bear Is Rd, Orlando, FL 32830, USA	t	f	f	[]	2024-10-09 13:44:40.427	2024-10-30 14:08:33.487	2025-07-07 15:15:49.379	\N
67801a509dcf3e02b0f6d800	Blessing for the Home on the Feast of Epiphany	<p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736448553108.jpg" loading="lazy" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736448562603.jpg" loading="lazy" width="5712" style="aspect-ratio: 1.3333333333333333"><p><br></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736448574104.jpg" loading="lazy" width="4284" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br>On Sunday, at the King of France church in St Louis, MO, after church had ended, the priest handed out these little cards with a piece of chalk. The directions are to all gather at the doorway of your home, write 20 C M B 25 to represent the current year as well as the initials of Casper, Melchior, and Balthasaar, and to say the following prayer, "May all who come to our home this year, rejoice to find Christ living among us and may we seek and see, in everyone we meet, Jesus, who is Lord for ever and ever. Amen." The marking of the doorway is a ritual used to bless the home and is linked to Epiphany, a Christian celebration. Epiphany marks the the visit of the three traditional Magi (Casper, Melchior, and Balthasaar) to baby Jesus. For the new year, it is meant to bring security and blessings into the home for all who enter.</p>	rdhZC6H9O1bzb92yPQMyCM8WbPO2	38.66205925614059	-90.29577606558367	6124 Horton Pl, St. Louis, MO 63133, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-01-09 18:49:52.103	2025-01-09 18:49:52.618	2025-01-10 19:00:11.219	\N
674a1d446c31e61d6ff373a2	Church mouse traffic box?	<p>On a busy intersection in Columbia, Missouri, an ordinary traffic box has become an installation of public art incorporating medieval architectural motifs consistent with those found throughout western Christianity. The cathedral “windows” open up to an unpeopled streetscape that includes at least one steeple. Each window has an occupant: a mouse looks “outward” from its perch while, in another window, a lounging cat faces “inward” to catch the gaze of passersby. This street corner is adjacent to the campuses of the University of Missouri and Stephens College. Aromas from nearby restaurants representing cuisines from around the world and the bustle of foot traffic along this college town street are poignant juxtapositions to the traffic box’s imagined landscape.</p><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1732910382002.jpg" loading="lazy" width="3024" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75"><p><br></p>	8H86gZYSFIRHGy8q39OgBAfQFeC2	38.624696231060916	-90.34763440124124	1909 S Brentwood Blvd, Brentwood, MO 63144, USA	t	f	f	[{"label": "public art", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "architecture", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "street religion", "origin": "user"}, {"label": "street art", "origin": "user"}]	2024-11-29 20:00:03.199	2024-11-29 20:00:04.162	2025-04-10 17:12:15.162	\N
678157889ab7f3dd8796687c	home religion	<blockquote><p></p><img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1736529852449.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="1079" style="aspect-ratio: 0.827454 / 1"><p>This picture showcases my family's monthly ritual of worshiping the gods. It is an important family tradition in which all of us participate, expressing our respect and gratitude to our ancestors and deities. The ceremony is usually held at home, where we set up a table with abundant offerings, including fruits, snacks, homemade dishes, and wine. These offerings symbolize our sincerity and reverence as we pray for the blessings of our ancestors and deities to protect our family's health, happiness, and success.</p><p>At the end of the ceremony, we burn joss paper, typically in the container shown on the left side of the picture. Joss paper is a symbolic currency representing our care for our ancestors’ lives in another world. By doing so, we hope that our ancestors will have sufficient wealth in heaven. It is a way of expressing our gratitude to them.</p><p>This custom is part of the unique cultural traditions of southern coastal regions in China, deeply rooted in family values and local beliefs. We hold this ritual every month, not only to seek blessings from the deities and ancestors but also as an opportunity for family reunion.</p><p>location: shangtou city Guangdong China</p></blockquote>	nE1zFFEYadXIDMeGfvyiZLoVJTs1	23.4296253	116.7609334	CQH6+V93 Shantou Waisha Airport (SWA), Jin Sha Road, 龙湖区汕头市 China, 515041	f	f	f	[]	2025-01-10 17:01:56.766	2025-01-10 17:23:20.862	2025-01-11 19:45:00.388	\N
677ec1e99dcf3e02b0f6d7ff	Stupsko Brdo - Bosnia	<p>The roads are kind of run-down, and there are many houses lined up one after another. There’s also clothing lines hung up behind each house, indicating families and life at each one. Each house is two houses divided in one building, so there’s always chatter going on. There are bikes along most of the front doors. The people are all Bosnians, many are related or have known each other's families for decades. It is a very small neighborhood, so everybody knows and interacts with each other. The evidence of human interaction would be the garbage cans at every house, the bikes being gone then reappearing, and the constant chatter you hear. Front doors are always open, indicating that anyone is welcome to come and visit. Everyones’ race in this neighborhood is white, since most of Bosnia are white people and not many foreigners choose to live there. I have many memories of this neighborhood as well, since it holds the house we stay at whenever we visit. Majority of the neighborhood are Muslims, which is obvious when Eid or Ramadan is happening, and there’s celebration all along the street and many religious individuals go to visit the Mosque every Sunday.</p><p><br></p>	gb9lsunG6oXMjsxmLnXgzmYcXG82	38.63375136862252	-90.2331131190918	Marchetti Towers West, 3530 Laclede Ave, St. Louis, MO 63103, USA	t	f	f	[]	2025-01-08 18:20:25.333	2025-01-08 18:20:25.496	2025-01-09 20:43:55.657	\N
6825970c56ac5d90d588ccda	Bishop Joseph Rosati 	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1747327182793.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="266" style="aspect-ratio: 0.8270091457482001"><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif">The above oil painting was completed in 1887 to memorialize the first bishop of Saint Louis Diocese. Bishop Joseph Rosati was responsible for commissioning a suitable structure and landmark for the growing diocese, and in 1831 laid the cornerstone for the current building. Within 3 years, the structure would be commemorated as a cathedral, the very first west of the Mississippi.</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Title: </strong><em>Oil Portrait of the Right Reverend Joseph Rosati, First Bishop of St. Louis</em></span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Author: </strong>Schultze, Louis 1820-1901</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Publisher:</strong> Missouri Historical Society</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Contributor: </strong>Kathleen Jochman</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Format: </strong>JPG image 238 x 288</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Rights:</strong> Copyrights Undetermined</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif; font-size: 12pt"><strong>Source: </strong></span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="http://collections.mohistory.org/resource/374049">http://collections.mohistory.org/resource/374049</a></p>	TrWZ9PxTspOnzWwVD6UeQTxC5s12	38.624233967454416	-90.18724721742703	209 Walnut St, St. Louis, MO 63102, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-05-15 07:26:04.526	2025-05-15 07:26:04.663	2025-05-16 01:53:04.056	\N
6826d1efcfd83da30865d4e3	Umbraculum	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1747374742138.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="210" style="aspect-ratio: 0.4371596145789694"><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif">The <em>umbraculum</em>, or “big umbrella”, located on the right side of the photo, is one of two ritualistic papal objects which denote the historic significance and honor given to the Cathedral to designate it a Holy Basilica. The Basilica honor was sanctified by Pope John XXIII in 1961.</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Title: </strong><em>Umbraculum</em></span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Author:</strong> Self-created photograph</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Contributor: </strong>Kathleen Jochman</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Date: </strong>1961</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Coverage:</strong> East Enclave of the Basilica of Saint Louis, King of France</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Type</strong>: Image of Artifact</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Format: </strong>JPG,<strong> </strong>Silk, metal – 40” diameter x 36” height</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Source: </strong></span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://gcatholic.org/churches/data/basUS.htm"><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif">https://gcatholic.org/churches/data/basUS.htm</span></a></p>	TrWZ9PxTspOnzWwVD6UeQTxC5s12	38.58607579200123	-90.26748758637333	4348 Ellenwood Ave, St. Louis, MO 63116, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-05-16 05:49:34.883	2025-05-16 05:49:35.467	2025-05-16 05:53:36.661	\N
6826d22563707c94a6aad4ae	Coronation of Saint Louis IX, King of France	<img height="auto" src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1747374667882.jpg" alt="Image description" loading="lazy" width="273" style="aspect-ratio: 0.8564073226544623"><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif">The above painting resides in the East Side Chapel of the Basilica, an enclave to the right of the main altar. The painting was originally gifted to Bishop DuBourg by King Louis XVII of France in 1818, along with several other oil paintings.</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Title: </strong><em>St. Louis IX, King of France </em>1751</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Creator: </strong>Coypel, Charles-Antoine</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Contributor: </strong>Kathleen Jochman</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Date: </strong>1751</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Format: </strong>Photo of Oil Painting</span></p><p><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif"><strong>Source:</strong> </span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="https://www.metmuseum.org/met-publications/france-in-the-golden-age-seventeenth-century-french-paintings-in-american-collections"><span style="font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;, serif">https://www.metmuseum.org/met-publications/france-in-the-golden-age-seventeenth-century-french-paintings-in-american-collections</span></a></p>	TrWZ9PxTspOnzWwVD6UeQTxC5s12	38.586075790042464	-90.267487590299	4348 Ellenwood Ave, St. Louis, MO 63116, USA	f	f	f	[]	2025-05-16 05:50:28.95	2025-05-16 05:50:29.057	2025-05-16 05:52:09.536	\N
696141b2e501b2dec4d9df58	Mercado de San Miguel	<p>This market is located in Madrid and is very busy and loud. There are many people who are eating, drinking, and just moving from one stall to the next. A lot of the stands had different kinds of prom products such as jamon, chorizo, and different kinds of pork sandwiches and at the same time wine and beer was served everywhere. The menus are mostly written so they assume most people are comfortable with eating pork and drinking, which for me was tough as from my religion we don’t eat any kind of pork. The space feels very social and also like there is a celebration going on and shows the way food is a very important part of Spains culture and tradition. While walking thru the market it made me very aware of how food is a reflection of religious and also cultural norms. It definetly was very exciting and lovely but I felt a bit out of place due to my religious food restrictions. Nobody is being directly excluded but there is an assumption that’s built into the food options. This market reflects the Spanish Christian culture history through its food traditions. It shows the way religion can shape everyday life but in an indirect way which influences what can be considered normal without openly labeling it as religious.</p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767981445257.jpg" width="1080" style="aspect-ratio: 0.8 / 1;"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767981451623.jpg" width="1200" style="aspect-ratio: 0.562588 / 1;"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767981459595.jpg" width="1200" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><p><br></p><img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1767981469212.jpg" width="1200" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><p><br></p>	9hRMb71q0udjGm64G10Fpktd2xz2	40.42544941990123	-3.706560627679916	Cl. de las Pozas, 16, Centro, 28004 Madrid, Spain	t	f	f	[]	2026-01-09 17:58:09.215	2026-01-09 17:58:10.079	2026-01-09 18:03:50.756	\N
69619760e5cae7d05d1f4f6a	Mall of Emirates - Dubai	<img src="https://livedreligion.s3.amazonaws.com/media-1768003932345.jpg" alt="Image description" width="100" style="aspect-ratio: 0.75 / 1;"><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt;">When I visited the Mall of the Emirates in Dubai, I was struck by how intentionally designed the interior space felt. Walking through the mall, the architecture uses modern luxury (reflecting its construction in 2005) with motifs drawn from regional culture, particularly elements of Islamic geometric design that appear throughout the rest of the country. Arched walkways, patterned tiles, and decorative details repeat across the space, creating visual continuity. One detail that stood out to me was the use of soft, warm yellow lighting rather than harsh white lights often seen here in the U.S. These design choices shape how visitors experience the space, subtly grounding the mall in a cultural and religious context.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt;">Although the mall functions primarily as a commercial space, religion is clearly integrated into everyday life. The mall contains multiple large, clearly marked prayer rooms that are easily accessible. Although I never entered them, they were very large and scattered all throughout the mall, which suggests that prayer is expected and accommodated alongside daily activities. This observation highlighted how religion in Dubai is embedded in public architecture, allowing sacred and secular life to coexist seamlessly.</span></p><p></p><p><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt;">The mall is famously known for its attractions, such as an indoor ski resort, a large floor to ceiling aquarium, and indoor waterfalls which reinforce its identity as a leisure destination. Despite this, the atmosphere remains surprisingly calm. High ceilings, open corridors, and controlled lighting create a sense of order, even during busy hours, which reminded me of how sacred spaces are often designed to guide movement and attention.</span></p><p></p><p></p>	eLWLpX673KMwT150ppTaUYqyKMg1	25.118107	55.200608	Mall of The Emirates - Sheikh Zayed Rd - Al Barsha First - Al Barsha - Dubai - United Arab Emirates	f	t	f	[]	2026-01-09 20:19:20.436	2026-01-10 00:03:44.997	2026-01-10 00:19:21.716	\N
